      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-05_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>CRICKET, BOWLING, AND</head>
        <p>CRICKET, BOWLING, AND</p>
        <p>ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>Cricket Notes. TO-DAY'S MATCHES. Warrington w. Wigan (Peet, We Benefit) : B. Bilcock, TV Richardwo, Pro ; J Callon, J Bainbridge, 8 Baxter, G Woodcock, H Speakman, T Dixon, W Bennett, A Duckworth, Peatell. le second team match.</p>
        <p>AV ARRINIII,N AND DISTRICT LE Vat. finlike!, v. Viderspool Brewery Whiteeroas Recreation v. Walker'. Brewery Orford v. Sankey Wire Mills. Warrington Conservative. v. Ryland. Bros.</p>
        <p>-- There was some keen and interesting cricket at Arpley on Saturday, and after an exciting finish the honours rested with Blackley 17 a single run with three wickets in hand. The Warrrngtonians had first turn at the wicket and the great feature of their innings of 178 was a grand display by the young Botelerian, G. Woodcock, who lab. fair to make a name for Memel( lathe world of cricket. He hit out merrily and for a long time defied the bowling of Taylor, one of the best professionals in the Mancheste district Habit three o's sod four i's and altogothsf messed 60—the top score on the Warrington side. Peatell, the home profession-lb batted in masterly style and his 11 was certainly his Faint batting per- formance this season.</p>
        <p>Blackley owed their vietnry chiefly to Oui magnificent work of Taylor who, in addition to (ming most of the bowling, made the handsome aeore of U. Ile batted in really skilful style, scoring well all round the wicket,and had the satisfaction of making the winning hit three minutes from time.</p>
        <p>Woodcock also gave a taste of his abilities in the bowling department, capturing three wickets Lr 26 runs.</p>
        <p>To-day Pretell tokens "rt 11-earned benefit, and it is hoped there will be a large attendance at the match with %Vigao. The professional has put in some Nary useful work for the club and, altogether apart from the personal element, the gtme should prove an attlartive fixture.</p>
        <p>Warrington 2n4 med—e—a good show et Blackley bet failed to registers victory. The draw, however, was sot at all to their discredit, Blackley having been dis- mireed for 186 while the Warrington score reached 148 for six wicket.. 'l' Dixon wits in tip.tep form for the Warringtoniana and carried out his bat with II to his credit.</p>
        <p>Warrington Conservatives maintain their prestige in the Warrington and District League, Wilderspool Brewery being accounted for on Saturday by a majority of 33. The most ammessful batsman for the visitors Iran Scott (3.1) and Carter contributed n useli.l 14 for Wilderspool.</p>
        <p>Sankey Wire Mill.achived a good win at the expense of Nylon&amp; Bros. W Hunter (221 and J Lomas (17) shared the batting honours for the Wire Mills, and J Jolley (14) was the higheet scorer too Rylands. Lomax bowled very effectively.</p>
        <p>The following is the League Table np-to.date z— I Phi. Won Lost Din. Pt. W'g'en Conner ll . 10 1 0 ... 20 Sankey l2 8 a 1 ... 17 Whiteeroas Roo Walkers Brewery Sankey Wire 51111 l3 ... 5 ... 6 ... 2 ...12 Latchford Wes. Rylands Bros Orford Wilderepl Brewery It ... 2 ... 9 ... 0 ... 4</p>
        <p>A well•oontested friendly match between Warrington St. Mary's end Whitecross ended in a triumph for the Saints by two runs. McKay knocked up 29 in splendid style, and also took seven wickets for 22 rues.</p>
        <p>Latchford Baptists met disaster at Moore, the bowling of Woods and Antrobus being • sore trial to them. II afaybary (19 not out) was the only Latchford tlx.ritiobngtitnel finely for kl,iiiklinforsttr4l:ln,alctorlrnisplay. H</p>
        <p>Greppenhall inflicted a monad drubbing on Runcorn. The most notable batting displays were thrift of R Gifford (29), J Burrows W Watson (23), and E Banks (21), for the winners and W U Draper (20) for Runcorn.</p>
        <p>The Grappeohall 2nd string fared badly at the hand, of Arley also immured a majority of 70. J Lsos beaded the Arley scores with 29, but only one Grappenball baumso, T Woodward (12) reached double figures.</p>
        <p>Oughtrington Park put up a very poor show agsiost Manchester Y.M.C.a. whose mainstay in the battiny department wee J. 11. Elliaon. with the magnificent swore of 1;6. The Park eleven could make little head- salty against the dellverim of Tallantere and only N. Wood (25) reached double numhers.</p>
        <p>Wilderrpool were victorious over Leigh Vine.. W. Stringer taking five wickets for 11 sod Boyd five for 8. It Shekesbaft betted well for 12.</p>
        <p>Winwick Asylum experienced a heavy defeat on their own ground, Warrington St. Panic winning by 53 rune The chief scorer were J Farmer (Winwiok) 21. T Seed (St. Paul's) If, and W. Lawless (St. Paul's) 16. The howling of the visitors eras deadly, and altogether too good for the home hemmer. Li Mann cent down too ovors and one hall, taking three wicket. for only One run. P llnrnr (St. Paul's) took Ilve yrictets for twenty.flvo runs, and 0 Floyd (Winwick) took 7 for 36,</p>
        <p>Warrington Police hive been busy this week. Oa Tuesday they met Walker's end cipital game resulted ins draw. The Police opened tbs betting and for. time it looked an if an easy victory was for their opponents with 5 wickets down for 22 runs, but Newer joined Hayes et the wicket Rod shifted the scare to 66 before Nosey Ives caught in a vain endeavour to knock another boundary. Hayes wee in good form,but scored slowly the wicket being rather treacherous. Armstrong also batted well making one of the fine efforts he does on occasion when his side in In a tight corner. For the Walker's team, Griffin made the fluent show, knocking up 22 and was caught the last wioket down, this being the only chance he had given the whole innings. In the last over of the game Wells had a near squeak of being caught behind the vvicket, ia fact the batsman stye he seas out, hut the umpire decided otherwise, and time being up it was declared a drawn game.</p>
        <p>Os Thtirsdsy the Liverpool Police were entertained and victory rested with the home team. The visitors hatted that Jewkes and Ashcroft Veing responsible for 45 runs out of the total of 71. The Wsrrington team -..ned at the score, sad the first s‘s first few minutes for 2 for the Liverpudlians, t Is won, and Summers it end to make thing. • rithont losing any more I, when they retired.</p>
