      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-05_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</head>
        <p>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>It is a bold assertion in these days of competition. to style oneself "The eccenti ic comedian of the day," but in the cepa of Chris. Richards, Nebo again appears before a Wart ington audience this week, the claim is no idle boast. He is a miithmaker with a distinct style of his own, and hearty laughter greets his humorous antics, while clever hat manipulaiions add further n welty to the show. Another good comedian is found in the person of Syd Walker, who presents an amusing furniture removing scene. Described as " the man on the time," Canard is seen in an original "turn" and is one of the best cootorti nuts who have appeared at this place for some time. With his !nipple body he is able to perform some really remarkahle feats. A go-id Meech entitled "The ideal woman 'b given be the Violet Shelley Trio, while the "Martha Ann and Sammy" act by Will Lindsay and Victoria Cross is screamingly funny. Daisy and Dot make themselves warm favouritea with toeir duets and ilancing and Nisbet Moss gives eridence of con- siderable weal ability in her rendering of chorus songs. A kcal artiste deputises for D. uglas K. Lorne and with the assistance of his dog presents a credit- able item. The intelligence of the dog is wonderful : and it roust hare been highly trained. The pictures still maintain their high standard, while the orchestra under the direction of Mr. E. A. Wild, p, execute souse charming music. The work of renovation wife\ has been in progresa during the last three Week!, is now completed and adds in great measure to ths comfort and thin fine sppearancs of the house. 'rho colour scheme has been retried out in soft tones of cream, geld and Wedgwoed blue. That of the circle and pit is in a rather more bold and free style of Venetian red. A little hist ire attaches to the ceiling panels, representing " Spring, Summer, Autumn and Winter." During one of his tripe abroad Mr. Macnaghten, whilst staying in Milan, Italy. became much interested in the work of a rising , artist, Signor Rafael", and commissioned him to paint , these panela. Al this time of the year a number of Mr. Macnagliten's theatres are being decorated anew, and there has been much competition for the panels. But Mr. Ttirpin prevailed upon Mr. Mscoagliten to allot them to Warr inoton. They are well w..rthy of inspection. The whole of the work has been carried out under the personal supervision of Mr. Frank Macnaghten.</p>
        <p>A SELF-SUPPORTING EMPIRE IN WHEAT. In t ' log the world's wheat crop for the current year, the Economist drool attention to the remarkable expansion of wheat growing thst has taken place within the British Empire, and points out that if the Canadian and Australian crop. yield., they promise the British Empire wotild this meson he rell-supportiog. India could spore an eight to ten million quarters; Canada ten to 12 millions (some matieticiaos any 15 milliorra, while A usual is may give eight to nine millioos, 0r.., 29 million quarters in all. Stith a good home crop the United Kingdom needs 281 million quarters of imports; South Africa and Newfoundland about two millions And all this without any preference to pr.:leper.* colonists or other &amp;cal manipulations at the expense of our working ohms,</p>
        <p>is</p>
        <p>RAILWAY DIVIDENDS. The London and North-Western dl•idend is declared at the rate of 6 per cenr. per annum, as;th £141,000 carried foreword and £I00,0;0 o the general reserve. The Great Western Railway di•idend is sanounced at the rile of V. per cent. per annum for the balf-yeer, with £120,400 carried f .rwar I, against 4 per cent, and furnard last 3esr.</p>
