        <p>THE CON MAST RE CIVEEN LTBEE.kI. AND</p>
        <p>been asleep niece the last election cede. are oertaioly going to he very ;e-awake doling the autumn campaign •ed if the whole Tory party in Warrington is so infatu- ated as to think that Lenc.hire of all counties in Eogland is ever g dm; to be p •rsuaded to go back to • the rotten system of Protection--; cries of Never so fir as I am concerned I give them choice of their ground, and if they want t • fight 03 they groan l we will meet thorn then. " (Cheers., The Tariff Reformer., 111,. Crueltid weut on, wen: fill of dodges —in fact the movement was</p>
        <p>C000.5..1. DOD4II from beginning • end. altar, bear) Their moda oporandi WM to heat aVention on one segment of the circle, with the el jent of preventing the people from having. well-bslaucied and accurate bird's eye view of Ike whole sul,jrct. The Liherale, however, did not intend to allow them to take a little bit here and there. If they wanted to tight on that question they could choose theirgrounl, hut the Liberals would take core that once more, during the comng autumn, all the harmful fallacies of Protection were exposed end they would not be ictjafted tottl Warrington took it. share —cheer.)—in the work that was being done hy the great indent rid constituencies of Britain and the pernicioue figure of Protection was trampled in the dust. (Cheers.) The position reminded bin of the story of ante. who did not quite hit it off with his rnotherin-law, io fact they got on very badly. (Laughter.) At last the or lady died and the, telegraphed to him to convey the Bad new, and torah what they Bhould do about the funeral, because the "ill did not my whether Me wan to be buried iv crennted. Ile promptly wired back " Both. Taken risks." 'Loud laoshter. Well, that wos the way they were going to treat Protection. 'Cheers.) They were not going to shirk the issue, but to fight it to a finish. (Cheers.) Whatever great force, or poser of the puree were to be brought agniust them, they must not be satisfied until—to 119 P cricket porlanco—they hal captured a third wicket aith the (math ball of the over. (Cheem). Apart from the old fallacies he had referred to, the tariff reform meeting am chiody devoted to reference! to Mr. Co/weber- lain. Now the Liberal party, he was glad to my, had never heeitated to express ito sympathy St the rather tragic epectacle of the old man laid asiife from active work. " I may say " continued Mr. Crortisld. " that for my port I have never tailed to admire either the courage, the energy or, in some ports of his career, the good and democra