      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-05_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>TORY AIMS.,</head>
        <p>TORY AIMS.,</p>
        <p>The CIIAIIIN kg, who was cordially welcomed, said he would like in the first place to congratulate Mr Peacock and Mr. New on the fact thtt they were that night opsning the new Liberal Lsti.iire Von. (CheerS.) 'Caere were a considerable number of friends who had assisted those gentlemen, but it was rnaiuly due to their efforts that they now bail that Liberal von practieally Seer of debt. ((Seers.) Mr. Ne v bad worked splendidly end he (the spesker) truste I that the rank and Me would rally reined him in the work he was doing to prepire them for the nem election ant for victory. ,Cheers) Long after the Veto Bill had been placed on the Statute iteok, and the falsely-named crane of tariff reform had preyed away, to be remembered only as the other three silly attempts to take the country back to Protecti in were remembered, the Liberal van would stand in Warrington, and frost ita platform Liberal principles wantil be maintoined and upheld. (Clews ) He could very well leave the questions of current politics, tempting as they were, to Me. Madilson and Mr. Crosfleld, and confine his remarks to the principle, that tho van would stand for. It was a Libeal van, and s. - • •</p>
        <p>IInER ALISM STOOD ABOVE ALL THINns AS AN ENI.RKiS ON or T. I: CREED OF DE,IOCRAFT.</p>
        <p>tHear,hear.t That creed wog somet lines embodied in the words" rule for the potpie and by the people," but th It scarcely covered the eh de ground. The essence of true Liberialism and of dernocrney was the proclamation, and the enforcement of tha right of all to share in the whole heritage of man. (Cheers) Its proviace was to see titot no one should, hy accident of birth or position be shut out from all the rights and privileges which ought to accompany citizenship. Liberalism had dons much to tithe this England of ours great, great not merely in possession of territory and gold, but great lit justice, in progress, in liberty. Liberalism had freed our trade, tmaocpnted us from tests, from injustices it had broadened the basis of tho franchise, It bad limited the power of privilege, prefer..re and protection. Liberalism stood for freedom in the littlest and widest sense of the word—icheersi—e freeLtm which not only emancipated fro to tr sir, dis abilities, end disfrAnchisement, hut also ft All those unjust social conditions which made it impossible for a large number of our feliows to live the life of free men. The danger in this count, was not, as Tories thought, that we should experiment too rashly but that we should AC, EVE THE FANNIN,. ORDEN Tao COMPLACENTLY. (Bear, hear ) Liberals claimed that the people of this wealthy nation should Imre their (sir share in the wealth sod contentemot that eaisted. Now whit were the principles of those on the other side in regard ti progre, ? Far he it from him toot tira any sort of perfection for the Liberals or to asstrt that the Liberal party possessed all the virtues. lie quite reoegnized that the Conservative party was one that had ire rises in the Sate. But in regard to progress if they moved forward at all it was with en att'tude of anspicion, of reluctance, and of compulsory resignation. tyeey ft ught against every progressive measure and only submitted when they could not help themselves. ale., hear.) It was a case cf those behind crying, " Forward" and those before crying "Dab." But Toryism had characteristics much more objeetiopside than distrust of the people's rule. Toryism was the embodiment of the creed (anti too often the gte.d) of thspmestairg classes; it was Ilia .....</p>
        <p>CITY 1!F</p>
        <p>for those wilose object and whose interest it was to uphold stewed interests. And no they had the psrsons, the Peers, and the brewers all under the shelter of the wan which usually stood on the other side of the road. (Laughter.) There one not a reform wonted by people of this country, but which, as it was being pushed for- ward, tan sgainst some wall of privilege, preference or ' monopoly. We wanted better houses, better homes, end the privileged interest of the lend monopoly stood in our way. (Hear, hear.) We wanted the people to have on effactual control or the liquor traffic. 'I he brewing interest stood at guard end dared the country to touch its privileged position. We wanted the right of the people to go, ern themselves sad control their own destinies through their own elected representatives. No! said the members of the privileged limns who held their place by birth end not by election, "Wears the sons of our fathers and we contend that, we have the right and wisdom to interpret the will of the nation better thsn the nation itself." Every teal ad venue and every step for the betterment of the people had to encounter privileged monopoly sad power. And to he raid, " Success to the Liters] van. (Cheers). May it ever stand for progress and liberty, and may those who *peak front it never be afraid to preach the full principles of democracy and progress." (Loud cheers.i.</p>
