        <p>A memorable meeting closed with the National Anthem which was sung with great fervour.</p>
        <p>REMAREABLE ENTHUSIASM,</p>
        <p>A procession Wee then formed hesdel by the Brick- street Band. Amid FrAlled of tremendous enthusiasm Mr. cod Mre. Crosfield, Mr. l'arker and Mr. New were esc-irtril in an open mirriage through the main et revts to the Liberal Club. A hare coed 101 l -sired the vehicle and a continuous storm of cheering was kept lip. On teaching the Club the thoussole in 11,Kseg-street clamour.' for speech from Mr. Croafield and when he sppeßred St the window there was a SCOne teminiscont of. great triumph at a general elation; the people waving their Oats and handkerchiefs and crying Crosaeld for ever." .</p>
        <p>In the course of his rpeech Mr. ritoseirT,l) slid that the meeting had bee, held that night in over to celebrate one of the greatest, and most triomphant events in English hintory. Though they were beaten in Warrington at the butt election they we e only the more determined that their politiost hones should be thuronghly set in order and so In the future they intended to wink shoulder to shoulder In order to obtain another grand victory at the polls. (Cheers.) Mr. Parker and Mr. Now also addressed • few words and then the meeting broke up.</p>
