      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-05_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>DANGEROUS FIRE DISPLAY.</head>
        <p>A DANGEROUS FIRE DISPLAY.</p>
        <p>One item in • Scout display at Sunderland on S.tttr'lry, iefore 2,000 people, was the rescue of two Scouts from the second storey of • woad and oiled canvas building. Owing to a misunderstood @lanai the building was filed before the resole party mu notified. The 11snort instantly enveloped the atructure, but the boys jumped to the std.&amp; One was uninpired and the otter burned on the arm and hid all bis hair singed off.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-05_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>HEATED ELECTIONEERINO.</p>
        <p>Does anyone ask whence come I this week, my answer ti—from going to nail fro in the Middleton Divinion and f,oav walking up and down in it ; and in peregrinations I have wen and heard strange things. I hare come across at least one ardent young War- rington politician enduring the hardness of open-air disciission in defence and furtherance of the came of Tariff Deform, so-called, and suffering the slings and arrows cf outrageous Itadic.l workingmen. who not only declined to dance to his persistent piping and that of his fellowcrusaders of the wealthy League.but laughed the elaborate organisation to worn and made light of the moat plamible arguments of its indefatig- able agents. Even at outdoor meetings in the oorn- parative coal of the evening, it was warm work fur the enthusiastic Warringtcnian and his friends. Day after day until the going down of the sun they strove earutelly with the enemy in the gate, and altogether displayed amazing determination. IA sure they had a hackneyed wory, the points of such are worn exceeding thin, Litt that made their audacity the more noteworthy. A CIIIVALROrS WARRINGTONIAN.</p>
        <p>More than once in the crgons of my tour of the Palliati”ntaly Disition in which Sir Rlland kdkins vits early called upon once more to fight for Ilia dI encounter the little !mod from Warrington and the region round about endeavouring to hold the fort for 'lliad! Reform. One of theca occasions W. at the eastern extremity of the comet imericy. They hod decided to unfuil their banner to such slight breeze as there was on the market wound ,if the borcugh of Todmorden, which straddles across the county border, but there found two rival hosts also ili warch or actual possession of " pitches." With one of these. representing the Iron Trade League, of ' course it nos a case of war to the knife : but with the others, a party of lady suffragists who had journeyed thither in a " swank" ni ,tor car, driven by the incipal advocate herself a young person of con- siderable personal charm - c mpromise was found to Ire by no means impossible. Thanks apparently to the tact and courtesy of the gallant Warringtonian in the straw hat, the la-dies had first innings, attracting an immense multitude ; then tine Tarillists. w-ho had meanwhile joined the other crowd in order to fortify themselves with material upon which to base their address., mounted the rcstrum and "went for" the big audiences which they had ready made to their hand. A ROLAND FOR AN OLIVER.</p>
        <p>lie that goeth toh-thumping in public place. within the meaning of the Act should be smart at repartee. but the prick retort in each circumstances is all the better when it does dot smell of the lamp. Of such ea, that of in aged hearer at one of last week's meeting.. The speaker, oracularly began began : "</p>
        <p>" Sixty years ago" - and the old run'n alore"said completed the isenteme with , '' I was working for eighteenpence a day." It was a good and a fair hit, and the interrupted declaration lost all its intended impressiveness. Such instances might be multiplied, tot I must confine myself to the Warrington viattor, who did protest too much. Ile was s, anxious to score ash a brand new retort evidently prepared for such occasions that the moment he was interrupted he exclaimed : If fist rnan fell into the river he would he drowned, bee.as he can't keep his mouth shut." " 0 wad some power... The 'lw.lnfi,to sprang involitot wily to my lips. Talk of Satait reproving sin! The allinsio I was one of the morn unfortunate possible for the speaker himself. for lie happened himself to be afflicted to a quite abnormal degree with the very physical defect which he thus put to the account or hisunknown opponent. Ile was happier in. later impromptu. One in his audience wished him plainly to nutlet-wand that it waa worse than waste of time and energy on the tart of any spouter from Warrington or elsewhere to reach such false doctrine to lAnc.asliire cotton operatives. " but," was that ready resit mere, "you are only one, and we are hoping Ii c,invert a fee hundreds in Middleton, as we did in Warrington at the general election, turning our small minority int a substantial majority. We coins not to call the righteous, but sinners to repentance." MANNERS.</p>
        <p>If it be still true that" manners makyth man," our outlook is not encouraging -.that is, if a strict litmal interpretation is to be put upon all that Arch- deacon Cunningham ha) to say of the mother on Bootle, Grammar School Speech Day at the gathering in the Parr Hall. That many others share the Yen. Arch- deacon's view needs no proof. " The !rare and happineis of many families are being ruined by the lack of discipline of the children, who are permitted lobe a law unto themselves," said the Earl of Meath at a Health Congress at Birkenhead a while au,. Sir Thomas Oliver holds that children are worse mannered to-lay, and get beyond parental c in- trol stall earlier age than at any period during the last century. A writer in " Baby'. World " stator he heard the head 'f a preat and succe-sful undertaking make the remark," 'here is no time for politeness irs an office." and pertinently asks - if this is the real truth, why is it that there is always time f y curie- neon? Ititilenes• certainly requires more time and more energy than politenti., and never, never brings hick a return in val.. Take as example . Coldly Angry Person " You're a liar, a thief, an idiot, a scoundrel, a fool—in fact, there isn't a redeeming feature in rue character." Mil I Person " Yin hut otherwise I'm all right, ain't I Seriously, Swift wrote that the company a man kept could be td I, at once by the manner in which • matt expressed himself ; and how easy it into tell the person who has line I amid surroundings where it has never been considered worth while to cultivate the more polished alumni'en of life. Yet there is actually a bock with the title How info happy though It is by the Rev. E. J Hardy, a witty and occasionally severe critic, who thinks too many in all clasKes of s ciety are gums over to vulgarity of speech and manners. Ile rightly exalts and commends the virtues of civility, courtesy, simplicity of speech and 00l behaviour. Another writer on the subj,ct insists that tue politeness must be taught to every -bill from babyho of. The hops should he taught • chivalrous relyect for womanhood, whenever and wherever they mett with it, for the sake of the woman who has brought them into the world, and relic has lavishe I tender love. pi ide, and care upon does. They grow the more manly, the mare moral, the more lovable in after-life when brought up to protect and honour women. lade girls must learn to show deference, reverence. and respect for those of either wx who are getting on in yearn, and g radually learn to render helpful litt'e services in a form which will make their childhood full of hippy memories to those who have watched them grow up. Mr. Hardy's complaint is that there is at the present time • want of reverence :or everything in !leaven and earth, and this expresses itself' in a disregard for the fee rugs of others, which is the eveenee of bad mentors.</p>
        <p>Listen to the talk or • men of seventy yeses of age. and then to that of one hetwer n twenty and thirty. The former clothe. his thoughts ins correct morning coat, the latter in a loose lounge coat. Ile tells of one school stall events where manners •r, considered of as much immotarce as arithmetic or geography. Lately, he us, vi•iting a ,ry poor woman, and she produced and showed with pride a price for polite- ness which her son had gained at this school. It would be wed if politeness sere encouraged like this in all schools, for though •Il boys cannot be clever or . .</p>
        <p>learned, all can be unaelfiell and well-mannered. A. 4</p>
        <p>if they are, they will gain valuable prizes in after.life. That, I take it, ia the moral which Archdeacon. Cunningham would have drawn by all aho heard him and all aho read the report of Lts address the other duo. OUR CITIZEN SOLDIERS.</p>
        <p>On Sunday last the Warrington " Terriers " went into camp for their annual training—which, when I beheld Ise Bunyan writes of the pilgrim's admin.., to the Celestial City), I hid wish myself among them. I hare been there, many a time and oft, and still would go. I have repeatedly written from the tented field ; this time I ha,. to commend, and may even presnme to advise. cur citizen soldiers from the office desk. As Mr. Haldane obaerve,l ioofening a new drill hall not far from Warrington • little while .so, the territorial force is something tome than a military force. It is part of the great oganised life of the State on the lines that religion, education, and public life have been organised. I recall Lord Kitchener's addrem to the Atiatralian public respecting She earn. subject_ S.:id he .• The fore° must be an iniggral portion of tire national life. The citizen should be brought up from boyhood to look forward to the day when he will 14, enrolled as fit to defend his country : and he should Ire accustomed to practice them habits of self-deuial of devotion to and emulation in the execution of his duty, of reticence and of pr mpt olwilience to lawful authority, athich are essential to the formsti. of . . , _ . .</p>
        <p>patriotic and efficient cithen soldiers." Surely these word, will be nr,st be accepted aa ably spitomg tbe whole ccxlecf soldierly duty; anyhow, believers ia</p>
        <p>I and supporter. of our territoria system will cordially echo them.</p>
      </div>
