      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-05_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>Miscellaneous.</head>
        <p>Miscellaneous.</p>
        <p>Medical.</p>
        <p>TIIE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>\ I ['RDAS'. AUGUST sth IsIL</p>
        <p>The Lords are to have THE FINAL one more chance of CHANCE, bowing to the people's will before the Royal prerogative is brought into use. The latest phase of the situation is the announcement 1 that the Cabinet have decided to send the Veto Bill back to the House of Lords with- out any reinforcement of Liberal peers. It is believed that, allowing for the abstention of Lord LANsnOwNE's followers and the opposition of Lord HALSBURY'S friends. the Bill will pass by a small majority But if the "Die Hards " should succeed in collecting enough men to defeat the Govern- ment on Wednesday next the Government will then proceed immediately to the creation of peers on a large scale—sufficient, in other words, to give them a majority against all possible combinations. In the meantime there are to be two motions of censure on the Government. Ono of them will unquestionably receive over- whelming support, for it will be brought forward in the House of Lords by Viscount Cu RZON. Nothing will be( proved by it. That the House of Lords dislikes Liberal Govern- ments does not require to be proved, and the cendure of the Government by the Upper Chamber simply produces a situation which is described with some particularity by the late WALTER BAG EHOT in his very sound work on the Constitution. Mr. BAGEHOT explained that the House of Lords censures Governments in vain. The Government</p>
        <p>is entitled to say as the lady says in Skakespeare's comedy: "You may talk as much as you like, Signor Benedick, nobody marks you." As a matter of fact it makes no observation whatever,but goes on with its work as if nothing had happened. Nothing so much emphasised the inferior place of the House of Lords in the Constitution as this inability to influence policy by means of a censure of the Ministers of the Crown. If the vote of the House of Lords were absolutely unanimous in such a case the result would be the same. The situation would be just what it was before. A motion of censure in the House of Commons is an entirely different affair. If it were carried on a division it would smash the Government at once. There would be an immediate resignation, a chang ing of places and possibly a general election. But we may be certain that nothing of this kind is expected by Mr. BALFOUR. His motion will be defeated in the House of Commons by a majority probably as large as that which</p>
        <p>will carry the motion of Lord CURZON in the House of Lords. But it will have been a demonstration of the possible unanimity on certain occasions which is not merely divided but actually torn in strips. It will, too, have had its justification on other grounds. It will have given Mr. BALFOUR the oppor- tunity of showing, as he most surely will. that if he is to be driven from the leadership of his party he must also be driven from Parliament. We have had the strongest reasons for disagreeing with him at almost every stage of what the future historian must regard as a singularly diversified &lt;•areer. But we are not in the least disposed ,to think that the Conservative party has at its service at the present time a member of even half his talent for leadership. The motion of censure gives him an opportunity not only of rounding up his party for the moment but of making a fine display of intellectual ability. The term; of Mr. BALFOUR'S motion are such as might have been expected. They do not express a reality ; they will no more command a public assent than did Lord HALSBURY'S suggestion of an impeachment of the PRIME MINISTER. The leader of the " die-bards." and the leader whom he praises and declines to follow each strikes a false note. Mr. BALFOUR'S motion of censure says that obtaining from the KING a pledge that a sufficient number of 'peers will be created to pass the Parlia- ment Bill is a gross violation of constitu- tional liberty. It is, of course, nothing of the kind, and that, in fact, it is the method provided by the Constitution for the preven- tion of the establishment of an oligarchy. The course that may be recommended to his Majesty by Mr. ASQUITH was followed in connection with the the Treaty of Utrecht. The same power that the Prime Minister has ehind him at this moment was behind Lord GREY when the first Reform Bill was passed. The power that is now held in reserve on that occasion was not exercised only because, on the advice of the DUKE OF WELLINGTON, the Tory peers gave way. Mr. BALFOUR'S motion, therefore, is merely a form of words which will give occasion for speeches to those gentlemen who last week liberated their ouls in other ways—and, of course, to there.</p>
        <p>Although one or two HAROLD AND THE of the " Hughligans " " HUGHLIOANS. " have had the temerity</p>
        <p>to attempt to justify he disgraceful scene in the House of Commons last week, Mr. Harold Smith, M.P. has SO far been silent on the subject. Perhaps he is not particularly wishful that his constituents should class him with the rowdies, but many of them are already doing so. They argue that as "FE." was one of the ring-leaders in the organized attack on the Premier, it can safely be assumed that brother Hsrold would not be far away.At all events, some statement or explanation from the member is called for. In the absence of a disclaimer, electors are expressing indigna- tion at the possible association of the name</p>
        <p>of Warriniton with Ituit—Week's."-oui;lge on parliamentary traditions and reckless ..</p>
        <p>bresoh of the courtesies of debate. The participation of the member for - Warrington in su:h political excesses— which, be it remembered, have called forth stern rebuke from influential men of all parties—would, in their view, be distinctly lowering to the reputation of the borough. They are wondering whether Mr. Smith can possibly have anything to say in condonation of the whole discreditable business. He has already declared his allegiance to the anti- Balfourites. Will he next avow himself an unrepentant " Hughligan "?</p>
        <p>Mr. Harold Smith, THE TORY REBELS. M.P., is making no secret of his alliance with the "no surrender" faction. After his exertions as ticket-seller for the Halsbury banquet he found time to send a message to the young bloods of the local Conservative organisation, counting upon their support in the conspiracy to which he has now openly committed himself. To the habitues of the</p>
        <p>cen'as of light and leading in (go; I Le, loyalty to the tried and hitherto trusted leader of the Conservative party may seem a comparatively trifling matter, and the juniors will no doubt be content to play the inglorious part of humbly following where Mr. Smith leads. We must, however, confess to some surprise that the Chairman of the parent association (Mr. W. Peter Rylands) is found siding with the extremists. He may, of course, have persuaded himself that the best way of showing confidence in Mr. Balfour is to scorn his advice, but there is no disguising the fact that the " die-hards " are intent upon undermining Mr. Balfour's authority and, if possible, supplanting him in his present position. The Conservative Press is almost unanimous in its condemnation of the rebellion and the "Daily Mail" has had some exceedingly caustic things to say of Mr. F. E. Smith and his fellow-accomplices. Having plainly dubbed the action of those who pose as followers of Lord Halsbury as a " plot against Mr. Balfour," that journal goes on to say As to the motives i tispiiing Lord Halshury's so-called followers there used be no delusion about them. These people have no other motive than to depose Mr. Balfour from the leadership of the Unionist Party. They are confirmed and inveterate intriguers and deserters. It ii not their first cabal, and since its doomed to failure it •ill not be tlwir last. They are using Lord Halsbury on a stalking-horse, and we believe Lord &amp;Urn° as well. These gentlemen are no doubt perfectly sincere in their wrong-headed views of the sittution, hut it will not be long before they realise, if they have not yet begun to do so, the nee which is being made of them. 'those who are exploiting them have, as we have said, no misapprehension 88 to the meaning of their action. It is tat disloyalty, and its methods are sheer insincerity. Na Unionist who realises this will have anything to do with them.</p>
        <p>- - Mr. J. St. Loe Strachey, editor of the Spectator" is equally blunt. He writes However disguised, the real point at inane is whether the will of Lord Lansdowne and Mr Bantur is to prevail or the will of Mr. Austen Chamberlain and Mr. F. E. Smith. If the will of Mr. Austen Chamberlain and Mr. F. E. Smith prevail. and the Peers are created, Ixtrd Lansdowne and Mr. Balfoir and three of their colleagues who have stood loyally by them must matte to he factor, f importance in the conduct and management of the party. They may remain I, • few months, Cr even a year, as nominal leaders, but the control of the party will have passed to the men who have scouted the advice .given in Mr. Balfour's letter, and who have in . •</p>
        <p>....- Tflect described it.. a disgrace. The prestige of victory still be with Mr. Aueten Chamberlain and Mr. F. E. Smith. Remember that they are men nurtured in the political tradition that no quarter should be given in polities and that the spoils should tort° the victor.. Tinge two maxims are</p>
        <p>certain to be put into action. Surely, then, thom saho care for the internal welfare of the Unionist Party and who have confidence in Mr. Balfour and Lord I.los Mane, must do their best to prevent the creation of Peers and the conseittent enthronement of the leaders of the Efalsbury faction as the rulers of the party. The prevention of a cre3ti,n2 of Peers hen become ~mild not •nly on its merits, but in order to save the Umonist Party. This internecine warfare in the Conser- vative camp, unedifying as it is, has undoubtedly an amusing side, for have not the Conservatives repeatedly posed as the great united party and eagerly striven to , magnify any semblance of dissension in the progre3sive ranks? Unhappy indeed is the present plight of the Lords' defenders, for, realising that defeat is inevitable, they are devoting their energies to unseemly quarrels as to whether the Peers shall die in the first or last ditch. Whatever be the outcome, Liberals can view the situation with the utmost calmness, for they know that the will of the people is at last to prevail and that Liberal measures will no longer be kept from becoming law by the ruthless obstruction of a selfish and violently partizan Upper House</p>
        <p>These are stirring days A MEMORABLE for Liberalism every- MEETING, where and in Warring- ton the outlook is full of promise and encouragement. The magnificent open-air meeting at Bridge Foot on Saturday evening marked the opening of a campaign which we believe is destined to make ample amends for the mistake of last December and regain for Warrington its rightful place among the progressive indus- trial constituencies of Britain. There is no sign of depression or pessimism in the local Liberal ranks. Everywhere one meets with a spirit of cheery enthusiasm and of earnest determination to wrest the representation of the borough from the reactionary party. The note of triumph was dominant at Saturday's memorable meeting. And was there not good reason for it? This is the, triumphant day of Liberalism for, although Warrington went wrong in December. the kingdom as a whole declared with no uncertain voice in favour of freedom of government and of trade. No longer will a non-elected, hereditary Chamber be able to over-ride the will of the democracy as expressed in the people's House. The unqualified veto of the Lords—for generations the despair of the social reformer— is to be swept away. The Parliament Bill, which in a truly statesmanlike way secures the supremacy of the elected assembly and allots to the Lords their proper functions of revision and delay, will soon be on the statute book. It matters nothing whether a few Peers prefer to die in the "last ditch" instead of calmly accepting the inevitable. The Parliamentßill will become law and democracy —too long denied its just rights—will at last enter into its own. It was therefore not surprising that the meeting on Saturday should have taken the form of a celebration of victory. And it was in every respect worthy of the occasion. The attendance was by general consent the largest ever seen at an open-air meeting in Warrington and a splendid tone pervaded the whole proceed- ings. With absolute unanimity a resolution of confidente in Mr. Asquith and his Cabinet was passed and every reference to the Government's Constitutional and social policies was cheered to the echo. The new Liberal van, which will be an important means of disseminating progressive views in every part of the constituency, was started on its career under the happiest auspices and between now and the next election every opportunity will be taken to keep the electors in close touch with the political situation and to ensure a decisive verdict for Liberalism whenever the trial of strength comes.</p>
        <p>The speeches sounded THE SPEECHES, the right notes of loyalty to the principles and ideals of Liberalism, and of calm con- fidence in the present political situation. As the new van is to be the means of spreading a knowledge of all that the progressive forces stand for in the constituency, it was a happy idea on Mr. .3arker's part to devote his speech to an examination of the essential diffdrence between Liberal and Tory aims. While Liberalism is the expression of the creed of democracy and would give equality of opportunity to all, irrespective of position and influence, the Tory party was aptly described by Mr. Parker as the great city of refuge for those who seek to uphold vested interests, privilege and monopoly. The electors of Warrington have no need to look beyond the borders of their own constituency to appreciate the truth of this statement. Liberalism has, however, beaten the strongest forces of selfishness and reaction before, and it will do so again. Mr. A. H. iCrosfield was in first-class speaking form and delivered an excellent fighting speech on thoroughly argumentative and forceful lines. He was voicing the feelings of all who have a true regard for the dignity and decency of Parlia- mentary life when he condemned in scathing terms the outrageous conduct of the " Hughligans " in shouting down the Prime Minister. These rowdies of the "gentle- manly " party have attained a little notoriety, but they have belittled them- selves and injured their cause—even</p>
        <p>in the eyes of political allies. IsitTorosWid did not hesitate to emphasise the real, sinister object of the "no surrender" move- ment—a deliberate and ungrateful attempt to depose Mr. Balfour from his leadership of the Tory forces in the Commons. The Conservative party as a whole, however, shows no disposition to entrust itsrarlia- mentary fortunes to Mr. Austen Chamberlain, Mr. F. E. Smith and their fellow rebels, and there is every indication that their bid for the reins of power will end in an ignominious fizzle. Mr. Fred Maddison is one of those sterling, outspoken Labour men who always command the respect of the workers. He is an old favourite with Warrington progressives and they were delighted to find him as vigorous and unyielding as ever in his advocacy ot democratic reform, his speech being a model of lucidity, good humour and comprehensiveness.</p>
        <p>The attitude which is TORN CoNSTITU- taken up by the Die- TIONALISII. Hards is at least instructive. It has demonstrated beyond a shadow of doubt the enormous sham perpetrated on the nation when the Tory Party posed as the constitu- tional Party, and the Party of law and order. When the Tories could strain the constitution to its utmost limit, as in the rejection of the Budget, in order to get their own ends, they professed themselves con- stitutionalists. To-day they bid defiance to the forms, traditions, and rules of Parlia- mentary procedure, and they even threaten physical violence and armed resistance if legislation which they dislike should be carried through the ordinary stages. Their doctrine is revealed in its unashamed naked- ness as the doctrine that the Constitution was made for the Tories, and if they cannot be its master they will never be its servants.</p>
        <p>Lord Selborne, father LORD SELRORNE of Lord Wolmer, M.P. . .</p>
        <p>AND PATRONAGE, and one of the leaders of the "Die Herds," has recently been waxing virtuouily indignant at the "unblushing effrontery" with which he alleges the present Government conduct their system of political patronage. He pro- fesses great anxiety that patronage should be governed solely by merit, without regard to favour or affection. This has moved Mr. Swift Macneill, M.P. to write a trenchant letter to the Press showing that Lord Selborne is himself a contradiction to the homily of which he is the preacher. The following extracts are instructive : '' Lord Selborne was Under. Secretary of State for the Voloniss at a s dray of t 1,500 a year from 1890 to 10041. He was First Lord of the Adtriralty from 1900 to 1905, at a salary of £4 500, sod from 1905 to 1910 he was High Commissioner of South Africa and Governor of the Transvaal and Orange River Colony at a stlary of £ll,OOO per annum and allowances. To the p *Won of Under-Semetary for the Colonies and to that of First Lord of the Admiralty be was appointed by the f orour of his father-in law, the late Marquis of Salisbury, who was then Prime Minister. To the position of High Commissioner of South Africa he M. appointed by his first cousin, Mr. Balfour, the Prime Minister in Cabinet on the eve of Ps fall, which notoriously had lost the confider:eel of the country, and which resigned office before a dissolution because it dried not face a diegosted people. When Lord Selborne was a member of the Tory Administra- tion it was 8, constituted as to provoke L rd Dosebery's genial criticism that Lord Salisbury, at Chrieimes, which is pre-rminently a family festival, would have the happiness of seeing around him on members of his own domestic circle one out of every three men in his Adminis- tration." _</p>
        <p>neill further points out that Lord Selborne has received between £135,000 and £90,000 from Crown offices, conferred in turn, as a politician. His father, the first Earl of Selbourne, must have drawn something closely approaching £200,000 from similar sources. "A sum merging on £300,000 obtained already by father and son from offices as politicians" caustically adds Mr. Macneill " shows that the Selborne earls have not been treading the vale of politics without scrip and staff."</p>
        <p>A proposal has been ORANGEMEN IN made ~.by .an Irish</p>
        <p>LONDON. Privy Councillor which, if it is carried out, will add greatly to the gaiety of London, and will have a very stimulating effect upon the cause of Home Rule. This gentleman pro- poses that when a Home Rule Bill again is introduced, the " Heroic Men of Ulster" should charter ships and special trains to convey them to Lonaon, and assemble some fine morning "in Trafalgar Square, in the heart of London, the scene of many great meetings in the past." Subsequently, says this ingenious Privy Councillor," they could march through the streets of London with their bands and banners, and on the way they might call at Buckingham Palace, not to threaten the King, but to proclaim their loyalty." He suggested that it would create an "enormous impression" if "some night thousands of , loyalists sailed for London from Belfast, Derry, Dublin, and other ports." Agreed.' This is an excellent plan, and every Radical' and Nationalist in the country will con- I tribute to the expense of having it carried out. There is one stipulation, however "The Heroic Men of Ulster" must march through the streets of London as they have been marching through the streets of Belfast on each 12th of July for the last fifty years —that is, with their drums, their bludgeons, their deacon-poles, and their swords, waving their ridiculous banners with repre- sentations of King William 111. on a white,' horse crossing the :iver Boyne. It will be ,an object-lesson from which Toryism in London will not recover for at least fifty I years and which will result in the collapse of all opposition to Home Rule.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-05_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</head>
        <p>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</p>
        <p>Householders on holiday bent would do well to ask the police to keep an eye on their premises during their absence from the town. In one Wm rington case in which this precaution was neglected, housebreakers had a happy time this week, the place being entered and ransacked at leisure, without risk of disturbance. _</p>
        <p>• • • Once more the Town Council was engaged in talking about Warrington Bridge on Tuesday evening, but the ratepayers will approve Alderman Tinnion's terse observation that 'too much time was spent in talking about the bridge. It was time they got on with the work." Now that the contract has been signed we should be well on the way to something tangible, but it is becoming monotonous, as well as most uncertain, to forecast the day when a start will be made. We had almost forgotten Howley Bridge —and so apparently had many members of ' the Town Council until the Chairman of the Bridges Cotnmittee resurrected the subject on Tuesday. However, the Deputy Town Clerk says the bridge may be an accomplished fact in six months, HO there is hope for Howley bowlers who want to use the new green in Victoria Park next season. . .</p>
        <p>Congratulations to-the. Mayor on receiving the King's Coronation medal! No chief magistrate could have proved himself more worthy of the gift, for the local Coronation 'arrangements made exceptional demands upon his Worship's time and called for no small degree of self-sacrifice. Dr. Joseph, however, did the work with characteristic thoroughness and the thanks which the Council so warmly accorded to him on Tuesday will be endorsed by the townspeople generally. _ .</p>
        <p>It is questionable if we have progressed very much in the matter of life-saving. Even to-day it is not every experienced ' swimmer who knows how to approach a drowning person safely or to avoid the terrible death-clutch. Again (says the "County Gentleman ") it is not every person who knows how to effect resuscitation when a rescued man or woman has been brought to the bank or shore. Therefore our authori- ties should continue to encourage by every possible means the use of swimming baths by children of both sexes; and the life-saving drill should be a compulsory item of the curriculum in every. school.</p>
        <p>The sensation recently caused in the House of Commons by the appearance of a member in a straw hat serves to bring home the change which has come over the House in these matters of recent years. Time was when the conventional silk hat was the only wear at Westminster, but this has now long since passed. The Labour members led the way (who will forget the sensation caused by Mr. Kier Hardie s deer-stalker ?), and in due course others have followed their sensible example. Mr. Lionel Rothschild once attempted a compromise by appearing in a straw topper," but after wearing this remarkable creation once he never appeared in it again.</p>
        <p>There to.e unpleasant times ahead for residents of Le:lli-street and Sankey-street, and those who have to traverse those thoroughfares daily. The water main in process of com.truction is rapidly nearing the centre of the Own, and soon we shall have some of those delightful excavations which divert traffic and cause multitudes to blaspheme. However, we have the assurance of the chairman of the committee (Alderman John Evans), that by working night and day the scheme will he pushed forward with all possible haste and the wood pavement in Sankey-street—of which the Paving and Sewerage Committee is particularly proud— is to be treated with merited tenderness and care.</p>
        <p>* * * In his capital speech at the open-air meet- ing on Saturday evening Mr. A. H. Crosfield told the tariff " reform are" that the local Liberals would always be prepared to tackle them on their own ground.An instance of this willingness to thrash out the question was forthcoming on Thursday evening when Mr. Harry New had a debate at Bridge Fo3t with Mr. Henshall, a Manchester tariff " reform " lecturer. There was a remarkably large attendance and the discussion was followed with the keenest interest. Mr. H. New expounded the Free Trade case in a most convincing manner and had no difficulty in demonstrating the fallacy of Protectionist contentions. He undoubtedly had the better of the argument and at the close of the meeting his supporters accorded him quite an ovation.</p>
        <p>• ° • Mr. Masterman has recived many con- gratulations on his victory at Bethnal, including telegrams from the Premier. Mr. Lloyd George and Mr. Winston Churchill. It was indeed a triumph to be proud of. Mr. Masterman has fought against tremendous odds. In fact, forces were arrayed against him that had never been brought into opera- tion against Mr. Pickersgill during the whole of his half-dozen contests. The most unscrupulous methods of controversy and the grossest personal abuse were brought into service as a despairing attempt to cap- ture the constituency. Had Mr. Masterman failed to keep the seat for the Liberals there would have been abundant material on which to have based one or two libel action,.</p>
        <p>4*. It was like an el ec tion night at Warrington on Saturday. After the remarkable demon- stration at Bridge Foot, Mr. and Mrs. A. H. Crosfield made a triumphal progress to the Liberal Club, escorted by thousands of cheering supporters, and the great crowd would not be satisfied until Mr. Crosfield had addressed them from the window of the club. Tories looked on in amazement at the unmistakable proof of the enthusiasm and determination of the Liberal sof Warrington. The local workers for the cause were never in better heart or fighting trim and if thet present spirit is maintained Warrington will at the first opportunity reverse its rash verdic of last December and once more be numbered with the progressive constituencies of England.</p>
        <p>• • • The Warrington Territorials have been roughing it at Farleton camp this week. No longer is the fortnight under canvas contemptuously termed "playing at soldiers." It has become a serious military business with a definite object. Moreover each succeeding camp gives proof of the increased usefulness of the force. General Bethune, who is again in command of the West Lancashire Division this year, frankly con- fesses that the progress made by the Division during the past three years has passed far beyond the limit set in his most fanciful dreams. He was more than pleased With the conduct of the Division at Caerwys in 1909, he was delighted with the way the men worked when in Lune Valley a year ago,and he will be bitterly disappointed, he openly admits, if the improvement shown at Farleton is proportionately lees great than that shown a few miles further south in August last.</p>
        <p>-ass Dr. Clifford arrived in London on Sunday, after making a tour of the United States and Canada with the object of addressing meet- ings as delegate representing the League for the Promotion of Friendship bAween IGermany and England, and of presiding over the Conference of the Baptist Alliance at Philadelphia. "My impression of the ' Yankees," said Dr. Clifford," is that they are I not so go ahead as is popularly supposed. In many respects they are behind the English, more particularly in regard to the treatment of the working man, and especially as to the treatment of the working woman, and still more with regard to the treatment of the working child. In all these respects the English are much in advance of the Americans. I mean to tell Mr. Lloyd George that the effect of his democratic measures ,will have an international influence, particularly such a measure as his National Insurance Bill.'</p>
        <p>The victory of Sir Ryland Adkins in Middleton division is striking evidence of the confidence of Lancashire workers in the policy of the present administration. Every effort was made by the Tories to excite fears and prejudices with regard to the Insurance Bill, hut the electors showed themselves alive to the great benefits which this measure will confer upon the country as a whole. I Furthermore they declined to exhibit the slightest sympathy with the Lords in their day of humiliation The Middleton contest brings to a close the long series of by-elec- tions which has marked the early days of the Government. The Opposition have had every opportunity of discovering a change of opinion, if such a change existed, but as a fact the net result of all the by-elections is to show that the country stands where it did last December and last January twelve- months. Here and there, there are small changes in the voting, but the change is inconsiderable, and on the balance the Government is entitled to say that its strength is thoroughly well maintained. • • . .</p>
        <p>Singing, amongst women, is the art of screaming artistically.—Mr. Harrison Frewin in the "Musical Standard."</p>
        <p>There are three classes of English society who are the butts of various individuals— the mother-in-law, the lawyer, and the parson.—Rev. L. S. Robinson at i3radford. You can achieve no laurels in any career if you will not scorn delights and live laborious days.—Dr. Figgis at All Saints', St. Margaret-street. All things are worth to us ultimately just what they cost us.—Rev. G. E. Darlaston at Crouch-end.</p>
        <p>In America the pace for education must be forced, so that culture of the mind becomes a kind of intensive gardening.—.M. C'. Delisle Burns in the" Daily Telegraph." Modern training tends to make young people lacking in grit —Miss E. H. Major at Birmingham. . .</p>
        <p>_ Idleness and ignorance among women, with the frivolity and waste they cause, are dangers to the community.—Miss Be retail at Pendleton.</p>
        <p>For good or bad the music-hall represents the genuine taste for amusement of the people in this country.—Mr. Filson Young in the "Saturday ReTiew."</p>
        <p>I Of pensions enjoyed by peers at the present time, a list of which has recently been furnished by the Chancellor of the Exchequer, in answer to Mr. Kellaway, only Lord Nelson's £5,000 is in the nature of a perpetual pension. Lord Hardinge's £3,009 and the £2,000 pensions of Lord Gough and Lord Napier of Magdala, will terminate with the life of the present holders. The pensions con- ferred on the first Viscount Hardinge (Governor-General of India from 1844 to 1848) and on the first Viscount 'Gough (the victor of Sobraon and Goojerat) were limited to themselves and their two immediate successors to the titles.' The pension conferred on the conqueror of Abyssinia, on the other hand, was for himself and his next surviving male heir only, who lis the present Lord Napier of Magdala. At least one peeress has been in enjoyment of a pension. That is the Dowager Lady Mayo the mother of the present earl, whose pension of £l,OOO per annum was conferred thirty- nine years ago, after the assassination of her husband, the Governor-General of India, in the Andaman Islands.</p>
        <p>• There are people who frighten themselves with the foolish idea that the Germans will some day attack England. I suppose we must have a bogey, but this one seems to me the most absurd and far-fetched ever conjured up in human brain. It is some advantage to have a memory going back for 70 years. I can remember when France was supposed to be our" natural" enemy ; when Russia was foredoomed " by the will of Peter the Great" to gobble us up; when the United States must perforce drift into conflict with us unless the providentially split up in two ; when the great " yellow peril" of China was threatening our posterity ; in fact, I can remember all kinds of imaginary happenings. But I think the wisest Englishmen of all parties have ever in my time seen that in the Germans we have not only near kins- men, but natural co-workers, the two countries being so placed that war between them is out of the question. As it has been in the long past, so it must and willctotthe long future. , Great Britain and ay I are bound together and cannot Edward Owen Greening in "Agricultural Economist and Horticultural Review.'</p>
        <p>The base ingratitude of the plot against Mr. Balfour is the most contemptible feature ,of the "no surrender" movement. Writing ' in" London Opinion" an M.P. thus states the position wonder what Mr. Balfour thinks of the action of some of his friends in raising the s'andard of revolt. Lord lialsbnrv. whom Mr. Balfour initiated on keeping in tile Lord Chancellorship in Mance of the claims of younger and abler men ; Lord Selborne, on wh rn Ito conferred the High Commissionship of South Africa, with a salary uf of £ll,OOO • year; Lord Milner, 'whom he also pushed into public life an I high eroulaments; and Mr. F. E. S.nitb, whom he recently c ius..d to he honoured with • Privy Councillorship, lure all joined the revolt. But perhaps the most c n- epieuous of all is Sir Edward Vireo°, whom Mr. Balfour found a brieflless barrister in Ireland, a. d raised to a Law Officership in great Bri,ain Sir Edward became • police court proseent, in Ireland when 'Mr. Balfour was Chief Secretary. . and was known in those days as "Balfour, Junior'; and his subsequent rapid rise was due almust wholly to Mr. Balfour's loyrltyond interest. Tin one imp rant man amongst the insurgents who owen nothing to him —who, indeed, was abandoned by his leader in a time of stress and trial—is Mr. Wyndham.</p>
        <p>LOCAL PARS.</p>
        <p>Sommer .Camp Trip Postpoad.</p>
        <p>Owi,g to the pie;ure of rpuslic engagements in August, the annual trip t, the Children s Summer Csasp l'iestatyn bus been postponed. Stockton Heath Garden Soelet/. The tenth annual .ummer exhibition in connection with thi, society sill be hod in Wilderspool Park on Saturday, August 12th. Mrs. Legh, of High Legh 1, Hall, ell' pelf rm the opening ceremony. St. James' Church Scout Patrol.</p>
        <p>On Wedn,day evening four member' of this Patrol, If. Woodward, 1. Lightfoot, J. Clarke and F. Slater, ancceintfully passed the examination for the I Ambulance :tadge. Dr. C. li. Law was the examiner Warrington Student's Success. Amongat the auccesiful candidates in the July examination of the Joint Matriculation Board of the I Universities of Manchester, Liverpool, Leeds and Sheffield is William Swains of the Warrington Munkipsl Secondary School. MOTOR CARSI for hire by the day Of sreek•—.7..B GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge it., Warrlngton.—Advt. Death from a Fall.</p>
        <p>Sarah Conntff, the 14 months' old daughter of Thomas Conniff, 152. Evelyn-street, died r n Thursday from concussion of the brain caused by falling from a go-chair on the Ist inst. The inquest will take :ace at the Police Station to-day ;Saturday).</p>
        <p>Aged Wesleyan's Death. We regret to record the death of Mrs. Neary, which took place at her home 5, Allcard-.tree', on Friday night, July 28ili at the ripe age of 81. The deceased lady es.juyed the respect of a large circlu of friends and was one of the oldest members of Ilawsey-road Wesleyan Church.</p>
        <p>Football Club Sports. A splendid list of entries have been received for these annual sporty which will be held on the Wilderspool Ground on Monday. A number of valuable prizes ere offered and a large nember of Sno•n athletes are noticed among the eatries. Given a fine day, a good af ernoon's er joyment is promised. CORRUGATED SHLETS. Wine Netting, Rooßos Felt Eatimatee Free.—J. S. GARLAND, Ironmonger Bridge Warrington.—Advt. Be - opening of Kest - street Lulea • - • • •</p>
        <p>Catchfird.</p>
        <p>1 On Thursday evening next Councillor Henshall and other. will re-open Nentsstreet Mission, Latchford, which has just been enlarged. From Pie 13th inst, to the ISth, Mr. James E. Shaer (Evangelist of Cliff College) will conduct service, every evening. Walton Show. We again call the attention of our renders to the annual show of the Warrington and District Agri- cultural Society which is to take place in Walton Park on the 1 lb iron. Prices, cope and medals to the value of 1:700 will be competed for. The famous bend of the Cheahire .Earli..f Chester's) Yeomanry have arranged to play selection. throughout the day.</p>
        <p>Retire of the 5.3 " Atplesktri." The SS. '' Awlesbire had a iemarkable reception at lAtchford Lock., on Sunday evening last. Hundred, of people visited be locks to eee the largest vessel which has yet passed through the Manchester Ship Canal. It was reported that she wool,' arrive at 10 am. But owing to traffic arrangentents she did not pot in en appearance until 6 o'clock in the evening. She was safely berthed in the' big lock ' for the night, and set sail the following morning as 5.30.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-05_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>Ammonia' Application Ratitiod.</head>
        <p>Ammonia' Application Ratitiod.</p>
        <p>At tbe Warrington .11uty on Thursday. before his Honour Ju lge Stanger, K Samuel John Allen, 23, Elizabeth-4rest. lead worker. applied Ithriongh Mr. A. Browse for .11 order for the regiatra- tion of a mem3randu u of agreement come to between hirneelf and his employers on S.ptemlier 21st, 1907, for the paymint t him of lIC per weak com- pensation by the Meisey White Lead Co. Mr. Cuthbert Smith, barrister finetructed by Ileum. Woods and 53.) appeared for the respondent.. After hearing a long legal argil rient, his Honour refused the application. Mule le the Park.</p>
        <p>To-morrow (Sunday) afternoon Walkers' Brewery Band will play the following programme to the Park ; —Hymn " Veep•r " Charles 11. Motown (War- rington) march, " Steady. and troe " Allsopp ; over- ture, " Teneredi " Bonito ; 'selection " Maritsa" " Wallace; cornet polo, " The song that reached say heart" soloist, Mr. John Peterson ; .election. " Wales " Douglas ; grand march " IL /al Wedding" 11,1•nes .;,1 rave the King.</p>
        <p>Railway Clerks and National Insurance Bill. Al the :numbly meeting of the Warrington Branch of the Railway Clerks Association, held at the Winmsrleigh Cafe on Saturday the following resolu- tion was pained —" This m,etiug heartily endorses the action of the Executive Committee, in striving for complete exemption from the up.ration of the National Im,urance Bill, believing that inclusion would den- mentally effect the conditions at present existing , between the railway companies and their clarion' staff. and esks Mr. Lloyd geom., to aympathetically consider the views of the staff as expressed through the Railway Clerks Association." Death of Mrs. J. Lyon Whittle.</p>
        <p>Deep sympithe lies been expreesel with We. J. Lyon Whittle, Town Clerk of Warringtim, on the death of his wife, who passed away very suddenly in her residence ficse Cottage, Latchford Without, on ISaturday morning. The previous day Mrs. Whittle was in town, end appenred to be in good health. Der death, which was entirely unexpected, was due to the intense heat. The deceased lady enjoyed the respect if,. large circle of Lie:ids. and it was with profound sorrow they heard she hod passed away. The funeral took place in tirappenball Churchyard on Monday in the presence of relatives sod Itienale. Sympathetic allusion to the Town Clerk's bereavement was made by the Mayor at the meeting of the Town Council on Tuesday evening.</p>
        <p>TIMIS. Croquet, Cricket sal other outdoor J. S. S. GARLAND, 51 to 35,11r1dp et, WlUtllsl.oll Picnic and Bowling Male.</p>
        <p>The annual picnic and bowling main in connrc- tion with the focal Exchange no took place on Saturday when the cu-tomera visited the Norton Arms Hotel, Halton. An excellent turnout ma pro- vided by Mr. Magian. The day being an ideal one, the drive was greatly enjoyed. On arriving at the hotel the min was commenced under condition• if anything • little too hot. At the interval an excellent repast was served by the herd and bootees (Mr. and IMrs. Birch). The main was afterwards concluded the I chief prize winners being :—I, Mr. A. Overend ; 2, Mr. J Leah • 3 Mr. J Archer • 4 Mr. C Mil mart was then made for Warrington which ese reached at 10 13. A bealy vote of thanks wars accorded to tradespeople and others in the tow, who No generously gave prizes. Junior Conurtatives En Fete On Saturday last members and f. iends of the I Warrington Junior Coneervative Association hell a fete a.,d e orbs in the splendid grounds attached to the residence of Mrs. Broadbent, The Latch- ford. The prize-winners verse, follow —lOO yards sack race . 1, F Boyle ; 2, J F Furnival ; 3, F Thomason ; 4, A Bibby. 100 cards anted, race I. G Hart 2,11 Roden; 1, T Hughes; 4, 0 Thomas. 20 yards potato gathering race 1, T O'Neill; 2. Smith ; 3, A Sankey; 4, F Thomason. Ds) yards flat race handicap .1, W J Llewellyn ; 2 1 Kean ;3, .1 Boden ; 4, P Shakeshaft, Ward squadron I. How:ey; 2, Orford. Running jump, members under 17 I,l' D O'Neill; 2, D Lythgor ; 3, J 11 Broadhurst ; nvor 17, 1, W Harrop; 2, 1' Sutcliffe ; 3, T Litherland. &amp;uprise packet race for members over 21) : I, T Litherland ; 2, J Griffiths ; 3, 11 Boyle. Mrs. Broad- bent distributed the prizes sod afterwards speeches were delivered by Mr E J Hall, Mr W P Itylanda, 'Alderman Bennett, Mr. Fulton-Soith, and M. Jobx Come.</p>
        <p>GARDM FURNIICRE —Tents from 21/., Ilaromodr Chaim, dm. loopectioo invited.—J. 8. OAKLAND, Iroomooger, Bndg.t,s Warriogtoo.—Advt</p>
