        <p>At the Borcigh Polo,. Station on Monday Mr. S. Brighouse hell an iimuiry into the death of John Berne., aged 39 years, a London and N,irth-Weisters Railway driver. of Dickenson-street. Warringtoo, who was fatally injured at the Wineforil junction of Over end Wharton-station, on Fliday. Mr. Steel, solicitor, appeared for the widow and on behalf of tbe Associated Society of L-comotive Engineers and Firemen. The evidence showed that Barites bad descended into the "misdeal " for the purpose of examining his engine, when he was struck by . passing train. After the accident he was. at his own request.remored to the Warrington Infirmary, where he died shortly after his admission. The widow said she visited her huaband in the Infirmary. He complained of pain in bin back hot gave no account of the accident. In reply to Mr. Steel, John O'Brien. Catherine-street, Warrington, said that there was a curve where the accident happened end the driver of the pinning train wool,] not be Able to see Barnes until be got under Over Bridge, John Iron. of Wharton. said tie was in charge of the '212 train from Over sail Wharton to Acton Bridge. When be had gone iinder Over Bridge, at a speed of I.", miles an hour, he now Barnes step down from his engine, :19 yards &amp;man,. Barnes. the moment lae reached the gentled, stretched his blithe as though yeeniag. At the time witness sounded the whistle. He did not me the train strike Barnes but it must have done and it was impossible to stop before reaching the man. A. verdict of Accidental death" was returned.</p>
        <p>..... ...... At Warrington Police Court on Saturday, Kate Thanell, 5. Stoney-3ard, was remanded for a week ins charge of wounding Selina Rourke, a neighbour. It is alleged that the garlic." had sante words," with the remelt that prieoner picked up knife and attempted to strike Rourke on the head. Prowcutris put up her left 11-,nl to ward off the blovr, and received a severe wound between the finger aod thumb. When charged the prisoner alleged that proaecutrix Bret struck her w:th a cap, and she put up the knife to shield ',emit. ALLEGED STABBING AFFRAY. ht the Warrington l'olice Court, on Tuesday, Joseph William, a basioetto maker, It, Dutton-street, was charged on remand uith unlawfully wounding William Forster, a clog repairer, 10, Dutoon•etreet. Chief Inspector WilliliMßOO said that about midnight on July nth, the prowicutor wept to •isit a neighbour but by mistake knocked at the prircner's door. The prieoner used La.l language. lie afterwards came out, of the house and neat towards the prosecutor who was on his one doorstep and started to pick • quarrel. A struggle ensued and the prow eutor found he had been Blabbed below the km o from which the blood flowed freely and for which he Lad to be treated •1 the Infirmary. Dr. Matson said the wound was about.. inch in leng.h end it was probable for the knife (pro- Aimed) to have ittl clod it. The prosecutor, however, woe free from all danger. . _ .</p>
        <p>Prisoner denied having uved the knife. Ile WAS lined IN. and cdata with the alternative of id days.</p>
