        <p>Borough_ Police Court:</p>
        <p>MONDAY.—Before ta Mayor (Cminc;llor Dr. Joseph), Aldermen Foulkes., Smethurst, And Tinnion, Messrs. CL Pilling, H. Thornton, W. lichens, IV. Fumes-, T. Locker and Dr. Fox. Somusit CHARS:no WITH LAIIIEY.ZI.ENI ENT.—Frvok Wood, a Dragoon board of Aldershot, was charged on remand with embezzling on or about May Ist, la fiLl. and other some amounting to 9s. Id. on divers otter d sten, the moneys of his let° employer, Alfred Hadfirth, Thelwoll, 'rho trades as the Latchford Lsundry. Mr, T. S. Steel prosecuted and Mr. W. H. J. Jenkins, who represented the prisoner, put in a plea of guilty.—Mr. Steel said that on, dier eels vanman and as such bin duties consisted of going round to the various customers to receive their clothing. When the work was done Wood hnd to return the goods and the book in which the articles were priced tip, and to receive the money I due to the firm. For the purpose of tollecting theA money he had a receipt Look. The system from thel employer's point of •iew was as complete es omissible. Wood's system of embezzlenir at appeared to have been varied. In one ome he received 4s, 111., and instead of giving the customer a proper receipt tie tore an earlier receipt from her book, altered it •nil pasted it in the book. Ile never accounted for the inoney. Inanotber cane he gave a costonier a receipt for Is. 2 i. and then en the any to the laundry altered the counterfoil by ranking it out to another person for ales. amount. Mr. Radfirth eventually became suspicious and told Wood he wished logo through the accounts with him. r'risoner went Oct on. round, but never returned. Ile, however, managed to return the horse and yen, after doing which he absconded wPh the previous day's • takings. England isms 'searched for him, and eventuslly be use found to have enlisted at Aldershot. The case was a serious one. Wood had been very cute in the way he had falsified the books and Mr. Itsdfirth hoped the Be, ch woold deal with the prisoner in such a rot poet as