        <p>. _ Councillor Jeckenn</p>
        <p>CUT A WOMAN', HEAD WITII A i UP.—Catherino Kelly, of no settled abide, was charged on remand with committing an aggravated assault upon Winifred CuDemon by striking her on the heal with. teacup on July 21th.—Chief Inspector Williamson said on the date in question the prosecutris wan passing the door of 11, Litherlancheiyard, where the prisoner ',Apr', when the prisoner rushed out, and after using bad language threw a pint cop at the prosecotrix who Led to be Dotard at the Infirmary, but wee now progreeting favourably. The prisoner had been drinking.—Pitef ner had nothing to • y, and eleven previous convictions having ben proved 'anima her, she was sentenced to toe month's hard labour. Octusno lle,, ,11 171 A T. Psnooxo. John Rimy, It, Williemson'elquare, smi charged with bring dr sok and dig. 'deity in Ctureh-atteet, with ateaulting his brother, Christopher Barry, tad with neglecting hie three children. The charge of assault was withdraan. He pleaded not guilty. Mr. A. Browne, who prosecuted on behalf of the N.s.p.c.e mid that the brimner and his wife had been living •ptrt but butt December at the wile's request they went to live together again, end sha found him work, He prornifted to amend his waso and for n time did no, but be again broke down. The crse had been tinier the eloper Onion of tie S iciety since 1901. lie had been warned by the Society's Inspector, on innumerable ocomiona. He was a good workman and the Vett:ion-street Tannery appreciated his aervlcee. The Inspectois hail to provide food for the family who were so terrified of the prisoner that they had ti run out of his way. Ti,. first wages for the past tew months he bad given to his wife was at Wallung Day when he gave her 18s. lie stayed in bed until nine and ten o'clock in the morning and was alwaya demanding money from hie wife. Inspector Preston, of Widnee, acid he visited the house and found it fairly clean. It was, however, poorly forniehei, and tiers was cnly one bed for five persons. Evidence ems also tendered by Mr. Aukland, the Relieving Officer, who stated that the prisoner waa the worse for drink nearly every day. Eight previous convictions were proved against the prisoner, four of which related to family neglect. The Bench sentenced prisoner to three m friths hard labour. WeliNKSDAV.—Before Mr, Charles White (in the chair), Alderman Smethurst, Dr. Fox, Messm.J. W. Hayward end W. T. W EARLESTOWN iturvlteu to TROUBLE.—AIIan Griffith, 21, Wargriammad, Earlestown, a butcher, wes charged with being drunk and disorderly, and with aemultlng Sydney James Colter. He pleaded guilty to the first charge, snd not guilty to the mooed—Sydney James Colter, a pawnbroker. inelegant, Winwick-street, said about 10 30 the previous day the prisoner went into the shop where he was employed, and salted to see am utle of gold watch chains. The chain. were produced and the prisoner then otated that he had mat a sovereign since he event into the ahop. He eterted to use bad language, and alit, a great deal of difficulty was removed from the shop. A few minutes afterwards the velum,.r had occasion to go outside the shop to hang up some clothes, whereupon prisoner rushed at him and struck him.— Pelsonrr stated that Colter struck him first but the willleia denied having done se—The Bench Sand the prisoner to.. and coats with the alternative of 14 days. Tilt ItSPAY.—BeforeMr..l.Ryreir Young tin the chair, Drs. Fox and Stirlen, Meagre. W. T. %I illiams and</p>
        <p>E. Woocs.-- A BRUTAL lir SIIAND.—.Ichn Barlow, carter,37,Tilley- street was cliargr d with comn itring an •ggraysted amuck upon his wife Mary Elizabeth Barlow the previous day. The oomplainant stated that on the morning of the creel on day :Me erred her husband if b‘s was going to work. Ile replied that he was not going to work to beep her and that he would give bee 3s. 6d. • week if the would Only go. She snatched a paper out of hie hand and called him abed name where- npon he bit ter hand, struck bee on the side of the head sod kicked her on to the floor. Mete nes rising to Lee feet he caught hold of her sod ttrew her into the street She fell on to her left shudder and the side of her head and she Immune unconscio.a. The police were called and the injured women was conveyed to the Infirmary. After attendance she was taken to her mother's house. In reply to the Chairman the com- plainant stated that prieouer had pryyi ugly illtreated her.—The Bench fined prisoner 20e. and costs with the alternative of a month.</p>
        <p>1' RUM S.—Before the Mayor (in the chair), Alderman Smethurst, Ides... If. Thornton, G. Pilling sod T. L.ker. _</p>
        <p>A1.1.1,01:0 Foniii.r NEccr., -r.—JoEn Hickey, 90, Hoyle-street, labourer, was °barged with neglecting bin three children during the last nix months. lie piraded 11.V..C,(11ra.tidAVald"tZa7 "p7i's'o"n'e r on norbmeehAlvf ohatdhea good situation which he threw tip for no Cause what- ever. He was now earning 24.. a week. Inspector Wninwrlght visited the house and found the children fairly well nourished and clean Lot poorly clad. Tbe house was almost de•Lid of furniture. A previous .11M1130011 was brought against the defendsnt for being an habitual drunkard but it was adjourned upon his promising to leave the drink and keep to his work, Ha .lid keep hia prornine for. lime hot again broke down. If the mu, would ostler' aite to give up the drink and</p>
        <p>go nark to work he (Mr. lifOlto) would agree to an adjournment. Ilia work was open for him if he would give that undertaking. Prisoner eater' that be was sorry for what had occurred but be would begin afresh. The caae mi. adjourned until October 17th. HABITUAL DIII,BARIL—John Emmeas, 41, Old-road, wan summoned by his wife, Ellen Ernmeas,for being an habitiml drunkard. Complainant stated that the defendant waa never sober and he hod given up Lie work for that mode. Ile had been drinking for about sin year., and they were no terrified of him that they bad to run out of the house. Ile was employed 10 a carter and was earning 71s. a week. Ho was •Iways compelling her to give him money fir drink.—Toe {tench granted a separation order with 104. a week, the complainvnt to have the custody of thy children.</p>
