      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-05_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>IRISH AFFAIRS. NOTES FOR IRISH READERS.</head>
        <p>IRISH AFFAIRS. NOTES FOR IRISH READERS.</p>
        <p>THE HUGHLIGANS AND THE NO SURRENDERS."</p>
        <p>After the thunderstorm in the political atmosphere we are now enjoying a period of calm. But so interesting is the whole situation that even in com- parative calm ths observant supporter of the Veto Bill is Lot without occasional thrills. Apart from the internecine Weil,' between " Hedgers " and " Ditchers," between the Lansdowne-116Hour section and the lialsbury-Chamberlein section, we have been favoured with some really diverting letters and articles - the now famous night when tie Peens party dished all claim to mniiners and sense of dignity or private. One of the roost impudent of these uctione is the letter of Mr. F. E. Smith which we feel suie will not soon be forgotten by the responsible leaders of his almost disintegrated party. We wonder why (tin disoledient Tory and his henchmen, who followed Lord Hugh Cecil in his now historic rowdyism in the Holum cf Commons, do not recall the amendment which this patrician Hughligan made years ago during the discussion on thorules of the House for the suppression of disorder. It wr_s then proposed that the first offender in the first instance should lie suspended for a month, then for six menthe, and for the third offence hi, suspension should c iver the remainder of the session. The great Cecil of that day was not satisfied with that. The name Lord Hugh Cecil, leader of an organinad °Beck on the righ's of free speech rind the prestige of England's First Minister, this name blue-blooded represt nt.itive of the privileged caste who were credited with a monopoly of the highest breeding pr posed that any member, guilty r f disorder should be or nt to jail I, What a pity for the Hughligans themselves that they who in the past have hailed with fiendish delight the arrest of an Irish Member should not have had a done of the medicine which their own fender prescribed. After the letter of Mr. Smith comes this purple patches of Mr. Garvin in the " Observer" - the one pen and brain in all the land that can bold mt any Lope to the the rna'conter.ta behind Lord lialsburv. But the Garvinian threats of blacklietir.g ana political ostracism are, we fear, having very little effect since the returna from Lansdowee House show a steady rise in the number of Pee• a who will accept the will :et the people by permitting the Parliament Bill to pans. It in an amusing anti not uninstructive reflec- tion to the Irish mind that the " No Surrender " men have been the viral,rs of a wholesale dm eertion by the Irish Cnionist Piers, especially cowardly when it is recalled that the most disa.drous fight of the 'rory party of recent years lion been fought in their behalf. Iniagire the feelings of the Orangemen, following the wardrum of Carson, on finding that the first to turn tail was their own Lord Londonderry ! Not less amusing to the I: ish mind is the natisfaction which S r Edward Carson pretends to find in the censure of his conduct by the Nationalist Preen of Ireland. The truth, of course, is that from the Nationalist point of view the attitude if there two patriotic lawyers Smith and Carson and the other "No surrender" men io too blatantly foolinh to succeed in its object. Nationabata wili not break their he wt.. should Sir Edward Carson's tactics welt in filling the House if Lords who will give a mlllibrity for Horne Rule, on the contrary they would mete ont to him their warmest praise. IRELAND AND THE INSURANCE BILL.</p>
        <p>...... ..... This week's debate on the Insurance Bill rather trnds to emphasise the points we put forward in this column recently on the application if that measure. So long as the Chancellor of the Exchequer col fled himself to general it les : the beneficent objects of his Bill were widely acclaimed. Now that the Bill in put forunn as a notiparty measure, "as a bu nines, proposition the derails of the scheme must be tested la, that sharper measure. Searching questions must 1,0, Put on such dominant points as the average rate of sick nes% the contributions necessary and ths Slate's share. Note on these riuestions no far as England is concerned, answers have been given whose securer); the Labour members, notably Mr. Snowden, have thought fit to impugn. Yet the e dculations underly- ing dieue results are, we ate informed, the product of three years' painstaking inquiry. Without in the least degree doubting the excellent inten ions of the big human who framed the Bill, we think it of the fi it unpottance to insist now that every factor in the calculations differ in the ease of Ireland. Beyond all cloubt the average rate if sickness is different. The figures in the Bill are based on the rate for industrial Lancashire ; they can have eta possible reference to Donegal or Waterford. The Chancellor of the Exchequer, it in true, has 14muised to recommit the Bill ehou'd a good case be made rut alter:ng the State's proportion, and for our part we do not tee how the Irish patty can do other than ask for a recommi,tal for the purp-se of altering the State's proportion in the rase of Ireland. The most fervent advocates of the Bill's application to Ireland—and thus far we are of opinion that Ireland should be included in the operation of the meastwe —admit that it wood be better to defeat it altogether than to have it thrust upon tin in anything apprcaching its preeent state. We scarcely intaginn that the Chancellor will Inc willing to force on Ireland a measure based on the study of English conditions ouly, and to be worked by English m rchinery particularly now when we are within a few months of the Horne Bole Bill. We bear it ;n mind that he is a sound Plume Ruler ; and he known the Irish Party.</p>
        <p>"on I th p aet tic feel</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-05_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>WOMAN'S DEATH.</head>
        <p>A WOMAN'S DEATH.</p>
        <p>Inquest Adjourned Pending Iniestigallons. 51r. Brighouse, county coroner, opened an inquiry at ‘);rington on ThutadaY, concerning the death of slstul White (95), v ife of Peter White, cool miner, 94, Athol-street, Newton Common, Earlestown, sho died in the Warrington Colon Hospital, on slonday evening Dona the effects of puerperil fever. The Coroner explained to the jury that he was hold- ing this inquiry in consequence of a letter he had receieed from Dr. Teylor, resident medical officer at the Workhottle hospital. Suggestions had been n.ade with regard to the midwife Witt attended Sire. White,and be thought the fullest inquiry should he made as to her conduct in the case. Ile need not point out how important it was that the midwife should take every precaution to avoid convey ing infection. No only should she she the ordinary precautions suggeated by her own commonsenre, but also all the precautions laid down in the Act of Parliantent for her guidance. Two other cases, it was stated, hod occurred, but bad fortunately recovered. If ever there was cease for iequity by a jury and himself this was one. He pro- posed to take evidence of identiflettion and then adjourn for further inquiriee to be made. Peter White, the husband, said that en Thursday, July 20th, his wife gave birth to a female chi,d. Later she became very ill, and Dr. Lawrence wee celled in, sod he ordered her removal to the Union Hospital. The midwife never said she wanted a doctor until July 24th, though he had told her to call one in if she found it necessary.. - '</p>
        <p>Witogies:i.a reply to the coroser, mitt he thought a cage of this sort wanted iaquirins into. fie would be more satiliflad.</p>
        <p>--bire..--IViaaie mother said she also wished there should be an inquiry. The /worst was adjourned mail Friday, the 11th inst.</p>
