      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-05_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>HURCII or OUR LADY, LATCHFORD.</head>
        <p>HURCII or OUR LADY, LATCHFORD.</p>
        <p>One of be moat imposing and beautiful of local churches is the Church of Our lady, Latchford. It was ofencd on Surday, October 19th, 1903, by the late Lord bishop of Shrewibury, ite eriction having been rendered necessary by the inadequacy of the oil school-chapel to cope wirh the increasing congregations. The church, • hicb ha. cant .t. 11,000 wee built to the designs if Mr. Robert Curran, CE., Warrington. It provides accommodation for fits/ people, there being a nave and two aide aisles Tire sanctuary, which is divided (run the nave by a white polished marble eV, is large and lofty and the arrangement of the high altar is most dignified and tasteful. There are also two vile chal.els, earl. lighted with a wheel window. The choir is accommodated in a gallery over the main entrance, reached by a turret atiircase. The baptistry is placed at the end of t'm sorer aisle, having run uuni anion with the presbytery by a donder. The clerestory is supported by polished matedvo granite columns, with bosses and caps of Yorkshire stone, the arches being built of red atice through two nick's at the entrance to the sanctuary, above which springs a hes•ily moulded chancel arch rising to • height it 10 feet. The lighting of the church is very satisfactory. The aisles are lighted by ten twc-light tracery I ended window., aml the clereatury by twelve three-light tracery beaded windows. Thr sanctuary has four two-light windows. There is al-o a large tracery window at the west end, immediately over the main entrance. The building has an open timbered roof, composed of pitchpine, which bans very effective appearance. The pews are of pitchpine, the floors are laid with wood Wicks, and the building is electrica ly lighted thrmghout. It is intended when means allow to cmiplete the church by the enction uf • tower tieing to the height of 170 feet.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-05_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>St. James's Church.</head>
        <p>St. James's Church.</p>
        <p>Tbe dedicaticn festival in connection with St.</p>
        <p>James's Church, Latchford, was held on Sunday, the Sunday following St. James's Day. There were good congregations. The preacher in the morning was the Vicar, the flee. 11. It. Firth. and in theevening the Rev. A. J. Bra t. A children's flower service won held in the afternoon. Tim collections were iu nil of the Sunday school fund. Presentation to the Rev. E. M. Swift. St. Jarnie Magazine states Got the following books were presented to the Rev. E. AI. Sa if t an a testimonial on his having the parish —Westc itt's "St. John,' Westcott's " !lel:reran." Mason's "Faith of the Gospel," Robineoes "Studies in the Resurrection," Robinson's " St ~dies in the P.f .410," Robinson's " Studies in the character of Christ," Gore." Roman Catholic Chahar," Liddon's " Advent and Christmes, in St. Peas," Edersheim's I.ife of Christ," Chase's C. Mirmation in the Apostolic Age,' Swete's " Ascended Christ," Wert- cott's " Gospel of the Resurrection," " The Holy Spirit in the New 'l:fitment, " " Early Chapters of Genesis." Illineworth's " Personality Homan and Divine," illingworth's"Divinelornsnence,', Church's " Gsford Aloremenr," Green's "Short History of the English People," F 's "Eternal Hope," Gore's " Tbe Body of Christ," "Constitutions and Canons of the Church of England," Bickersteth's " Letter's to a Godson." Denrrner's Everymno's History of the English Church," Trench's " Parables and Alirscles," Dean's " Reformation." This Iv an exceedingly veluable and useful collection of bookmand Mr. Swift desires on to sAy how greatly ho sppreclates the Sind:was of those who bane contributed to the presentation.</p>
