        <p>In the COlll,O a his monthly letter to hi, parishioners the Rev. 11. It. Firth, Vicar of St. Jan.', Iwtchford, alas :—Atigust Is generally s hot and trying month for those people w ho are not aide to go solo fora holiday, It is always a melancholy time for inland congrega- tion,. People [luck If to the ...Ude and there the churches are full to overflowing : forms and cliairs have to be brought in to accommodate the 1101191.0 number of worshippers, while at home things are very different. Those of on who are at home should be specially diligent in oar attendance at church to make rip for the absence of the huliday-niakere. A half empty clurch, even in August, is a dt pressing spectacle- Let tan do what we can to furnish the church with aa liirge and reverent congregations as may he possible. On July 13th the Day tchool visa visited by the Diocesan Inspector end examined in Religious knowledge. Ilia report is highly gratifyingand trachea and acholsts are, he s.s, to be congratulated on the result of the examinatitn. It was delightful to he present at the examination and to note the intelligent, ready •nnwtring of the children. Those of you who have children in attendance at St. J•nies' Day School should le—as I beliene you are—thankful for the sound religious education that thejtereceive. What in to be the future of our Church 0y Schools rern•inn to be seen ; perilous days seem to looming ahead, hut it will be a dismaying day for the religion of England if ever the time comes when definite Church teaching in no longer given in the schools. We grs very fortune in St. James Parish in having good Sand.ty Schools ea sell as Day Schools, We eight hot , that plants would be as much alive to the impottioce of the former., they ore to the latter. Why—after a holiday on Saturday should it he impossible tit send children to morning achmil on Suntliy ? We certainly have a fair number of children at tobool tm Sunitay niorning, but there are not half as many ais there ought to be. they , are allowed to get into no; ways and instead of grow- ing tip Sunday morning worshippers to develop into 'Sunday morning sleepers. It teas with the parents to prevent this.</p>
        <p>St. Paul's Parish Garden Fete. Divers methods ere iniefl for the obtaining of the mean. uleroby church work may be efficiently carried on end not the least popular is that of • garden fete, as was evidenced on Thursday afternoon when the parishioners of S. Paul's met in the beautiful grounds attached to Captain Crostleld's residence, the Tower., liewmy. St. Pwd'a haa a reputation for the amount procured at its animal tale of work and thin necessarily lovoly es a great deal of labour. Happily for the ladies this will be curtailed somewhat se the now raised will be devoted to the purchasing of certain goods which will not require. much time to be spent on them nil entirely raw material were bought. There were only tbre, stalls at the fete, ie., cake, refreshment and sweetmeat.. A fair number of people were presect at</p>
        <p>the opening ceremony performed he Mrs Itylands end amongst those present were i—the lies, Mrs and Alias Ilracecamp, Hey and Mr. J II Ilinket, Alderman, Mr. cod Miters Smethurst, Mr J II Smethurst, Mr T Locker, cod Mr .1 WWI. The 1t... IV. LnArt•ca NI it said that the idea of hold ing • garden fete ear suggested just aft, their lent sale of work. 'I he Indies telt that it would be nice to hare one to that they could get • little money locate them some stitching. They at St. Paul's were always needed—</p>
        <p>trying to raise money- sad a lot and they ender. soured to raise it as pleasantly as possible. (Meer, hear ) Ile might tay in conclusion that Cant/tin Croineld had been very kind to the parish inilSoallit on he tied not been at Ilewsey many hours before he tent hint (Mr. lirvoecemp) • letter placing the grounds at bin de [hotel in order to raise funds for the carry rig on of their work. (Applauie). In declaring the fele open Mrs. IiVLAND4 said thet the perish of Sr. Nora wav a largo one. It had a great many orgenteat ion/lime she boned that they would raise that thy • subethotial amount to nesiet their work (Applante). Lin the I roposition of Rev. J. 11. 8A,.v0, reconded by Aldermen SNII,III-It9l, • vote of thanks eon accorded to Mr. Rylands. During. theisflernoon Fred evening Min Spink's pupils gave a cieditatble display of dancing end Mr. E. A. iddop's bind ploed sppieciated selectioris in the afternoon. Tennis mid telnet mete inilulged in, and of course mu h enjoyed. The prize Kirin..rn of the melee competitioh (the rake to I.e mode entirely from Ero en and Poleon's flour) are as follow : -I, Miss think+. 2, Man S. Lawton, 3, el ok Rigby, and 4, elm. Brough.</p>
