        <p>WARRINGTON BRIDGE DELAY.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Town Council ada in a holiday mood on Tuesday conning and the luminea was disposed of In en hour. The Maya [Councillor Dr. Joseph) presided and there wren nista present :—The Deputy Mayor (Alderman Forkbasr, Aldermen A Itcrinett. John Evans, W Cierpoint, .1 Smethurst, I) Tinnion. Councillors G Archer, W II Iletlian, T Binles, T Chantler, E Conninaton, J Dickenson, J Ila Hawthorn, It Ifenahall, C Jacimon lloltuee, W II J Jenkins, T Lashford, J T Locker. Dr NlcKee. J l'entherton, II Roberta, T S Steel, 0 Toole, an i (NJ Wilson ; Maas A T I. Hallaway (deputy town clerk), .1 Fairkurat (borough treaaorm), W 8 Haddock (aris engineer), J kl Murray (director of education', Dr J C Ilibbert (medical °dicer), and J Gray (water engineer:. THE TOWN CLERICS BEREAVEMENT.</p>
        <p>The NIA VOIC began the meeting uith n sympathetic reference to the death of Mrs. I.yon Wnittle. Ile You all know V.e moon of the Tessa Clerk's absence from thin meeting, nod I feel on you will join with me in an expression of iryo psthy end regret at the owase. (liem, bear). I don't propose to tiring forward a special vote of condolence, se 1 think an expression hv me, on !ellen' of the Council, to the Town Clerk Would better meet the wishes of the relatives concerned Olean, hear.l CONGRATI:IATfONS TO Slit CILIA:Fa FENTALI • •</p>
        <p>The NI Avon then referred to the honour conferred open Sir Gilbert Groenrill by Lin Mejeaty the King. We •Il look pxin the honour,' be Raid," as one ehich has been very well merited. It is • high honour,higher than the Championallip of the H ith, and it is one that is conferred not for political services, but for personal services to the Sovereign, and on that account we can •II join in heartily c ogratolating Sir Gilbert." (Applarire). The General Purposes Committee ate recent meeting , passed a resolutibn of congratulation to Sir Gilbert reenall. When this minute was stibmitted, Alderman fIbstNETT Paid they knew the valuable service. Sir Gilbert had rendered to hi• own town, and it was a source of satiefaction to every patriotic Warrington'an that his mer•icos to his King and country Lad been fittingly recognized by the King. Alderman said Le felt that Sir Gilbert Ortenall had done probably more than any other man in England for the most important industry of the country—egriculture. IiCTFERNIAHKET-STHEET INIPROVEME:NT. . .</p>
        <p>At • meeting of the t into. and Estate Committee, it ma, r.olvecl that the Town Clerk he requested to [cake early application to the Lc"' Government Beard for the borrowing of the soma required for Butter- market-street improvement, and that the chops at klarket•gcte lately occupied by Mese,. Smith and Walker and Ittecti Cash Chemists (Lancashire Ltd be not rei.'et.</p>
        <p>-1)1:1-1 he—mol ion of Con,illor K It, seconded by Councillor JA, kSON HOLM F., the minutes were •gteed</p>
        <p>OPPOSEI lON TO SANEEV BRIDGE SCHEME. The 'town Clerk, at • meeting of the Street Impe.vm 1 merit and Lighting Committee, reported Oar the Lanes- Moire County Couccil bad applied to the Local Govern- meet Board for their sanction to the borrowing of 00l1•i0 monep required for the widi ning of Sankey Elides, and IL wee reeolved that the Town Clerk lodge an otjection to the proposed loan either with the Local 'Government Boned or •t the kcal inquiry to be held by an Inspector of the Board. Alderman BENNETT moved the confirmation of the minutes. . . .</p>
        <p>—We-fireeTv-Maroß seconded, end the motion was carried. WARRINGTON'S RIGHTS SECI .RED. .</p>
        <p>......... At a meriting of the (Ma Committee. July Ilth the Town Cletk relented that the opposition to the St Helens Corporation Hill Lad been successful and that Clause 21 bed been struck out. . . _</p>
        <p>Alderman PIEIII.OINT eapretwed satiafection that St. Helena would now for all time be debarred from invad- ing the Warrington Erna of 'Ripply.</p>
        <p>GAS COOKERS AICcPIIOARDS.</p>
        <p>The Ci to ENGINEER reported to the Gaa Committee that in many cases where slot cookers bad been tiled they sere not twed. It was decided that in all further cues bat. authorized to refuse to supply slot cookers untoil a minimum consumption of gas, to be Used by him, can paid for. Alderman EdmittoiNd remarked that lone of the cookers wenu not being used for culinary purpores, but they wore ntiliaNd as cupboards to tome eltPat. Ilditigbterd That would not pay the gas undertaking, and, therefore, the comm Edee thought it war only right that minimum soppily should be consumed by the holder, of.the cookere. That proposition mu, being considered by the gas engineer and it wee hoped that at the next meeting some lefluite arrangement would be d at.</p>
        <p>• --A CONTRACTS QUESTION. LOCAL FIRMS PASSED OVER. A QUESTION OF PRINCIPLE. . . .</p>
        <p>The Gas Committee had resolved that the tender of tits Carron Iron Company for 100 cookers be accepted. Aldermen Pi NT, who moved the confirmation of the minutes, mid there were too important gasi</p>
        <p>engineering firms in Warrington, end he yersonally regretted that the convnitte• felt obliged to path them ones, dieing the order to an outside are. Ile hoped the decision might hive the effect—he did not want to say anything offensive—of bringing the two local time to a more sensible consideration of the VILMA of the Cor- poration. At remelt they seemed to be forming • ring.- Ile had seen the cooker made ho the Carron company end it appeared to be admirable in every respect. ii. price was considerably lower than that of tie Warrington firms. Alderman Jona Li (Ns seconded the stoproval of the minutes._</p>
        <p>councillor 3t-as. stated that there was • consider- able feeling that the tender ehmild not 1m allowed to go outside the town. 111 n alderman had indicated that it sex a matter which might he reconsidered. and he moved we an amendment that the resolution be referred beck for further consideraticn. fir. M NI. seconded.</p>
        <p>Councillor BELLIIN ael,ed ahat the difference ia plice betaten the accepted tender and that of the local firms sae ?</p>
        <p>Alderman I'll:m.(1NT: Ten per cent. Councillor PFLLIAN : Of count., that is • very serloner amount.</p>
        <p>Councillor JENKINS observed that there were two firms who paid en lIIIMPOI`e stnn In rater arid COOttl- buted in other ways to the welfare of the borough. He asked whether, even with n,•rgin of ten per cent., it on. not worth their while to sire local firms every consideration. Even if the contractors mnde a little greater profit, would the Council not benefit in the long run by studying home? Coukcillor Prkrunitrox, whilst nupportiug the emend:nen., said he could not @unreal, the sentiment@ expressed by Councillor Jenkine. The members of the Council had their duty to perform, not only to one tection, but to the whole of the ratepayer. It would be wise if the chairman of thalami Committee withdrew the minute for farther oliacumir n. It was deplorable if they could not give the order in town and if it were ultimately found rrnpossible to keep the work In War- rington he should be very sorry. Warrington had two of the moat emlnekt firms of gas engiore.s in the country hut at the name time It behoved the (lea Com• mitt.. to act in the interfere of the ratepayers in gereral. " "-</p>
        <p>Alderoiu TINNION emphssized the f..ct that the committee had done everything that wits possible, and probably more than 1,111 degiroble, to keep the order it. the town, but without start. If the •mendment was pressed it meant paying so much more in order to Isisve thu. work done lo Warrington. When people were in,ed to rend in tendern it was only right they ehottld all teceit e rural coooideretion. It local firma were to receive the contracts, no matter how high their pricee might he, it would destroy just competition. No one In the Council would be more pleased thon he if they could give tho contract ton Werritigton liras, hut under the circurnstrinces, it was impossible to do so on fair and reastmalde term.. . . .</p>
        <p>Couocillor !tonna', naked why the minute should be sent buck? An extremely important principle, he j added, wan at stake in the proceeding. before the J Connell. Tenders bad been esked for in the open market from anybody who chose tojcompete. It wee curt 'tidy to the advantage of IVarrington people that. there ahould Ice the fullest end fairest competition in regard to ell requirement. in erery department. 11th. minute were lent back the Gs. ('ommittee were bound to return it unless they ehdinated their functions eltogetber,end he did not think they were prepared to do !h.q. (Iteer, Leer.) In the present ism/ince. tender win selected on the result of compectitioo—the lowest tender. Ile did not care whether there ween difference of 10 per cent, or of only • fraction of one per estoL— they invited competition and they ought to accept the los est bender, the other things being equal. I" ques- tion.") Ile supper...l the Ca. Committee could he credited with having some knowledge.. to whether the tender wasn fair one, :taking into consideration the difference In price. lie preeurned the tender on recommended because under it they got full value end a saving of ten per cent. Nobody would stand op more strongly than he for (preference, on 1111011 tem., for their own town•people. But it most he on eqoal term.. It mutt not toe on term• favourable to townspeople and unfevourable to outsiders. It was certainly most *ma to give preference to soy firm at the coat ot the owe' body of ratepayer.. Ile did not thltds 'tie eminent Warrington lirros in question would thank the Corporation to go out of its way to give them preference over their competitor.. their trade was j not • local nue. 1 heir reputation extended throognotet j the. d'lngdom, ocher td elbrit...ed,zdultdh Obey Ined.x to tnnielett,.t.c:Lrn: Cr."1"111‘," for inetta's.c•,. to recelveyprare'rence at tilts hone, of the nuthecritic •of that city ? It would mc. an extremely dangerous thing to depart from the policy nhiolc hel hitherto charecterieed the conduct of the Warrington Corporntion with regard to tenders. They were bound to accept the tender of t heCorron Company. It had I4en properly asked for and received and, on its mert.s., it should he eccepted. . . . . . . . . _ .</p>
        <p>711d-e'r.rh;;;;lir.t:h ili-v-rde-clared that be nri intended Ito support the amendment, but after the explanstion of Alderman Tionion be should vote for the minute. The local firma had riceived all all the consideration they could expect. I Alderman PIISRPOINT Ita• very plod at the tone of the discussion and hoped that Councillor Jerthins would near his way to withdraw the amendment. It was with regret that the committee took the step recommended, I but perheps good would be done for the future. I Councillor JENE I, said that alter the very foil, careful awl busier-as-like explanations he regarded it as his duty toe ithdraw the amendment. This course was mimed to by Dr. McKee, and the minute was then cotllrmed. 13IPIZOVING THE WATER SUPPLY.</p>
        <p>At meeting of the Water Committee, the Engineer ',Omitted apedilenfion fnr anew engine at Ilmighten Green pimping mei low It ens 'milled tiint the &amp;IQ • Pt. referred to 51r. Michael Longridge for bin opinion Llerror. . . . . .</p>
        <p>Mr. JOIN Fit In moving the adoption of the minutes, on tt.at if the engine at Houghton Green were to fail there would not be !sufficient pumping power at Winivick to keep the town etipplied with water. *Hie Committee therefore coniered it their duty to provide anothir engine. The Heat move waa to instruct the engineer to prepare a specification. It hal been prepared nod was in the bandy of Mr. Longridge for hi. inspection and report. The layieg of the new Hi Inch man wee proceeding aliafnet,,ilr, and the work had now got tut for as Croalleld *Heti. There were lireakers ahead, owing to the nature of the streets they Lad to pass through. It would be all plain sailing up to Ow and of Legh•street, and then the ntreet became no contracted that tin traffic would be seriously leiter- fered with. Ihe corn-mates had, however, deckled t.. Intl the contractor that he must provide two seta of wee and work bight and day to get forward. The inconvenience to the general public, nnd the shop keepots In particular, could nut be avoided, ati far as they could nee at present. Councillor ltocec to seconded.</p>
