      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-05_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>ROYAL VISIT TO H.M.S. ' HINDUSTAN."</head>
        <p>ROYAL VISIT TO H.M.S. ' HINDUSTAN."</p>
        <p>The Prince of Wales, who is sevente sn years aI, started his naval career this week as • midshipman on board the "Hindustan." As a midshipman, the Prince will gain his first experience in the handling of men, and will attend the usual classes for instruction in the complicated science of m Mern war, and his life on the " Hindustan" will be of the strenuous type which is the lot of young officers in the British Navy. The " Hindustan" Is not one of our most recent ships, but one of the most powerful of the pre-Dreadnoughts ever built. Liunched in 1903, and completed in 1905 she displaces 16,350 tons with a speed of 19 knots. She is commanded by Captain Henry H. Campbell, M.V.0., A.D.C., and hai been six years at sea. The King ant Queen, with Princms Mary and the Prince of Wales, paid a visit to th: s'sM Os 14311137. Oar picture shows the Royal visitors leaving.</p>
        <p>The Dowager Grand Duchess of Mecklen- burg-Strelitz in spite of her eighty-nine years, continues to enjoy good-health. She is the last of the Gaorgian Princesses, and is a granichild of George 111. On her marriage in 1813, an annuity of £3,000 was voted ; she has, therefore drawn °afar 6200,000 from the English Treasury. The Grand Duke died six years ago. Queen Mary, The Prince is, Wales, and Princess Mary are to pay lb' Grand Duchess a visit shortly.</p>
