      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-05_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>LATE DEAN GREGORY.</head>
        <p>THE LATE DEAN GREGORY.</p>
        <p>The Very Rev. Robert Gregory, D.C., the venerable Dean of St. Paul's, who died on Wednesday at the Deanery in his ninety-third year. Ho went to the great Cathedral in 1868 as a Canon, and became Dean in 1891.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-05_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <p>Child Burned to Death. screams and went to Ruby's assistance, trying to extinguish the flames with her hands until Mrs. 13arton An inquest m's held ut the Borough Statim arrived. her little girl, aged four yearn, who was in on Monday by Mr. S. lb ighonee resprering the death the field playiug, said Ruby had a match.- The jury of Ruby Lea, aged five pars, dattg.ter ol Frank Lea, returned a verdict of " Accidental death." a tailor. The child lived with iLel grandmother, Mrs. • Dutton, 302, Lovely-lane, who stated that at three o'clock on Thursday afternoon she wan inf rmed that Ruby had been burned whilet play iug in a field at the rear of Lonely-lane She Lad the ebild taken to the house, where it vas Ken by Dr. Ned( n, who ordered her removal to the le.firmary, where elm died on Saturday evening. She beard tlmt the grass became o sited while the little one wan playing in the field.— Barton, 281, Lowely-lene, raid she was at her back door on Thursday afternoon, when she heard nereenis, and then she ease Ruby standing in the field in Barnes. Obtaining a rug she ran to the little one and extinguished the throws. Then she carried the girl home. 1 here were three other small children in the field. She retired that the grass had been on fire. —M no. Blade'', of Lovely-lane, raid the also heard</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-05_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>TRUTH ABOUT GERMANY,</head>
        <p>THE TRUTH ABOUT GERMANY,</p>
        <p>Marlon e. the German empire was established the development of railways was undertaken on a great male. From 1695 to 1865 only 8,887 English miles had been constructed. In ten years afterwards the system tvas:doubled, the mileage standing at 17,592. By 1900 it was railed by 30,591 mike, Co hooting the British system in extent. The same spirit of home enterprise has been turned on to roads, canals, postal facilities, industrial developments, ecientific and technical teach- ing and higher education. Germany has, in feet, earned or present great position by hi nest, peaceful work. tier diplomatic triumph. under Bismarck and remark- able warlike victories under kloltke, were but incidents in a because interval strength had been developed by industry peace.—Edwerd Owen Greening in "Agricultural j Economist sad Hottioultorel Review."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-05_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>PILGRIMAGES</head>
        <p>PILGRIMAGES</p>
        <p>IN SEARCH OF DICK WHITTIhGTON. THE HOME OF THE FAAOUS</p>
        <p>LORD MAYOR.</p>
        <p>The hero of a thousand pantomimes was the hero of our pilg image. We were to track to his original lair the cat-loving and lucky Lord Mayor of London, known to history and to fairy tale as Dick Whittington. Those who are not interested in Dick have surely something wrong with their constitu- tions. He touches us at some point most arrestingly. If we are not ford of Lord Mayors we are fond of cats; and marrying beautiful girls with lots of money and making fottunes out of a gamblii g chance, with success and immortal fame thrown in, are things that no romantic heart can resist. No wonder Dick Whittington still holds the boards at Pantomimes. DICK WHITTINGTON'S SHROPSHIRE HOME. But how many who know Dick of the cat, parcel and milestone, have ever sought out the pretty village that rear( d him. It is "Dick of Whittington" who is the hero of the romance, and the Whittington that gave him name still rests in unspoiled sweetness in one of the quiet spaces of Shopshire. The cyclist will encounter it upon the Whitchurch ar.d Oswestry road, and will hasten to declare it one of the very prettiest of England's villages. On one side of the lane the massive towers of a Norman Castle rise from the placid waters of an ancient moat ; on the other a straggling row of typical village buildings stand in pictur- esque array. The village Inn, rejoicing in its title of "The Boot," and the village smithy stand sleepy and half dead, and the village church, ruined by alterations, stands amid a grove of beautiful cedars. It would not be greatlf differents five and a half centuries ago when Dick Whittington lived amid its stillness and dreamed dreams of the bustling oisiness of London. How do we know that it was from this •ountry lane that Dick went forth to make nimself and the village famous?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-05_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>WHITTINGTON CASTLE.</head>
        <p>WHITTINGTON CASTLE.</p>
        <p>rhere are two bits of evidence that seem quite good enough. For the first we look across the quiet wayside water to the stolid towers of Whittington Castle. Seven centuries ago at the bidding of Norman Barons those round towers rose in grim defiance. The castle was an outpost to push the Welsh still farther back, and mark the boundary of what the Norman had claimed and meant to keep. It was the lordly home of the once-great family of Fitz Waring and has witnessed scenes and consultations that have borne terrible fruit in our rough island story. One of the Waring was reared with Prince John and broke his head with a chess board. The same tough member of the family was afterwards one who stood over him and made him sign the Magna Charta. Some of the Warins turned outlaw and made the Castle their headquarters. One of them joined Llewelyn and fought against the English, but the grimmest tragedy of all that were hatched behind these walls was that by which the Princes of Wales were destroyed. The two Princes Llewleyn and Griffith, heirs to the Welsh kingdom, were placed in the guardianship of one of the Waring and Mortimer. They took the lads one fair day upon the River Dee dropped them into a hole there and left the waters to dispose of their corpses. These kindly guardians then took possession of their estates and gave the King of England a good slice to keep him quiet. Such were the human stock who lived</p>
        <p>here and from whom our noble houses delight to trace their apcestry. And the birds sang for them and the waters sparkled as they do for us to-day. But this is only introductory to the fact that it was in the house of Hugh Fitz Warren a well-to-do merchant in London that Dick found his shelter. The probability is that he was a natural son of some of the Castle folk and was sent to Hugh Fitz in London, but this is a point that is likely to remain unsettled. Arrivitg in the city plain Dick would become Dick of Whittington and as surnames were at that period coming into common use would soon be Dick Whittington. Yet another bit of evidence. In 1361, when Dick was setting out to seek his fortune, Roger de Hampton was Vicar of Ellesmere near by and gave a letter of recommendation to the Prior of St. Batholemew's, London. Thus far the picture is that of a village lad travelling to Londan armed with a reference from the vicar and probably from the Castle. The established fact is that 133 lived with a merchant prince Hugh Fitz Warren and legend fills in the rest with a loving hand.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-05_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>FINE OLD LEGEND OF DICK,</head>
        <p>THE FINE OLD LEGEND OF DICK,</p>
        <p>And now we will leave the realm of stern fact and strained conjecture, and trust</p>
        <p>ourselves to the pamphleteers' who earliest told the story. Dick expected to find the streets of London paved with gold, but they turned out to be of we tched cobble stones that hurt his feet. He expected to he well received, but was put with the cook in the kitchen. His first order was "Clean the spit and dripping-pans, make the fire aged wind the jack, or I'll break your head with a ladle and make you a football "—which completely shook his faith in London and London manners. He was the scullion and drudge, and no one was kind to him except Mistress Alice, the master's daughter, who gave him dainties and clothes.</p>
        <p>But the dawn of day soon came. A mercLant gave Dick a penny for cleaning his shoes and after deliberation he invested it. ' The gloomy garret in which he slept was swarming with rats. If he could have a cat it would not only keep the rats away but be congenial company. A woman who had a good mousing cat offered it for sixpence. The future merchant prince bought it for a penny. It was just like a woman to value a cat at a fabulous sum and it was worthy of the future stock exchange gambler to get it for a penny. The cat did well, but everything else did badly. The cook went worse and worse and when Dick could endure no longer h, took his cat and parcel and ran away. But it was All Hallows Day and as Dick sat on the mill-stone the bells were calling the people to church. Bow bells rang out in the steeple and the lonely lad heard them say: Turn again Wbit-ting-ton Lord Mayor of London.</p>
        <p>fie lifted the parcel and returned, taking risks as to whether he would get a welcome or a wigging. DICK'S VENTURE.</p>
        <p>. -..._..... Dick's lucky star must have been high in the heavens. He got back to learn that his master was' venturing a ship" as they then called it to some remote foreign port. It was the custom for everybody belonging to the household or office to "venture" some- thing in these risky voyages, It gave the</p>
        <p>servants an interest, it ensured their prayers, and it often brought them a good return. When Dick was told he would have to risk something he fell on his knees and begged them not to laugh at him. He had nothing to offer but the cat. But the captain was jovial and said "The very thing—if it is a good ratter." Bo Dick handed over the cat, possibly with a sigh and a tear. . . .</p>
        <p>As everybody knows the ship got wrecked somewhere in Barbary at a place where they used gold for raving stones and jewels to play marbles with. In fact they seemed to have everything but cats, and when Dick's favourite was taken ashore and made a raid on the Sultan's rats, she was promptly bought for a bucketful of jewels.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-05_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>THEN THE LOVE STORY,</head>
        <p>THEN THE LOVE STORY,</p>
        <p>These jewels, of course, came back for Dick. But the first man to see them was not Dick, but Dick's Master. Where- upon he became unaccountably sentimental; wondered what would become of Alice when he was dead and sent for Alice to tell her how anxious he was to see her settled._</p>
        <p>Alice was old enough to take motherly oVersight of Dick from the first, and was therefore no chicken. Even if she had been the jewels would have convinced her that Dick was a good lad, and so the old man was able to arrange a very successful situa- tion.</p>
        <p>--Dick was summoned and Alice was to present to him the casket. On hearing the news he fell on his knees and bwed Mistress Alice to keep them for herself. She said she couldn't really, or something to that effect, and when they had said a few such things the father suggested that if they got married they could both own them and be happy for ever. So the wedding was ordered and Dick was taken into the business as a partner. Once he got on the exchange he showed his quality. He became a Mercer and a Merchants adventurer, and made piles of money by equipping the Army for the French war. The lucky star keeping well up he got on the City Council, and was three times Lord Mayor of London. Ho made lots of money but money making did not harden him, and there was always a tender spot in his heart. He had no children to leave his wealth to and his thoughts turned towards doing kindness to others. He wrote " A devout man should make dedys of mercie and pite and provide for the miserable persons whom the penury of poverty insulted." So he built almshouses, churches and hospitals. Then he built a prison (also out of " mercie and pits" we presume) and when he died they made ballads and pretty songs about him. The children took him over as a here and have kept his memory green ever since.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-05_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>TRUE OF -COURSE!</head>
        <p>TRUE OF -COURSE!</p>
        <p>How much of the old tale is true? If the ' atrict historical temper prevailed we should put it through the sieve and see how much was left. But the hot day is fatal to the historical temper. The swans on the moat will eat biscuits and the towers look as sleepy as they did when Dick cam, here to sail his boats. There is nothing in the village of Whittington to raise any doubts. The tale is good enough anyhow, and if it weren't for that unlikely detail about the Sultan and the peck of jewels it might be swallowed whole.</p>
        <p>In any case let the cold historian walk • • . .</p>
        <p>very warily. Crazy logic may say what it likes, but those bells did say "Turn again Whittington," Of course ther did and they sang Dick a fine secret, too. The human soul is too great to be crushed. It may turn craven and rebel. But it is made to be victor. _ .</p>
        <p>Let it try to run away and the bells in the steeple say "Turn again Whittington." lhat is why the old tale has lived ss long, and that is why it will live for ever. A.M.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-05_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>CHILDREN'S CORNER, Conducted by UNCLE CALEB. ....-,•.—k.n..--N.—,vw-,,,---v-v-,v-v</head>
        <p>CHILDREN'S CORNER, Conducted by UNCLE CALEB. ....-,•.—k.n..--N.—,vw-,,,---v-v-,v-v</p>
        <p>AN EGGSHELL OSTRICH.</p>
        <p>One eggshell, three twisted paper lighters, and a small piece of fringed paper go to the making of one ostrich. You will need a whole eggshell for the body and must empty it by making a small hole in each end and blowing out the egg. Bore the holes with a large darning— needle or hatpin, pressing steadily but not too hard and twisting the point round and round until small hole has been punctured; then enlarge the hole slightly with the sharp point of your scissors, being careful not to crack the shell in doing so. Make the hole in the large end of the shell a trifle larger than the one in the small end. Hold the egg over a bowl, put the small end to your lips and blow steadily until all the egg has run out of the shell. THIS WEEK'S STORY. THE BOY'S SACRIFICE.</p>
        <p>He was a good little boy and very thought- ful. He had heard about the great scarcity of water throughout the country. He came to his mother and slipped his hand into hers. "Mamma," he aid, "is it true that in some places the little girls and boys have scarcely enough water to drink?"</p>
        <p>"That is what the papers say, my dear." "Mamma," be presently said, "I'd like to give up somethin' for those poor little boys and girls." His mother gave him a 'fond look. " Yes, dear. And what would you like to give up?" "Mamma," he said in an earnest voice, "as long as the water is so very scarce I think I ought to give up bein' washed."—</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-05_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>LEGEND OF THE PANSY</head>
        <p>THE LEGEND OF THE PANSY</p>
        <p>Lek week I ;Old you of some fancifol ideas about the sweet yea. It will be appropriate if I mention this week some fond of things about the pansy. In France they call it the Stepmother Flower, and the ' children show each other all the curious things that are to be seen under it. The flower has five petals and five sepals. In most pansies, especially of the earlier and less highly developed varieties, two of the petals are plain in colour and three are gay. The two plain petals have a single sepal, two of the gay petals have a sepal each, and the thfrd, which is the largest of all, has two sepal.. The fable is that the pansy represents a family consisting of husband, wife and four daughters, two of the latter being stepchildren of the te ife. The plain petals ire the stepchildren with only one chair, the two small gay petals are the daughters with a chair each, and the large gay petal is the wife with two chairs. To find the father one most strip away the petals until the stamens and pistils are bare. They have a fanciful rcsemblance to an old man, with • flannel wrap around his neck, his shoulders upraised and his feet in a bath tub. Why entne of the children should be stepchildren I have not discovered, bEll no doubt there is some imaginative legend behind it which I have not heard. I am told that the saute legend is common in Germany, but I have never heard it in England.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-05_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>SOME GOOD THINGS.</head>
        <p>SOME GOOD THINGS.</p>
        <p>It's go d,Loys,—t—o—b; p-OP-uisTr tq he true! It's good to honor other,; better tbst they honor you! It's good to do things easily ; it's better not to shirk ! It's good to have some money; it's better far to work ! It's good not to be tempted ; it's better to be strong It's good to know no evil ; better to do no smug ! It's good something to have to spend ; it's better, boys, to save ! It's good to have no trouble ; ita better to be brave ! How many things are good, my boys But I rather think don't you? We'd better find the better, end the better things then do!</p>
        <p>-lEDELDEBT F. CALDWELL.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-05_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>MeV., FROM LABOUR'S STANDPOINT.</head>
        <p>MeV., FROM LABOUR'S STANDPOINT.</p>
        <p>By Philip Snowden, II P. SOME GOOD POINTS IN THE INSURANCE BILL</p>
        <p>The Insurance Bill is being assailed with so much severe criticism from so many quarters and from so many points of view that there is a danger of its good points being forgotten or obscured. It is of course the fate of every measure which is discussed to have its shortcomings magnified or empha- sised, and its good points put in the back- ground. Nobody, quite naturally, troubles to say anything about the things which they approve, except the Ministers in charge of the Bill. As has been pointed out in this column before, the ultimate fate of this Bill will be decided by the decision on the points as to whether on the whole the good in the Bill outweighs the bad, and in coming to a decision on that matter the good points should not be forgotten. The first thing that can be said in favour of the Bill is that it puts upon the State certain responsibilities whieh have not been accepted by it hitherto. The Bill organises the treatment of sickness, and to some extent the medical profession also. It does not, it is true, go so far as to say that the provision of medical treatment and the supply of what- ever may be necessary for the welfare of the sick or disabled parson shall be provided by tho State. In its small and tentative beginning it follows much on the lines of other State departures which have ulti- mately grown to be very extensive concerns. When the Education Act of 1870 was passed , nobody ever suspected that it was the first step to an educational system where the , whole cost would be met from public funds. The remark of Mr. Foster that he did not think the School Board rate would ever rise , to more than 3d. in the pound just shows to what extent the State at that time was pre- pared to accept the responsibility for national education. At that time it was generally , thought that it was the business of voluntary , organisations and of the parents to meet the • cost of education, with a little he! 1 from the , State where private effort might fail.</p>
        <p>A WARNING FROM HISTORY. In dealing with the Education Bill in his Life of Gladstone,". Lord Morley points out . . .</p>
        <p>that when a subject is legislated upon for the first time A does not matter a great deal whether it is treated very completely or not;</p>
        <p>the important thing to secure is that the basic principal of the Bill is sound and that it is so constructed in detail that it will be possible to recast the structure and add to it without making the foundations insecure. The trouble from which we have never been free in England over the Education question since that Bill was passed is due, Lord Morley asserts, to the fact that the National Educa- tion system was not hid on firm foundations in 1870. But forty years of agitation have succeeded in reconstructing our educational system until it is to-day amost a complete State system. The Insurance _Bill is very unsatisfactory</p>
        <p>from the point of view of tllOBO who would like to see the State accepting a much larger share of the responsibility for making public provision for sickness and disablement, and , it is for them to make up their minds as to whether the present scheme is one which can be enlarged and expanded into something nearer their ideal. It is something to have had the responsibility of the State for personal sickness recognised at all. Two other admirable things are embodied ' ' "</p>
        <p>in Ifive- .1-3711-namely, the organisation of the treatment of consumption, and the maternity benefit for mothers. In both these matters the beginning is in a very small way, and it may be argued with some force that in the matter of consumption it would be better if the money to be spent di sanatoria were devoted to dealing with the causes of the disease, which by universal consent are mainly poverty, poor food, and bad housing. The maternity benefit is one of the best things in the Bill. This is not a palliative; it is preventive. It recognises the fact that the State has an interest in the welfare of the new-born child, and that the mother is doing the State a service by giving it to the nation. The grant is small, and it may be said inadequate; but it is a beginning, and whatever may he the fate of the Bill as a whole it is sincerely to be hoped that this , feature of it will be saved. Medical opinion is not united on the value of sanatoria, but if they are useful for curing consumption, then . that part of the Bill ought also to be saved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-05_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>AN "ARISTOCRAT'S BILL."</head>
        <p>AN "ARISTOCRAT'S BILL."</p>
        <p>The Bill has been criticised on the ground that it is an "Aristocrat's Bill." The mean- ing of this is that while it undoubtedly gives substantial benefits to the well-to-do artisan who has been able to insure himself in a good solvent Friendly Society it did very little, if anything, for the very poor. It is important to note that a condition of the , substantial benefits is that the society is solvent. That, unfortunately, is not a condi- tion which many societies can fulfil. According to the latest Returns made to the Chief Registrar, of the 472 societies making valuation returns, 317 were deficient to the extent of £620,803, and 155 only showed surpluses which in the aggregate amounted to £182,981. . . . . .</p>
        <p>But a member of a good solvent society which becomes an approved society, will be put in a better position if this Bill passes. He will pay 4d. a week less to his society, the 4d. which he will have deducted from his wages being substituted for the 4d. he has hitherto paid to his society. For that 4d. he will be entitled to sick pay, medical treatment, maternity benefit, and sanatorium treatment. The balance of the contribution be is now paying to his society will be available for additional benefits, that is for increasing his sick pay, or it may be used to reduce the 4d. a week he has to pay to the State scheme. A man of 44 years of age who joined a first-class society at 16, and who has been paying 2s. a month, will have a reserve of Is. Bd. a month available for benefits, and a reference to the tables of his society will show him what Is. Bd. a month will give for a man 44 years of age. The contributions by the employers and the State enable all the insured persons who come into the scheme to be treated as if they were entering at 16 years of age. THE MINORITY MAN.</p>
        <p>For the fortunate man who has a reserve credit in his society this Bill is going to be a very good thing, but unfortunately that man I am afraid, will be the minority man. In this case Mr. Birrell's adage will not apply. It will be the majority which will suffer. The Bill, too, will give benefits to the healthy men between 25 and 50, who are not now</p>
        <p>members of any friendly society, larger than they could get by joining a society at that 'age. Take the Manchester Unity scale 4, Ordinary Occupations ; benefits 10e. a week in sickness for six months and ss. per week thereafter. These benefits are higher than the scale in the Bill. But a man col4o entering</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-05_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <p>the Unity now would pay for sickness and • doctor 2s. 814. a month. His contribution lunder the State scheme for about the same benefits (reckoning the maternity and sanatorium benefits) will be 4d. per week, or Is. 4d. a month. The reason the State scheme gives this better benefit is because of the subsidy by the State and the contribution of the employer. What really happens is that under the State scheme the amount paid altogether for this man's insurance is about 38. a month, but as the State has taken over a paper liability, said to amount to about £60,000,000, it is able to take in all persons up to about 65 years of age. For two classes of persons the Bill will be an advantage, namely the members of good societies which become approved" and the well-to-do artisan over 40 who can afford to pay the 4d. a week without any strain; bu if the benefits be confined mainly to this small minority it can hardly claim to be a National Insurance Bill. A National Scheme should help those who are not already pro- vided for owing to their poverty. The criticism of the Bill is almost wholly directed to that shortcoming. It is felt that the Bill compels the poor and needy to help them- selves by sacrifices they cannot afford.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-05_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
