      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-05_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>ENQUIRY INTO DOAN'S.</head>
        <p>THE ENQUIRY INTO DOAN'S.</p>
        <p>Warrington Resident Gives Evidence.</p>
        <p>The farther the enquiry into Doan's reports Is pursued, the more gratifying the e•iderace becomes. Local men end women who, in gratitude, spoke out unreservedly years ago, telling of their cures, now stand by nil they /laid, and, better still, unhesitatingly declare their curer; to be lasting. Mrs. 11. Daley, of I, Booth Street, Liverpool Road, Warrington, says :—" My kidney trouble started with severe pain. in my back, which were so bad that I was obliged to walk about with my back bent. After • time dropsy net in. My body began to swell all over. The legs swelled the most, but there were also swellings under my eye.. "1 bed bad headaches, end was subject to nasty diary bouts which made me feel as dazed that I could hardly stand. The cruel _pains in my back and loins seemed to wear me out. I got Into a nervous, &amp;premed eon- chtion, and quite lost my appetite. " I had been a martyr to these troubles for some Tears before I tried Donor ba-kache kidney pills; but had not taken many doe's of the medicine before I could tell that it wee doing m• good. The swellings gradually grew less, nodal last disappeared altogether; and my back became easier a. I kept on with the pills. "By the time I tad used three bourn I ase quite cured. I don't know u. but I should have done without Doen's pill., for I had tried lota of things before I used theru without Boding any ease. I shall always be ready to recommend theta. (Signed) Hannah Daley." Over .3 years later, Mrs. Daley said It is some years since Dorm's pills cored me, and I ern still well. I have not had any return of the old trouble beyond a t•iage of backache now •nd scab) ; sod then I have taken • dose of Doan's pills, which Imes always removed the pain." 2/9 • hoe, 6 boxes 13 .1; of all dealers. Ask for DOAN'S backache kidney pills, like Mrs. Daley had.</p>
        <p>Independent Order of Rechabltes. The members of "John Dimor.d" Juvenile Tent held their annual picnic on Saturday, when abait 280 members and friends left Bank Quay Station by special train for Frodeharn. The weather was racer tionally fine and a co,t enjoyable time waa spent in the sicinity of Mersey View. After tea programme of sports area arranged for the member., the folio. ing competitors being Nucceeaful in carrying off the prizes; —Girls rum .1 to 8 years A Hardman, l' Lowe, M A Barton; girls from 8 to II years : E Hardman, E Clare, S Barnes ; gi,l. from 11 to It years : L Wilkinson, 11 Ellis, IA Barnes. Boys front la to It rear. E Spruce, J Hardman , A Lightfoot ; boys -from R toll year.. H Jolley, H Spruce, T Holford ; boys from 11 to 11 years : T Littlewood, 11 Bollard E Wright. Bro. Wm Lowe (superintendent) pre- sented the p. ise sto the winners The return journey wa• made about 8 311 pm, Warrington being reached ab,ut 8 15.</p>
