      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-05_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>OUR SERIAL STORY.</head>
        <p>OUR SERIAL STORY.</p>
        <p>[A Lt. RIGHTS RRAISRVEDI.</p>
        <p>Jane Halliday might best be described as peculiar. She wan a woman of uncertain age. She neither affected youth nor advancing years. She dressed well, Nit quietly. Her one weakness wt. her Nail hair, which she 'kept cut short, And which invariably got out of curl in the slightest damp. It was a damp day now, and Lady Jane's short locks were in unbecoming disorder. She was an enor- mously rich woman, unmarried, and with • passion for patronising all young girls who came her way. She had met Rebecca neve- -- ”orrimer house, and had agreed with this lady in considering old Hen- shall's daughter attractive, ladylike, and sufficiently good-looking to take the world by storm.</p>
        <p>Now the morning papers had brought Lady Jane the information that Miss Henshall woe rich ; that, in short, she must be classed amongst the heiresses who were considered so desirable by young men and anxious mothers, and whose money lends them such a _gilded_ magnificence in. London society.</p>
        <p>Lady Jane Halliday lived in an enormous . house Portland.place. She lived there quite alone with a staff of servants, went a great deal into society, took whinm of liking people immensely or hating them profoundly, went her own sweet way without let or hindrance, for was she not the daughter of an earl, and richer than almost any other unmarried woman in London. On no account would she take to herself a husband, although she had innumerable offers. When Rebecca stood now on the thres- hold of the dining-room, her natural paleness accentuated by the depth of her mourning, her sweet bright eyes corresponding for the first time to the sad IMes of her mouth, Lady Jane felt more drawn by her charms than ever.</p>
        <p>"My dear," said Mrs. Lorrimer, "it is good of you to ee II". I said to Jane only this morning that you would be sure to re- fuse us admittance, but Jane never will take</p>
        <p>•no' if she can help it." "How do you do, my dear?" eaid Lady Jane, bestowing a kiss upon Rebecca. "You have doubtless had scores of letters with the usual condolences. You wool hear a word of condolence either from Cecilia Lorrimer ar myself. We have come on quite another matter." .</p>
        <p>"Yes," said Mrs. Lorrimer, "quite another matter."</p>
        <p>"•Sit down, my dear, do," said Lady Jane, "for if you don't Cecilia and I will have to remain standing. The fact is, Between, we want to know the inns and outs of a very strange story." Rebecca, who had scarcely spoken during this rush of words, now pointed her friends to two chairs, poked the fire, and seated her- self in such a position that she faced the full light which was admitted through the win- dows.</p>
        <p>--Lady Jane fidgeted. She was the sort of woman who could never keep still for a minute. She patted her disorderly locks and tried to push some of the most rebellious from her eyes. "Is it true," said Lady Jane, "that you, my dear Rebecca, are wearing about your person—yes, about your person— a diamond of such immense value that it is worth a king's ransom? Tell on the truth my dear child? You won't, I am certain, tell o lie in this matter. Say yes or no, and at once. You remember quiie well, Rebecca, that if I have a passion on this earth it is for unique and valuable gems. "You have shown me your own lovely dia- monds," said Rebecca, speaking in the calmest of voices, "and I cannot forget them. Who told you that I owned, or that I wear about my peraion.a valuable diamond?" _</p>
        <p>"Oh, my dear." said Mrs. Lorrimer, "von needn't look so excited and at the same time no determined to conceal everything. The cat iv out of the bag, my dear Rebecca. The knowledge came to Lady Jane and Me in the most simple manner. Jane had a visit this very morning from no less a person than your late friend and companion, Misi Mary Gray." Rebecca could not help starting. "She called to see Jane to ask her if she re- quired a lady to assist her in entertaining her guests, and offered herself for the post. Her idea was to go to Lady Jane on each of her "at home" (Ws to pour out tea and do • thousand and onir small things which she eonld do perfectly well, and which would lighten Jane's own labours when she is re- ceiving company." "The sort of things, you understand," said Leib. Jane, "that a footman can never do. But, my dear Rebecca, I was amased to hear that you had dismissed her, and no soon after your father's death." "I had my reasons," said Rebecca, very gravely. . . .</p>
        <p>"Hui there in surely nothing against her character, poor thing%" "No," said Rebecca, speaking hlowly. "I asked her to leave me because my dear father did not like her, lie preferred that I should live my life alone rather than keep Mary Gray as my companion." "But he must have had his reasons" "Please regard it," said Rebecca, speaking in the same slow, calm voice, "as a case of prejudice. Such things are unaccountable, and ought not to interfere with Miss Gray's securing another situation." "To be frank," Raid Lady Jane, "I took rather a fancy to her. She seemed so bright and capable, and, knowing that she lived with you for several years, I felt quite cer- tain about her character. Then, my dear, she told me this marvellous story about the diamond."</p>
        <p>How could she possibly have found out?" said Rebecca.</p>
        <p>"She did not tell inc how; she only said that all the gems I possessed were of no value a all in comparison to this one soli- tary diamond which you, for greater ' security, wear on your person. Did she speak the truth, Rebecca?" 'I am sorry," mid Rebeees, "I cannot say either ' Yes ' or 'No.'" "You will not deny it, then. Your not</p>
        <p>denying it proves ths't it.ie true." Rebecca was silent.</p>
        <p>"You might tell us, Becky," said Mrs. Lorrimer then. "Do you suppose that Jane or I would whisper a single word about it? Besides, to tell you the truth, darling, it is 'exceedingly unsafe for you to wear anything so valuable as that diamond about you. In days like ours, when there are no many un- scrupulous people everywhere girls cannot be too careful; and you are living by your- self with only a few maidservants! You ought to confide in us, and, above all things you ought to put the diamond into a place of safety. Mary Gray knows Your secret. If you dislike her, which in evident, there is no saying to whom she will repeat it. You are in danger, Rebecca, and it is only right that we, your friends, should warn You." "I am very sorry," said Between, "that I cannot give you the smallest information. I am forbidden by circumstances over which I have no control, to oar one word, good or bad, with regard to this matter. But I ap- preciate your kindness, Lady Jane, and yours, dear Mrs. Lorrimer." Lady Jane pouted. • . .. •</p>
        <p>' "It-was sciiroelv worth coming out in this horrid cold and damp And fretting all ray hair unfrinaled to be told that you appreciate out. kindneta., Rebecca."</p>
        <p>"But I dc;," said Rebecca, standing up suddenly and taking the lady's hand. "I do, most thoroughly.'"f hen she added: "Perhaps before long, perhaps in a very, very short time I may be able to tell you all that I am now obliged to conceal." ''ln that case, Rebecca,— said Lady Jane, with great eagerness, "you will allow me to see the diamond? I have a passion for gems, and would invest a very large sum of money in the purchase of this stone. I always con- sider that the purchase of line precious stones is one of the best possible investments for money. But now, my dear, I see you are obstinate—von always were—as obstinate as a mule. Will you do something else? I have not engaged Mary Gray; whether I do engage her or not remains to be area, But I want you to leave this lonelY house and come</p>
        <p>and atay with me for a few daya. 1 am par- ticularly anxious that you should do so, will your' "You are very, very kind," said Rebecca; "but I have a great acal to do at borne just now, and, Lady Jane, while thanking you most heartily, I cannot leave Paddock Row at present." Lady Jane looked at Mrs. Lot.rimer, and Mrs. Lorrimer looked back at her.</p>
        <p>"Then, Rebecca," she said, "if you will neither confide your secret to us nor take ad- vantage of Lady Jane's hospitality, may we do something else for you? May we send man here—a reliable person, to be in the house day and night for the present? We do not like the idea of a young and defenceless girl keeping such valuable property on her person." "You assume that fact, dear Mrs. Lent- seer. I have admitted nothing." Mrs. Lorrimer nodded. "I take the thing for granted," she said; "it is about as certain as that I am sitting on this chair. You yourself have admitted to us that you don't know how Miss Gray got into possession of your secret." "I cannot imagine why she told you the story she did," said Rebecca in a guarded tone. _</p>
        <p>“Well, if such is the case with her, and she knows, think how many more people may he let into the secret. Rebecca, if you have no respect for yourself and no fear for your own life, you ought at least to value that precious stone which you carry for safety on Joel body.” Rebecca gave an inwara start. There was no indication of it on her pale face ; but she remembered the letter in the manly hand which she had read, and the hope, no fully ex- pressed by the writer, and the pleasure, not far distant now, which would be here when she took the diamond from its eive and put it into his hand. .</p>
        <p>"I am very much obliged to you both," she Raid. "Forewarned is forearmed. I cannot believe even now that Mary Gray knows any- thing. She was always a very curious, rather prying person, and that I think was one rea- son why my father did not care for her. But I will consider your advice, and, if necensary, will act upon it to the extent of asking my parlourmaid, Lucy, to get her father to sleep on the premises." "Well, that is a little better than nothing," said Mrs. Lorrimer. "And now, my dear, having worried you to extinction, can we do anything to really help you?" "You have been no very kind al to call, and I am grateful," maid Rebecca. "You will come and see me in a week, won't you, Beek' said Lady Jane. "I wil if I can; thank you a thousand times for being no kind." The ladies felt that their visit was termi- nated. Rebecca rang the bell, and Lucy showed them to their carriage.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER ix: THI WRITING OF A LiTTIR.</p>
        <p>A week went by. Rebecca Henshall was seated one day in her drawing-room. The usual routine of life had resumed its sway at No. 9, Paddock Row. Rebecca had got over the state of nervousness which the visit of Lady Halliday and Mrs. Lorrimer had caused. She took the precaution of locking her door at night, but said nothing to Lucy with regard to her father staying in the</p>
        <p>It was now the eighth of February, and in twelve more days, if he was true to his word, Lometale would arrive and relieve her of that treasure which, in spite of all her bravery, was becoming an incubus. Mrs. Lorrimer and Lady Jane had visited Rebeccs more than once during this period, but had said nothing further about the dia- mond, and were kindness itself in every par- ticular. It was tea time, and Lucy had just brought in the tray and placed it before her mintrers when there came a smart ring at the front door. "Shall I show anybody in, ma'am?" she asked of Miss Henshall. Not without letting me know first, p:ease, Lucy. I can only see a few friends just now." Lucy went to the door. There was some csger convereation. It seemed to Rebecca, sho was listening, as though someone were pexhing past Lucy. The nest minute, the dining-room door was opened, and Mary stood in Rebecca's presence. She looked well. Her pale face had more colour than Rebecca had ever seen before. Her &amp;esn was smart and her hair becomingly arranged. She said, in an eager voice: "Forgive me, Rebecca, for forcing myself upon you. Lucy did her best to keep me out, but I really could not stand your denying yourself to me. Besides, I come in a new character. Congratulate me, please, Rebecca. I am now Mrs. Deepe." Rebecca rose, rind said in a cold voice: "So you have rely done it?" "Yes; two or three days ago. I am quite accustomed to the married state." But I thought—" began Rebecca. "Oh, we hurried the thing up with a special license; they are nothing like so dear as they used to be, and Arnold and I thought it: worth while. I am as happy as the day is long, and Arnold makes the most delightful, fascinating husband. A man with a spice of mischief in him is ten times am attractive as your excellent, good, plodding, dare-nothing fellows. My husband would dare a good deaf, and no, for that matter, would I. We've got the dearest little flat in Queen'a Mansions, Bayswater, and have furnished it to our heart's content. It is sweetly pretty." "I am glad you are happy," said Rebecca. "As you have come, pray sit down. Won't you have a cup of tea?" "Thank you;'' said Mary, seating herself without an instant's hesitation and spread- ing out her smart frock no that it might at- tract Rebecca's attention. "It does seem queer—doesn't it, that I should be drinking tea as a visitor in your house.' "I did not believe that you would call," said Rebecca.</p>
        <p>She poured out some tea and gave a cup to Mary. ,Mary NM not to be abashed. She took her tea leisurely, and helped herself to a piece of hot cake. "You look just as usual, Rebecca," she said, staring full at the girl, "and black be- comes yOU. "I would rather you didn't make personal remarks," said Rebecca. _ . . .</p>
        <p>Mary tossed her head. "I'm cure I do not care to," she maid. "I thought I'd come to see you, for I have a little request to mdke." Rebecca sat very still and cold, her eyes fixed on Mary's face. "You are, of course, well aware of the fact that neither Arnold nor I are well oft; to tell you the truth, we married on the two hundred pounds which you were good enough to sloe me in order to get rid of me. We are paying for the furniture of our flat on the hire system. We have enough for the pre- sent, but our money won't hold out very long. Arnold has his post in the city, but that only brings him in a pound a week—almost noth- ing, as you can well believe. Now, I want to help my dear husband, and have practi- cally got a poet in the house of a lady whom you know very well—Lady Jane Ilalliday. She saes that she can receive me provided'l bring her a letter from you giving me at least the qualities which the usual domestic is expected to possess, namely, honesty, quietness, orderley conduct. etc. She seems to he aware of the fact that you have no special love for me, but she doesn't consider it essential that a woman should be ruined for life because her late employer did not le,,tc. Will VI3II Writ° this very short</p>
        <p>letter for me, nod will you do a an once, Rebecca? Oh, pray don't stir. I know where to find your paper; I have not forgot- ten all the things I used to do in this house for no many years." Mary rummaged in Rebecca's little daven- port. She Mood with her back to Rebecca while she was doing no, who took no ap- parent notice of her. Rebecca was lying back in a deep chair, her eye. fixed on the fire. Presently Mary returned with a blob ling pad, pen, ink, some paper and one tnve lop. "Here," she mid, "you can write the letter at once, can't you?" Rebecca did not stir.</p>
        <p>" What in the matter?" said Mary, begin ning, iii spite of herself, to get a little es. eitetl. "Why won't yon write this letter when I ask you? What is the diAlculty?" "You want me to certify," paid Rebecca. "to your quiet and orderly conduct, also tc your honesty. Are you honest?" Mary felt her pale face turning crimson There wan a rage within her heart at that moment which could have murdered the handsome girl who looked up at her IN calmlv. "Wow dare you say such a thing to me?" *aid Mary. "Remember, I have a husband whose province it in to protect me. You had better be careful; although you are r:cli. there is such a thing in the world as depriv- ing a girl of her bread, and if you by any words of yours do ao, the law is on my mid, "It in not a quent!on of law," raid Rebecca. "I merely ask you a question: are you really, in the full sense of the word. honed Forgive me, Mary; I most finish chat I wise about to say. I don't mean for a moment that you have pilfered; you have not robbed my father or nit of money, or of silver spoons. You have been careful and methodical, and have rendered up the ae. count weekly of the cash you spent in house. keeping. Reverthelem Ido tusk you if in 3110 r ffeart of hearts you can give yourself character for perfect honesty, for openness. Can you on all cecanions declare yourself to be above board. For instance, Marv, I will come to the point at once. Why did you tell Lady Jane Halliday the story you did?" "Oh," said Mary, "we are coming to the point at last; you mean about that din- " You needn't clutch hold of the table as though you wanted to keep yourself from falling." "Yen; I know about the diamond; it is rather dangerous for you to wear it." "I admit nothing," replied Rebecca; "but why did you tell that story? If you have ally knowledge, you intist hare got it by die- honest means. That is why I heaitate to give you a character for honesty." Mary laughed. "If having wits means being dishonest," ahe 'mid, "I will tell you at once that I am guilty of that offence. Your father wore a black ribbon round his neck; on all occasions he wore that ribbon, and for that matter, it is trainiferred at the prevent moment to your neck. I WC it peeping up above the collar of your dress." Rebecca rose. She was trembling vio- "t," she said, "go! When you left thin home it was understood between us that you were not to return. Go, and never let me see your face agaM." "f won't stir," said Mary, "until you give me that letter. I want the pest an Lady Jane Halliday's companion, and 1 miff have it! You have no right to withhold from me the qualities you would give Lucy or any servant in this house." _ .</p>
        <p>"All," ,aid Rebecca. "if I could only re- gard you as I do, Lucy." She considered for a moment; then she .tid, briefly: "I will write a letter to Lady Jane and mend it to her by pont." that won't do. moat take it."</p>
        <p>RCl;;;ca—co-nlia-e-red a little.F;;;ttic.r. "Very well,' she said, slowly. She then wrote a few lines on the black- edged paper. "Dear Lady Jane,—At Mrs. Dcepe's re- quest I write to say that she lived in thin house with my father and me as companion to me, and as housekeeper, for fire years. During that time we found her capable, quiet, and orderly.—Relieve me, dear Lady Jane; yours with affection, “ilill[CCA The mordent Rebecca began to write the letter Mary went and eeoa at the opposite side of the fireplace. "I can't find any envelope," said Rebecca suddenly. "Menke go to my davenport and bring me a packet." "There is one staring you in the face," raid Mary. Rebecca took it up. She remembered long afterwards that it ICSIA not exactly in point of texture like one of her own envelopes, but it gave her no uneasinesa at the time. She directed the envelope to Lady Jane Halliday, put the enclosure within, and, raising the lap to her mouth, wet the vim with her tongue in order to fasten the envelope. 'lle next moment she fell forward with a faint groan.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-05_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>FOUNTAIN OF BEAUTY</head>
        <p>THE FOUNTAIN OF BEAUTY</p>
        <p>L. T. MEAD, Author of "The Rebellion of LI Carrington," "A</p>
        <p>Sweet Girl Graduate," &amp;c.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-05_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>CHAPTER VIII.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER VIII.</p>
        <p>INQVISITIVI V 1817068.</p>
        <p>Rebecca breathed freely when Mary Gray FAN no longer in the house. She was natu rally a very orderly and self-contained girl. Her deep feelings she kept to herself. In her father's presence she had been gay, cheerful, full of life, full of high spirits. But now that he was gone, there was no one to draw forth her gay laughter, or to bring a smile to her grave face. Her one idea wan to carry out his wishes, to follow in his steps, to do all within her power to honour hie memory, and to con tinue on her own account the simple life which he himself had no greatly preferred. When the will was read, Rebecca learned that she wax rich, that her income amounted to several thousands a year. But wealth meant as little to this girl as poverty did to Mary Gray. Money had been supplied to her from her birth in moderate quantities, and she had not the slightest idea of its real value and of all its potentialities for happiness or the reverse. " •</p>
        <p>During the remainder of the day of her father's funeral, she refused to see any visi- tors, and sat quietly by herself thinking much of the past, and now and then giving way to a silent fit of crying. When night came, she lay down to tneep with her hand clasped round the little case which contained the valuable diamond. She would keep her word to her father, and wear the precious atone until she could hand it over to Gerard Lonsdale. She did not give a thought to the fact that the gem would become her own pro- perty if Lonsdale did not appear to claim it before the end of February. The next morning she awoke with • feeling of great depression, but made up her mmd not to give way to it. There was much to at- tend to, and she must go through the painful Me. of reading all those letters that had come to her father during his illness. She determined to get through this work in the cosy study, where she best loved to sit, and where he and she, as a rule, spent their even- ings together. The fire wss lit by the orderly housemaid. The room was bright ana cosy. Rebecca entered, shut the door, locked it, and, going to the serMaire, where she -had put her father's letters away, opened it, and took oet a vast pile. These letters were not specially interest- ing. Many of them were small bills. One was from an aunt in the country who had not seen Rebecca for years, and offered to come up to town to pay Robert Henshall and his daughter a visit. Rebecca lad forgotten the very existence of this aunt, for her father and Aunt Martha had quarrelled years ago, and the old man was an tenacious in his power of hating as he was in his power of loving. He had never forgiven his sister Martha for marrying, as he expremed it, be- neath her, and had scarcely mentioned her name to his child. Rebecca read her letter now, and felt • sense of reproach at not hav- ing acquainted her with her father's death, in order that she might come to attend his funeral. She put the letter aside to answer it that very day, collected all the different small accounts. intending to send them on to her solicitor, Mr. Flower, for immediate pay- ment, and then continued to read her letters. Almost at the bottom of the pile use that epistle which had so excited the curiosity of Arnold Deepe. Rebecca looked at it, read the word "Teheran" on the postmark, and, all of a sudden, w ith the first feeling of real interest which she had experienced, re- membered the little black ribbon round her neck, and the precious gem whiab rested against her heart. What a pity that her father had not known of this letter! Rebecca opened it, and read it eagerly. She read it once, and then again. At the end of the second reading, she put it quietly in its enve- lope, and gazed straight before her. She was owning to • sense of relief. The black ribbon need only remain round her neck a very few days longer. Gerard Lone- dale must be already on hie way home. He, would call at her father's business address She perceived that the letter she had just read, marked "Private," had been forwarded from there to Paddock-row. She reeolved to write to Lonsdale to the City address ac- quainting him with the fact that her father was dead, and asking him to call on her; she would be very glad to give up the diamond. Altogether, this letter gave her a feeling of absolute pleasure. It would be nice to talk about her dear father with Lonsdale. Ile wrote in a manly and grateful fashion, too. How good of her father to have kept the gem no safe, and how valuable it must really be! Lonsdale was in danger on account of it. Rebecca could not quite understand this, lie would not know safety until he gave it back to the one to whom it belonged. Rebecca wondered with a sense of growing interest what the real story of the diamond could be. Then it suddenly occurred to her to look at it. She had never hitherto felt the slightest desire to do no. Every morning she removed the black ribbon from her neck, putting it on again immediately after she hail taken her morning bath; but she had never unclosed the little cam which contained the priceless gem. Now, having reassured herself that the door was locked, she put her hand to her neck, untied the black ribbon and took the diamond from its hiding-place. Mr. Hen- shall had put the diamond into a little silver case, juet large enough to fit it. This case was covered with a tiny black silk bag, so that it should not rub against the neck of the wearer. Rebecca untied the bag, slipped it off. and opened the ca.. She knew a little about diamonds, for her tether of late years had been lavish in the rich presents he had given her; but never before had she seen anything like no magni- ficent as this star of the Emit on which her eyes rested. She remembered her father's description of the strange and yet vivid rose light which caused the diamond to be no dazzling. In size, it was about as large as a very small hazel nut. It was cat in rose fashion, and eent out rays at every point. Rebecca looked down at it as one dazzled and amazed. am afraid of it," she said to herself. She had a queer sensation as thoug% she were looking at something uncanny. A weight !seemed to clutch at her heart. "I shall be glad," she once again whie. pered to herself, "when Gerard Lonsdale re- lieve. me of anything so marvellous, so alive, so—so--acid-ed. This is a sort of gem that would tempt men to crime. I shall be glad, more than glad. when I give it up to Mr. Lonailale." .</p>
        <p>Rebecca was still occupied over her father's letters, and had returned the dia- mond to its place of safety next her heart, when there came a tap at the study door. She went at once to open it. Lucy mood without. . - .</p>
        <p>"Please. ma'am," she anal, "Mrs. Lorri- mer and Lady Jane Halliday have come to see you. I said I knew you were engaged; but they want to SCO you very badly indeed, and are waiting in the dining-room. What shall I nay to them. ma'am?" Rebecca paused for a minute, then she said slowly: "You were right to admit them: they are old and kind friends. I will go to them at once, Lucy." She left the study, and entered the dining- room. _</p>
        <p>Mrs. Lorrimer was • pretty young woman of about twenty-eight years of age. She bad been fond of Rebecca for • long time, and the girl was oducerely attached to her. Lady</p>
      </div>
