        <p>, ilest•Wat es. -fleet-woven, especially after a 511 COM. sloe of them, are a Ii tle over. lodating, nod one generally notice. that at this tired mason of the year people are very prone to become more Irritating to their fellow-creeturea. lient.a eves are .11 right for the ice merchant, and the miner:ll water manufacturer, and also it seems for the Ir Jok •.I ler, whose sales sl a nys go up with the thermometer, hot reels forced to the conclusion tha., very hot wetter is not to be &amp;wired in this nervous age in *blob we lire. It metres intorno ia , is bad for the temper, end misses a Ivaco if dist crakes the duties of life,and most of its pleas Iros,vpry irksome. Those who have to go about their work a. tootal tell us that they are on the verge of • Dervous breakdown, sod that probably dreadful things will happen. They only recover when about to go for their holidays, and weave mostly very glad to sae them gone, and obtain some relief from the fretful enrppiahneas which is e eery catching complaint when the thermometer is continually in the eightiee. A Canteen Ileltdey.—A writer in • Loudon piper give. • tempting description of the joys of apendlog nee's holiday in a caravan. Mont of us have experienced the uneatisfactorineas of taking chance apartmente at the seaside, end although the growing practice of rent- ing • Cottage fore month, possemes advantagem' yet it would seem that a caravan is even more agreealole, and may be utiliwed with much her expense. The Mat c rot of the vehicle in put at from £3O to £lO, the rent of • " pitch " at less than £5 a year, and the yearly upkeep it is remarkel is represented by a coat of print An appreciable addition 'Mould be made for the hire of • borne, at any rate ocevelonally, for the occopents of a van are scarcely likely to desire to spend all their holiday at one I per, indeed it is perhaps the chief delight of caravanning that, with the assistance of a horse, one can move from place to place at will. The gipny van, abi.:ll the writer has ia contemplation, rultices only for two persons trots large party coo Ire accommodated be an additional tent or two.</p>
        <p>Seeing The bituation.—There are few more distress• log etuatiom than that caused by a would-be bride or bridegrtom waiting in vain oath. stepe of the altar for the other party to arrive. Usually it in the men who repents in haste, but this order of things was reversed inn recent instance in which • lady of title rem con- cerned. Another cam, in which • bride found herself repudiated it. the lest moment, Imel a more than usually romantic ending for another eligible gentleman of • independent fortune stepped in, and offered to take the place of the defaulting bridegroom, end ems PC.' pled. In olden tint's the lady would have been carried home , fainting in the arms of her tearful and moraine,' relatives. Big the old folks of those days were nut without wiadome, and they sometimes took precautions to guard against snob breaking of hearty. How this wa• done was illustryted by the following item in the register of a Huntingdon village church " December, 1617. Psid for wages spent upon the man that watched John Pickle all night, and the next day till he ISSII married." Cafe—The domestic, cat lise often been throatent ol with tantion, hut as threatened men live long, no the oat, with her proverbial nine lives, should not be much disturbed st the proepect. At the some time it is announced that the Munich City Council tom decided to tax Calf, partly atlas view to lessening their numbers. This seems • little s range hemuse cats are, and always have been, comparatively entree in the German States. It is mid th it there are not quite half a million cats in the whole German Empire, whilst it Is estimated that there are four millions in Fronce, and nearly eight millions in the United Kingdom. This is clearly the land of frour•legged mt., sad since the first cat show was held at the Crystal Palace in London in 1871, there have been formed several cat clubs, which hold annual exhibitions in the leading cities of the country. Irish Poplitt.—There Is • distinct tendency on the part of fickle fashion, to favour heavier and richer fabrics than those transparent materials that have been so much worn of let*. As • Wand consequeece Irish poplin is very much in eviderce at the moment. It in —in the hest qualities—a rich, beautiful and durable myteriel, and fund much favour with Queen Victoria. Duren Alexandra also wears it frequently, while queen %lorry has main brooght it into prominence by appear- ing in then,s made of it during the recent visit to Ireland, where the looms are ale,, in full stork and even no experience it difficulty in fulfilling orders. As most people 4now Irish poplin is • mixture of bilk and the 'Meet wool, the warp being of the former, the weft of the latter sutetance. The silk used in the beat makes is of Chousis fittest, neither leaded nor adulterated es other silks ere. The dyeiog process differs too, for it takee place in the thread before the fabric is woven. Dray, evening, Court drese, opera cloak., Jua., are being made of this nisterinl, duck lee&amp; itself to all occasions, whether Cer.nollial or otherwise. It isle greet demand too for moon ties, for it does not cream, ties easily, is manufactured in beantiful sh•des, and compares in durairility with other material. used for thin purposm 0.100111 and Trim n, ln tide season's cotton goods evils and stripes co me in for their share of favour, whilst the coloura aro prettier than ever before PeJe and soft are popular Woos, delightfully subdued ! the rose tints, thents tea illoliCOUrs bl.diog of them • with white. Forrially pleseing are the raspberry loom. ! One characteristic of this wirron has beer, the diversity —almost endless in their VariaLloll—of trimming,. The r quaintest combinations have been revived, sun t lie most bisarre mixture of eilk• and satins, turd numberless other fabric., successfully •coorepliabed. Evening dresses, at Plesent, are simplicity Itself ; classical lines</p>
        <p>,geoo.l</p>
        <p>lam timely lii mined and the Oche in tulle or any other disphanout mated 1 adds q minters. that snits the style ailmiratily, whilst it is chtrmiag and bemoning, and constitutes io ninny cum the entire trimming of the comers Long linsa of brawny placed closely r arc seen on cloth models, for early autumn wear. Seine bottont are q liet end unobtromive, lint the long lion of hit peril mitt..s tin coloured clothe elthougn hangmen...are certainly in duutitfol testv—se are those of • iliffererit colour to the material. New autumn ts inv.:tugs in hlsck and white heads display notch ingenuity in light end shade effect., especially where the de94. are II iral, and these Idea and white trictroings are very arocessfully used on coloured &amp;mem</p>
        <p>home-Made !Arend°, Water, —The lavender...tillers, "with their plaintir• cry, are now sprawling far sod wide in the land, and owing to the fine summer, the delicate greyish-purple !Tikes are more then usually strong is (regent parlor... housewives will therefore do well to lay in • good stock for their drawers, ward- robes, and linen cupboards, but it it a mistake to try to utilise the fl mats for other purposes. Memo-made lavender water may be better sad more easily obtained, by mixing together • pint of rectified merit, fear MMus. of distilled water, and three drachms each of oil of lereoder, and cringe flower water. To this mixture should be added fire drops each of oil of cloves, oil of oinnewon, end tete of roma h should ! be left. to stand for a fortuight, and thee Illterel through • muslin bag of OtagausiUss °aflame.* before bottling. ! It is better for being kept for four or lire months before using. A Complexion Desnlifier.—Fashloosble Isoll4o just sow pinning their faith to the pineapple as • com- plexion beautifier. Tney consider • slice or two of this delicione —but also sons 'what expensive frult,—iedis- pensab'e as a climax to breakfaet, lunch, aid dinner. wild° at tea time it appears in the form of aancivrichre.</p>
