      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-05_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>Ii DISTRICT tIW3 d</head>
        <p>Ii DISTRICT tIW3 d</p>
        <p>Moore.</p>
        <p>Trap TO COISTIIR.—On Seturihy shoot twenty-five members and friends of the Wesley Guild paid a visit to Cheater. The party left Daresbitry Station hy the 2.21 p.m. train, arriving In Chester soon after 3 o'olock. The time before tea was spent on the Hirer Dee, molt of the party taking the steamer to Eccleeton Ferry, and on retorning about G o'clock, tea was partaken of at Mame. Cottle's Cafe In Bridge-street. After tea, the Secretary, on behalf of the member, of the Guild, presented the Rev. F. W. and Mrs. Keraick with a pair of ell ver vases as • alight memento of their two years very happy and useful work with the Guild. Mr. and Mrs. Kernick acknowledged the gift and thanked the members for their kindness and appreciation. After Ira the time was spent in viewing the sights of this lotereating old City. The patty left for home by the 8.1:, p.m. train from Chester, after • very enjoyable outing.</p>
        <p>Stretton. DRATII ci, PRONIINID, INIIAIIITANT.—We regret to mooed the death of Mr. John Derbyshire, of Summit Cottage, Stratton, which took place on the 28th ult. Mr. Derbyshire, eh., was in his Slat year, bad been in failing health for some time, but was only taken aerioualy ill about a week tefore his death. He wee a son of the last surviving member of the family of the leta Mr. James Derbyshire who resided at Newton-by-Deresbury and mu also a well-known (Haker. When be resigned the surveyorship to the old Daresbury Highway Board, a position which heocoupied for many peers, he became a member of that authority and his practical experience wee turned to useful purpose. At one time he took a prominent part in local and imperial questions in War- rington and the outlying districts. The funeral took place on Tuesday at the burial ground at Whitley belonging to the Society of Friends. - - -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-05_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>Culdetb.</head>
        <p>Culdetb.</p>
        <p>iNDID.F.ADYNT NIETUODDST SUNDAY SDDOOl..—The annual field day in connection with this piece was held on Saturday. Early in the afternoon the junior mholsra assembled in the field where tea was provided much to the enjoyment of the youngsters. After tea a procession was formed and the scholars and teachers, headed by the Cottages Home. Band and carrying their ocentiful banner, marched round the villas.. Sports, games, Ac., were afterwards held in the field kindly lent by Mr. G. Taylor. The playing of the National Anthem at dusk brought the afternoon'n enjoyment to an agreeable close. NZWCHURCH CHURCII.—The annnal tea party and field day was held on Saturday afternoon. Early to the afternoon the animism and teachers marched round the eccornpanied by the Reformatory band from Newton-le-Willowr. After the procession tea wax pro- vided in the schoolroom after which an adjournment was made to • field kindly lent by T. E. Withington, Esq., where sports, !games, Au , were indulged in until dusk,</p>
