      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-05_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>Grappenhall.</head>
        <p>Grappenhall.</p>
        <p>'fur 1, sre Ma W. LIR /GFIEY.-ft is with regret we record the death of Mr. W. H. Malley, which took place late no Friday night at his residence, None (lids, (Happen,.ll. For the last three years be had been in • delicate sthte of health, having for nearly two years been confined to the house. Mr. Rigtey, who was 7; yean of age, had been twice married. Ile I a widow, two eons and four daughters. Mr. Itigbey was from IS% to I 80,; atstionmaster and goods' agent at the Central Station. It is interesting to note that he was the first stationmaster in Warringion.Previous to his appointment to that rmponsible office he held posts ender the Great Western Railway Company and the Cambrian Railway Company. Though • strong Conservative he took no active part in politics. He was a member of the C..tmervatith Club for many yeast He was. Churchman and etteeded the servloes at St. Ann's Church, where for a time he held the office of Vicar's warden.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-05_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>PARISH COUNCIL MEETING.</head>
        <p>PARISH COUNCIL MEETING.</p>
        <p>On Wednesdny evening tbe timothy meeting of the shove Council was held. Item were preeent—Messre A Lister-Kaye, T Vox, W Lomas, F Ellison, G A Leach, end Charles II Honlt (clerk). The Chairman of the Ccuncil (Mr. F. R. Roberta) wrote to say that it was impossible for biro to be present and apologised to the Council for his absence On the proposition of Mr. Lomas, seconded by Mr. Ellison, Mr. A. Lister-Kaye wee elected to preside.</p>
        <p>THE DST NCISANCE. The Clerk read • letter from the Cmuty Surveyor stating that all the grants from the Roads Board had been allotted for the year, but if the Council would bear one-third of the cost of tar-.praying. The Chester road could be done.</p>
        <p>Mr. It. accosted that the Council could not afford to bear the coat and all that they oculd do wee to nit that they might be placed first on the list for soother r t. This was agreed to. .</p>
        <p>A 1.1,1,1) DANCER TO 1100311. Mr. Leach load that it was eurprising that no notice had been taken of our report to the nuisance inspector about the open ditches. These ditches were in a fearful condition, bad enough to create an epidemic and ought to be mien to at once. Therefore he moved Ulla another letter be sent to the nuisance inspector milling his attention to the condition of these filthy ditches.</p>
        <p>A 116MBP.R.S RESIG:4ATION.</p>
        <p>M. leech nee • letter to the chairmen of the Council, regretting that owing to business reasons he found it absolutely necessary to reign from the Council. . . . . .</p>
        <p>Mr. Kaye For the information of some members I might explain that Mr. Leach ptataed his resigns- tion before O at the last meeting. I am sorry thst be now finds it ...lusty to resign. I think all will agree that we are losing a most useful mexther of the council. (Hear, hear.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-05_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>Penketh.</head>
        <p>Penketh.</p>
        <p>SUDDEN DEATN.—Many Of Our readers will regret to hear of the death of Mrs. Elizabeth Dwerryhouee wife of Mr. S. Dwerryhotwe Warrington•road, Penketb, which took place with painful suddenness on Sunday night about 9.15. The deceased was In the garden when •he wee seized with heart failure. Dr. Murray was at once seat for but Mrs. Dwerrybouse passed awes, soon after his arrivnl. The funeral took planc at the B.okoy Cemetery ton Wednesday afteruooon. The Rev. J. It. Jooes M.A., officiated. ERSII TRADE MgrrlNt.S, which have been well attended, have taken place this week. On Tuesday, Mr. J. W. Taylor, of the Free Trade league, addressed a meeting on the Recreation llround. Mr. W. Nloores presided.—On Monday evening the meeting was held at Huntley-street, off Hood-lane, near SAnkey Bridge and wu addressed by Mr. Taylor and Mr. J. A. Seddon. There was a good attendance. Mr. Taylor spoke first, and in his remarks, which were well received, gave ninny effective argurnent• in favour of Free Trade. Mr. Seddon followed and was received with great cheering. At the close three cheers were celled for Mr. Seddon, which were given heartily. Mr. Sorbed Shaw pee- Pusicutit INCoNIE TAX giIiXTION.--In the /loose of Commons no Wednesday,Vietiount Wolmer Newton. C) asked the Chancellor of the Exchequer whether he still order the return of IS.. t Mr. J. Woodward, of Penketh, Warrington, a hich the di. ict commi.ioners offence have admitted they wrongfully extorted from him as income Inc for the year 1909.10.—Mr. Illingworth in • written reply 1-1 am unable to accept tie aug4eation of the noble lord's question that income tat WAS wrongly paid by Mr. J. Woodward for the year 1909-1910. As my right hon. friend he- previouily explained, the ussagroent upon Mr. Woodward for that year was oonti,med by the district commls•loners of times in the absence of an appeal.</p>
        <p>WOMILN'S LAIfOrR LEACCIL—On Friday of last week a meeting nod*, the auspices of the Peeketh Vomen's Labour League, was held on the Recreation Ground. Mrs. Margaret Bonfield of the National Women's !Amor Longue, spoke from • lorry toe good crowd of men and women, for upwards of an hour. She slid thnt she bad come from Cardiff, where the women of the Labour Leagoe had done • great deal of goof work while the strike had been in progress. They had been organiaing hondreds of girls, moat of whom worked in laundries under sweated conditions, and now ohe won pleased to nay they had got for them better conditions all round. (Applause). She made • great appeal to the women present to join the local lengue, and so help in the good work they were doing. (Cheers). Miss Boofield was supported on the lorry by Mrs. Collinwood Cook (president), Mrs. W. Show and Mr. George Faulkner, who °coupled this chair.</p>
        <p>Lymm. CONGREGATIONAL, CIIItI,TIA, SY.RVICS There we a good gathering of young people at ;this meeting on Monday evening. The address wan given by Mr. J. D. It. Smith and was most interesting and helpful. Mr. Arthur Thomna occupied the chair. Owing to holidays there will be no further meeting. until Monday, August 2lsl, 1911. UNITXIr, Ml•sli. BAND. —The open-air service wan held in Booth'. Hill on Sundny evening. There waa • good attendance. The addresses were given by Mr. E. Tvreedie and Mr .11) El Smith, and woo highly appre- °kited. Mr J W Downwood rendered • solo very ' creditably I have a S ivionr in glory." Mr Patrick hlarr preeided. Mr William Moss was the accompanist. The meeting neat Sunday evening will be held at Brookflsld.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-05_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>A MAWS UNSELFISH ACT.</p>
        <p>At Runco; ge;Sio—ois—a;;lll;Oria—y7NfilliaM Faulkner, Porter-street, wee presented with the illumimted certificate of the Liverpool Shipwreck and Humane Society for saving a child from drowning at Runcorn on the Bth of May. The chairman of the bench (Mr. John Littler) mid that Faulkner saw this child 'Mopping in the middle of the kiridgemarer canal, and dived an and succeeded in getting the little boy to the side. Phe cases that generally came before that coon had their origin In aelfishnese, and it was a pleasure to. the bench to recogniae an rinselflsh sc.. They hoped that the country would never lack men of his cSaracter, and no long aa it did not they had little need to be afraid of the future. Faulkner reterned hi. thanks, and Superintendent Ennion avid case, of rescue were carefully Investigated by an police, end only them really worthy were recommended for reward.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-05_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>ALLEGED ASSMLT %Tr!! A HATPIN.</head>
        <p>ALLEGED ASSMLT %Tr!! A HATPIN.</p>
        <p>At ii;;;;;;"i;:s1W.(;,", itit,;;;;,*;.;i;;;.-t-h;:g. of assault was brought by Robert Done, no elderly man, residing in Sanuy•lane, Weston Point, sgainst Polly Hale, of Leonardatreet. Mrs. hole was also summoned together with Mrs. Violet Stevenson for damaging his propel ty. Complainant mid that on Saturday night, 22nd ult., his grandeon hod a diapute will] Mrs. Stevenson. At midnight Mrs. Stevenson sod Mrs. Hale came to his bonse and kicked a oanel of the door in. They burst the lock nod bolt oft the door. He opened the door and Mr.. Hale drew a pin from her bat and atabbed repeatedly in the hands and arms and once in the head. he defence was a denial, and a or...summons was issued, Mrs. Hale alleging that ,)one had blackened her eye and beaten bar till she was black, blue, and The first cam wan dismissed, but Mrs 17sle and Mn. Stevenson were fined for causing wilful damage.</p>
