      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-05_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>BUILDER BLAMES THE WEATHER. A meeting had been convened In Liverpool on Tuesday of the creditors of Edward Greenough,builder, 126, Thep. Heath-need, St. Helens. From the state- ment of affairs which bee been prepared it sppears that the liabilitien expected to reek for di•idend amount to £299 13s 7d. sod the assets are estimated by the debtor to realise E2f.S. The debtor attributes bra failure to " had weather for building and inexperienoe of buildnig regulatiuma" In the absence of any creditor. no meeting was bold, and the matter remains in the hands of the Official Receiver (Mr. E. D. Symond).</p>
        <p>COLLIER'S ATTEMPTED SUICIDE. At St. Helens, on Monday, Isaac Kinder, collier, 3, Reker.street, was charged with attempting to commit !suicide by Jumping into the canal, and also with , neglecting his two children. Chief Inspector Goodell stated that cc Saturday afternoon Constable Wholley Ines near Island's-hrow and eaw prisoner struggling on the canal bank with a roan named Lanaghan, who bad pulled him out of the I canal. Malley arrested prisoner, who said he intended , to drown himself and would do Regain, adding that if , he bad had his daughter aged Nor with him they would never have got him out of the canal only with irons, and that it bad been in his mind all week. Kinder was remanded for a week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-05_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>ROSE 91:FEN FESTIVITIES.</p>
        <p>Favoured by glorious weather the Rose Quota festivities, promoted by the local football club, took place on Saturday. The procession wan of no great length, but what there was of It wee disticc:ly good. At the head were Mr and Mrs 0 F Clarke in a curiae*, and they were followed by the Haydock Prise Band. Then came a batch of 45 rose pole and Parisienoe Belle dancers, who had been trained by Mr T Hardman, and they were followed by a tableau representing " The white slave." A batch of flower girls preceded another tableau "Little Ba-Peep and Boy Blue," for which Mr. Large was responsible. Fruit girls, Highland soldiers, a comic bandand other tableaus made up the proceasion until the Eariestown Prize Band headed a troupe of Highland dancers (IS boys and 18 girls), who bad been trained by Mr. .8. Openshaw. who slim trained the troupeet Rose of s Coronation t to w. eote . n d a nce,rs sa . carriage gRe drawny lance who y four bays, with outriders, attended by swordbearer, sceptre bourn, crown bearer, lady-in-waiting, page bo3s, train bearers, and maids of honours, while she also had a body guard of Boy Scouts. The Eccles Hooligan Hussars Ilmd headed the last section of the prccession which was made up of tableaux, fl:c. After parading the principal streets of the town the procession halted at the Market-plaoe, where Mrs G F Clarke crowned the Rose Queen. After the ceremony there MN a rose pole dance, a display by theliny Scouts, a Highland dance, • display by the Vulcan Gymnswium, a dance by the Parisienne Belles, and another by the Coronation dancers. The whole affair was r,cru.edioglv creditable and it is to be regretted that the attendance was not at all good. LORD WOLMER SUPPORTS THE " DIE }LARDS."</p>
        <p>Lord Wolmer, M.P., Rooks at no.ne length on the Constitutional crisis at the quarterly meeting of the Newton Conservative Association at Warrington on Saturday. He mentioned the fact that he, in company with Mr. Harold Smith, M.P., Mr. Rigby Swift, attended the Helsbury dinner. " Lord Lansdowne and Mr. Balfour," acid Lord Wolmer. ,`• have dacide I in titand aside end allow the passage of the Veto Flill through the House of Lords under this threat of Mr. Asqnith's. I for one frankly regret that decision. (Hear, hear.) No one be. greater respect and admira- tion for Lord Lansdowne and Mr. Balfour than I been; no one is devoted to them with greater loyalty than lam. But I, for one, cannot follow them In their view of the present situation. The question arises—at what point should a man throw off hi, pert. loyalty, or rather his loyalty to hill lenders? I cannot attempt to give you the enact point but therein one p lint upon reaching which amen must act by himself, and that is when he Bads he cannot honourably an otherwise." (Applause). Lord Ilalsbury snd others, continued Lord Wolmer, hat determined t vote against the bill whatever happened, and he for one respected them for the stand they inale,nnd supported them In it. They maid to Mr. Asquith : "Do this dirty work yourself ; we are not going to stilly our heeds by acquirmieg when our consciences tell as we ought to resist." There was no party nplit. As soon es this difference was settled they would he together again pledged moat the name to the principles of their misty on such questions as Home Rule. Lord Widmer predicted that it would he necessary to create about 109 Peers to carry the Parliament Bill and he added that any Tory Peen; who supported the measure otigh t to he turned otit of the party. Mr. W. Peter Ryland., meow:ling a vote of thanks to Lord Wolmer, asked the meeting to send Lord ' Wolmer back to his work in London with the knowledge that, et all events, he was speaking in this matter in the name of this corner of the country.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-05_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>LIBERAL—VICTORY. _ Result of the Middleton Election. SIR RYLAND ADK-IN-S AGAIN RETURNED</head>
        <p>LIBERAL—VICTORY. _ Result of the Middleton Election. SIR RYLAND ADK-IN-S AGAIN RETURNED</p>
        <p>Polling took place on Wednesday in the Middlrton ' by-election, and the result was declared about 10 15 on Wednesday night as follows : Sir W. R. CI. Adkins (L) Mr. W. A. S. Hewitt. Cr)</p>
        <p>Liberal majo.ar Previous elections resulted is under</p>
        <p>5,339 ... 5,273 .... (L.) 11l 1,05 1097 (13y) 5,964 ... 5,66.1 ... L.) 300 1900 1906 1910 Pas 4 7,669 ... 6,266 ... 1,403 11110(Deo.) 1,071 ... 6,284 ... L.) 787 Speaking after the declaration of the pall, Sir Ryland Adidas thanked his supporters for giving hire three victories in two years in circumataaces which might have wraritd lukewarm workers while the glint rause ta which they were devoted still remained incomplete and awaiting float issue. In returning to Parliament he should make it his duty to convey to the leaders cf tbe Liberal Party the fact that there , had been co diversion of the great constitutional j issue., and no misrepresentation of any measure of social reform had been able to allure the majority of the electors of the Middleton Division from their allegiance to freedom, progress and social reform.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-05_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>CORONATION.</head>
        <p>THE CORONATION.</p>
        <p>THE MAYOR RECEIVES THE KING'S MEDAL.</p>
        <p>Before commeuc'ng the routine business at the meeting of the Wm rington Town Council cm Tuesday evening. the Mayor said: I have received from the King the Coronation medal which I am now wearing (Applause.) I received it on Saturday Met lard, with the following letter from his Majesty's Secretary of State for the Home Department, Mr. Winston Churchill. It reads as followa: Home Office. Whitehall, 28th July. 1911. • ' ""</p>
        <p>hare received the King's coininande to transmit toy, u the accompanyirg medal, which hie Majesty hasbeen graciously pleased to confer upon you, to be worn in remembrance of the Coronation of their Majesties. I am to request that you will be gcod enough to seed me on acknowledgment of the receipt of the medal. I am, sir, your obedient servant, The Mayor of Wet rington,</p>
        <p>WINSToN 8. Cuuniit.</p>
        <p>(Applause) The medal, which is of ether, is about as big as a florin. It is embossed on the one side with the King and Queen wearing their crowns, and on the other side with the letters G It., vormenntt d by e crown, and at the foot 0 jib the date, " 22nd June, 1911." itnllar medals hal, been received by cther Chief Magi vt ra'es.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-05_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>THANKING THE MAYOR.</head>
        <p>THANKING THE MAYOR.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the General Purpoaes Committee it was resolved that the thanks of this committee b. given to all who have waisted in the arrangements made for the celebration of the Coronation end that the hearty thanks of this committee be given to len Wonhip the Mayor for his valnahle services in connec- tion with the festivities. _ _ . .</p>
        <p>The following letter bee been received from Mr. Winston Churchill in reply to the Council's address of congratulation on hie 91.jesty's Coronation Home Office, IVhitehall, 220,1 July, 1911. Sir,—l em com- manded by the King to convey to you hereby bin Majesty's thank, for the loyal and dutiful addresa of the Mayor, Aldermen and Burgesses of the Borough of Wareham on on the occiuion of their Miljegise' Corona- tlon.—l am, air, your Obedient servant, WINSTON Carrot 11ILL. The Town Clerk of Warrington." Alderman Behmerr maid the feelings of gratitude to the Mayor for the way in which he had espied out the Coronation celebrntions were embodied in the General Furpous Committee's resolution. Ile thought his Worship had done admirably with the means which the Council placed et his diepcsvl. The sure allowed was not very muniflcent, but the Mayor displayed wonder- ful ability end skill in making the E5OO cover • very lips* number of excellent objects In such a eplendid manner. The way in which the celebration, were carried out under his guidance gave nnhoonded sat isfac- tion to the town. Any little murmur of criticism which might have been heard had died away and was forgotten and the permanent recollection remained in the minds of the people that during the mayoralty of Dr. Joeepit the celebration of theaccrsaion of King George was carried out in every loyal and satisfactory Manner. Alderman St,thor tt, in s toper ling, raid it was a berry responsib lily to undertake the dotiee which devolved upon the Mayor at Coronntion time. Alderman Tr MOON also associated himael I with Alder- man Bennett's expressions. Those people, he maid, who disegreal were either bait,ut or knevr nothing about the mutter. Ile knew that the interest the Mayor took lathe proceeding. throughout end the enxiety he dis- played to mske the catch rations • sitcom we. very deep. Nothing was too great a trouble for him and he was the embodiment of courteny to everybody. Councillor PE sai 1 there was no doubt the Msyor worked very hard and his duties were anything but light. He Ives astounded that in the midst of his professionel duties the Mayor was able to apace so much valuable time. Great Beeriness moat have been , involved, and the Mayor had worked much harder than I occupants of his Oltoe initially did. labia (the speaker's) opinion the Coronation festivities excelled there which were held a few year. ago. Ile hod been lorry to hear some criticisms hot whet hurt him most wane reference to the Iffindnome medal which the alayor preaented to each member of the Council. The souvenirs were provided out of his Worship's own private purse and it sum not right thrit such CritioiEM from people who ought to know better •borild ba ignored. The MAYOR* in ecknowledgment, mid he was deeply gratehd for their generous remarks. He pleaded guilty to having worked hard. Ile did not Isiah Ito be a hypocrite end could any that the matters which engaged his ettention required hard work, and alan demanded ancrifices—perheps mire than be himself knew of. However, it was sufficient reward to feel that he had the nor. II fence respect end appreciation of hie colleagues. lie led acted conscientiously but, of course, one could not plea. everybody. There sans an old saying " If you try to please everybody you please nobody," and he thought there was a great deal of truth in that. In addition to having done their best with the amens at their disposal, the committee bad twee , somewhat originnl in devising the form which the fentivities should take. For instance they had the motor trip for the cripple, and • treat for the deaf nod dumb. Perhaps the comparative matinees of the Coronation •Ilowance stimolated the committee to effort, which might not otherwise have been put forth. He thanked the Council,the members of the Coronntion Committee, end lb.t Metals for the loyal way in which they assisted him. Without their nupport he could have done little. (Hear, hear and applause..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-05_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</head>
        <p>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</p>
        <p>Re-opening after Re-decoration.</p>
        <p>When the Royal Court Theatre throws opes its doors thin (Saturday) evening, patrons will be favour- ably impressed with the transformation which ha, been effected in the interior arrangements of this popular houne Not only has the building been re-decorated and illumit.ated, but the seating accom- modation las undergone a great improvement, every part of the theatre being no titled up MI to ensure the , maximum of comfort to the salience. The decoration. are oir the mint tasteful and artistic lines. The fibrous plaster work has been embellished in it, ikingly effective style, the colours luring HO5O Du Barry pink and toritioise blue, with white and gold relief. The theatre in brilliantly lighted, about 10,1 leg crystal. having been used in the making of the electric , fittings which give a rich and luxurious finish to the theatre. The stalle floor has been laid with a thick pile carpet. underlined with felt, and new seats have been pr witted. These chairs. whic't were used for Mr. Beechanii opera season at His Majesty's Theatre, London, are designed in Chippendale, upholstered in rich crimson velvet, and there is ample knee apace, with r into for passicg. There is also a centre g ingwav, whi.t will be found to lien gnat convenience. it may be mentioned that each seat Clot afoot LI. Several rows of the old stalls have been placed in the pit, an that early corners to that portion of the bonne will have tip-up seats. In the circ!e the seats I, toe Item entirely re-arranged, giving a less cmalp.al appearance, with a more spacious view of the stage. A halt carnet line been laid nund the ci.cle to prevent any noise being made by patrons entering or leaving the theatre during the progress of the performance The upholstery work has been executed by Maws. G. end J. Weaver and the decorations by Mr. F. J. Dell. Mr. Tom Slater, the theatre carpenter, is responaible for the jninering aml general structur sl work. The ligh,ing improvementa have been carried out by the theatre electrician, Mr. flora, Tole, and the costal fittings have lteen male by Miss A. Howard and Mr. Arthur I / riffithn, meta'sers of the theatre staff. The theitre will, of Coors., continue to be under thesis'e management of Mr. A. M. Lowlier, with Mr. H. Warburton as acting manager sod Mr. Mtn Boglirryd as Bailer of the orchestra.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-05_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>RE.OPENING TIES EVENIXI.</head>
        <p>RE.OPENING TIES EVENIXI.</p>
        <p>For ths re-opening of the theatre thin evening a capital sttractroa hos boen 'newel. Miss Marie Dagmsr,who in. Invourios with Warrington audiences, and • strong company will present the powerful romantic Venetian drama " The Tyrant ' by the late Fred C. Soorrfie (fowled on one of Victor flugo's misterpiews). This play will occupy the b •ards for the whole of n‘ xt week. Miss Marie Dagmsr will play " Tinhe," the part crested and made famous in Paris by Madams Slush Betnhardt in • play written on the some subject. THE SEASON'S ATTRACTIONS.</p>
        <p>A moat interes'ing and varied list of engagements bas been made for the autumn and winter season They include : When Knights were Bold," " The Dandy D.cor," "Claudia," " Tantalizieg Tommy" the great Drury-line drama, " The Whip" ; " San Toy; by the opera erction of the Musical Society ; The Girl in the Train," " Brewstees Millions." the Moodyliannets Grand Opera Company. "The (limier Girl," Pew " (the new Gaiety musical comedy) ; Mr. Robert Coortneidge's cam pany in a new musical play, and" The Arcadians." A plutominds is to be produced at Christiuts.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-05_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>OPEN-AIK POLITICS.</head>
        <p>OPEN-AIK POLITICS.</p>
        <p>THE QUACK NOSTRUM OF TARIFF</p>
        <p>"REFORM.'</p>
        <p>The new Liberal van was in ore at the meeting of lb. Warrington Progreasive Labour League held at Bridge Foot on Monday evening. Mr. J. Morton presided and the speaker was Mr. liennish Brown, of Manchester. As usual there was a large and enthusiutic gathering. The CHAIRMAN said that the people of Warrington would not overlook the fact that during the present political situation our present membrr was taking a very Conservative positron and working IN OIRECT OPPOSITION 10 THE INTERMTS or THE I WORK ING•CLASSES.</p>
        <p>(filar, hear.) The House of Lords bad stood sod had bad the power to nand in the way of the people'. expiration. The present Government weal as epoch- making Government, because It was going to do away, once and for all,with the veto of the House of Lordayand in that way they could make it possible for progressive Governments to curry out measures for the realisa- tion of the aspirationn of the work ingclanes generally. Though the Innocence Bill hal been greatly criticised they were convinced that Ilr.l.loyd-George was sincere in his effort to produce • measure which would go to the root of the situation and when the Bill was put on the Stettin book it would boa measure which would greatly benefit the people generally,' (Applanr.)• Mr. BROWN devoted his interesting address to the relative merits of Free Trade and Protection. Ile began by referring to the fact that Scotland only returned a few Conservatire members who favoured Tariff " Reform." They could be nee that If Scotsmen gave up anything they had got a good reason for it. The Scotsmen were sufficiently shrewd to know what business ma, and ifs system of tariffs were found n hereby they could make more money they could in their bottom dollar, as the American maid, that Scotland would hen gone strong for It. Innen of that they bad Scotland almost wild for Free Trade. (Applause) What had the Tariff Reformers beep promising all along ? Is the early days they promised °Wage pension, end Mr. Cbantber• lain said they most have Tariff " Reform "to have old- er pensions, He said it could not be done under a Free Trede eystern, and yet it had been done—a fact which could not even be denied by the Tariff " Reformer." (Hear, hoer) Free Traders did not protein; that everything wee ratisfectory in this country, but when the Tories ceme along with the quack nostrum of Tariff " Reform" 11 hich they evict would core unemployment they were inclined to think before they trusted it. That employment bad improved was shown by the figures given in the latest none of the Tariff Reform League, the Tends. Unica return, showing that unem- ployment In May 101 l was 2.5 per cent and in May 1900 it was 79 per cent. I hen it wan said that they could only get higher nese, by Protection, hot yet they all knew that the dockers and carters had recently obtained an advanoe. If the workers of this country would only stand ehoulder to .houlder 'hey could get any reasonable wages they inked for. (Applane.) The Teriff " Reformers " said that they were not going to tax raw material, but what Win raw material to ores man was manufactured goods to another, just a. the billets which were Imported from Germlny by a local Item were raw material to them, tot they were the manufactured article to the billetmakers of England. (Applauee.) In conclusion the epeaker said that through the operation of food taxes the larger a man's family wan the more he would hsva to pay and this he con- damned as an unfair system of taxation. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
