      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-05_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>Open-Air Musical Festival in the Park.</head>
        <p>Open-Air Musical Festival in the Park.</p>
        <p>One of the most pleasurable and commendable of local summer event• is the annual open-air musical festival in aid of the Warrington Infirmary. It is organized by the Workingmen's Committee, which des such noble and self-sacrificing work on behalf of our leading charity, and sic aye makes a doubly , effective appeal for support by feason of its artistic interest and the thoroughly &amp;serving nature of the object. A new departure was made this year in 1 holding the festival on Sunday afternoon instead of on a week-day evening. From the standpoint of attendance and receipts the innovation was fully justified, the number of people assembling in the Park : reaching well over ,i,ts/0 while the receipts amounted to £43 Us. Md., as against 0:3 lfis. SI last year. Although no charge was made for admission to the , Park, everyone entering was expected to give some- 1 thing. , The members of the Warrington Male Voice Choir. and the Apollo Ma'e Voice Clinic and Crosfidd's band once more came forward in a public-spirited and generous manner to provide a most interesting programme. Vocal music is, of course, never heard In full advantage in the open-air, for it is impassible to ' judge of the finer points of detail and expression. The general effect was, however, eminently pleasing and inspiring. The combined choirs occupied a platform erected near the handetand by Messrs. Peter Walker and Sons, Ltd., and a line of flags and str, amers added lor ',redness to the scene. Mr. Frank Hickman conducted and had his forces ander excellent control. The choirs sang with refreshing crispness and vigour in such stirring pieces as " The destruc- tion of Gera " " Martyrs of the Arena " and "The Crunaders," whi'e a tenderly expressive and pathetic note was imparted to "The long day closes "and " Remember me "—two favourite items which were very well received. " The fisherman," with its vivid storm music, woe given in realiatic style—and the clouds contributed something to the effect by a shower when the storm at sea was at its height. The , pianoforte accompaniments were tastefully played by hife_ll..J. Mon.</p>
        <p>. ...---. • We have never heard Crosfield's band play better than on this occasion. There was e perfect tinder- standing between every Section ard the richness of tone was often of that solemnly impressive quality which one associates with an organ. The opening march " Monarch" (Rimier) was given in finished and spirited style. The ever-popular overture" Poet and pennant " (Suppe) received a well-ugh perfect interpretation sad called forth rounds of applause. The vatisti ns on the " Austrian Hymo " (Haydn- Soif I: afforded opportunities for some exquisite solo work, and each instrumentalist who had to play • prominent part roes to the occasion, with the result that the performance was highly artistic. The con- cluding item was the massive " Hallelujah Chorus," played with great precision. Mr. E. Smith was a most capable conductor. During the greater part of the afternoon there were alternating don] and sunshine and towards the dose of the progiamme a sharp shower was experienced, causing moet of the auiience to beat a hasty retreat : to the shelter of the trees .</p>
        <p>The arrangements for the fe.ti eel were admirably carried out by the committee, under the letdership of Alderman Burton (chairman) and Mr. G. Walker (deputy chairman). As ustml the secretarial wok wan in the bands of Mr. J. If. .1. fiamparto, the genial and hardworking secretary of the Infirmary, who overlooked no detail and put in strenuous but cheerful service to ensure ths success of the enterprise.</p>
