      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-05_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>Lost Opportunity. It. Harold Smith, and the " Hughligans."</head>
        <p>A Lost Opportunity. It. Harold Smith, and the " Hughligans."</p>
        <p>Sir.—lt was with no little surptive that last Satur- day I scanned the pages of the " Harold Smith Gazette." The plans for Imming Harold hare been so admirably laid and for months past no persistently, relentlessly. remoreeley earth,' out ; and here was such a golideu opportunity. such a chance ti earn a little cheap noton iety. mink of it --the lost ditch, and the heroes ready to dine hard and die hard in it. brandishing aloft empty champagne bottles as their weapons. Too had that we should not even have a =apish t All that is announced to as is the meagre intelligence that Mr. Harold Smith hae recently had his hands full. So doubt ; bat how' with what Why this unusual reticer.ce ? Why each unwonted rroodesty ? When was the gramaphone to record the symphany in which the brothers Smith to. k pail when they and thi in political colleague. howled down the Prime blitdster ? lot that gramophone be produced. Let ' an obliging Editor again turn on the limelight. Let each dttail of the edifying story be made known, 60 that every Tory Hooligan may lilt up his heart and rejoice in the Member for Warrington.—Yours 1,,,r1,111%. E.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-05_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>Mr. A H. Crosf ield and Parliamentary</head>
        <p>Mr. A H. Crosf ield and Parliamentary</p>
        <p>Rowdyism.</p>
        <p>Sir,— W hen speaking io WaTrington last Saturday. I quoted the " Daily Telegraph's " severe critim,in of proceedings in the Uouse of Commons, which the Times- itself also condemns as" parliamentary rowdyism." Many other Conservative newspapers of reiute (as readers of the •' Examiner " are aware) have added their pro!est and, censure ; but the eeverest and most appropriate criticism of all comes, strangely enough, from lord Hugh Cecil himmlf. Whet he said ten years ago, when the rules of the Home were being amended, is well wmtli qu_ting now. I take the following passages from the speech be defined at the time. when he was actually urging that Members of Parliarmmt guilty of violent mis- conduct in the Ikuse should be not suspended but</p>
        <p>imprisoned : " I am satisfied that the great body of sober, well- j idging people of the community. who are, let as not target, the true governors of the country. enteitain feelings of repugnance against violent diacrder which are not to be describ, d by milder terms than abhorrence and contempt. . . . iiilent conduct in the House is not only en offence against Parlia mentarysarders aid manners, but an offence against the ordinary law of the land ; it is a criminal cffencs. If the violence were done iii the street those who committed it would suffer imprisonment. Why ehould violence when it aggravated by a gri as Parliamentary offence be more leniently treated here ? Sober, well-juding people do not draw a distinction between members if Parliament and other persons ; if they do it is to expect a higher degree of morality and orderliness from their legislators." Without necensarily joining in Lord flugh's plea for the imprisomnent of offenders, one does realise that in this case his offence was greatly aggravated by the eery fact that the Prime Mic.ister habitually treats his political opponents with no admirable a combination Of courbisy, dignity and good humour. It is that fact which makes the more gross and contemptible this unmannerly insult to so eminent a statesman.— Yours etc.. A. H. CROSFIELD. 3:1, Hyde Park (late, SW. 3rd August, 1:011.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-05_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>Tariff Reform Treat.</head>
        <p>The Tariff Reform Treat.</p>
        <p>Lady Greenall and the Chairman of the Education Committee.</p>
        <p>Sir,—My stir mica has Lien drawn to a recent article in your paper, in which you purport to tell facts but do not tell them correctly. Ilay I ask by whom my coresiondence with Councillor Henry I Roberts is deemed to he ca,felential ? Cettrinly ct is not by me, and I hope he will puliish the whole of it. If, es yen state, the c;errespcnderce was read by Councillor Roberts to the Scho I Management Com- mittee, how can it be deemed to be confidential, and I can only suppose that the writer cf your article has invented the suggestion for some obscure purpose of his own. I dA not write to Councillor Roberts in his personal capacity, and lie evidently understood that I wrote to him as Chairman of the Education Committee, other wise there was no necessity for him to consult the Town Clerk and Director el Education, or in his reply to have signed himself "Chairman Warrington. Education Committee." It is true that in his requests to tie teachers he described himself its "Mr. Henry Roberts" but if lie hail not been chairman, is it probable that they would have taken notice of his letters? The unofficial assistance of Mr. Roberts was net asked for, and it was not required. In whatever position C Yuic il Jur Roberts considered himself, his first letter was a refusal to help, and certainly showed none of the anxiety which suddenly leapt into life in his later letters" to Inighien the grey lives of the children." My only object in writing to the Chairman of the Education Committee was to avoid any distinction of creed or politics in the dis- tribution of the tickets. lake this opportunity of sincerely thanking all the teachers for the very great trouble they have taken on behalf of the children.— Yours faithfully,</p>
        <p>FRANCES E. GREENALL</p>
        <p>I fully ag, ee wohyour ''''''''''''''''' that it use a pity that some nember of the General Committee did not aak for information and for the correrpoLdenee to be reed.</p>
        <p>Welton Hall, Warrinyten. August 3rd, 1911</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-05_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>Tariff Reform Distortion</head>
        <p>A Tariff Reform Distortion</p>
        <p>Sir,—St rue very well-known Protectione.t news- pipers, and many that are less well-known, have printed simultaneously an astonishing Tariff Reform letter about prices in Anierira. The letter is based on statements made ins booklet recently issued under the aichority of the Tariff Reform League itself; no that we have direct evidence once m, roof ctn. British Protectionists endeavouring to reconstruct their case on the shiftiest of all hundations Free Traders have pointed with grai cause to the burden of protective taxation in America and elne- esbere. No Tariff Reformer of et pate has ever denied that such taxation enables the I mare manufacturer to keep his prices above what they would be rile trete aubjecttd to foreign competition ; and Mr. Balfour was quite right when lie Paid " A Protective policy is a policy w }rich aimn at supporting or meeting home industries by raising hcme prices." Is there any Tariff Reform manufacturer in this country who doee riot bore list in just such a way he nary benefit under a tariff? Yet, in the teeth of all eviderce to the contrary, we have the Tariff Reform League end scene of its most reputable newspaper supporters circulating, a statement so purposely distorted as this . " The American working man's tools, his howler hat, his shca, the furniture in his home, his wife's hat, dress, umbrella, the articles which the Free Trade leaflets tell us are taxed anything from 1.", per unt. upwards—are made in the United States and not taxed stall. The Free Tratle leaflets should have said that import duties are imposed on these articles if; we rend them to the United States, but the American wi rkman boys home-made goods, end on these home-nrade goods no duty is levied whatever. Competition among the home producers is quite I sufficient to ensure moderate prices." It is right to say that no duty is levied by the Govern- meat en homemade goods. althorgh this meodaciorie letter ignores the duty which the manufacturers them- selves by legislation to Tut on, and mva nothing about the taxation of the materials of which; those goods are made, and does not reflect on the characteristic con- tradiction that if Americans buy none bnt home- made goods, there can he no revenue got out of " the foreigner." And its referm ce to the " competition among home-producers" is enough to make every dweller in America smile. For is not the whole</p>
        <p>country seeking wiih anger against the trusts that ' destroy competition with the sole object of keeping prices high ? And has not the Attorney-General of the United Stet, just said that the Senate should fix reasonable prices for commodities in order that the. trusts should not have it all their own way ? The I American people last Li, cember voted the high taxers cut of place and power. and since then the rival pliticians have been busy holding against one another for electonsl favour on the strength of their promises to reduce the tariff. Why should these things be, if,se the Tariff Reform League so cunningly implies, foreign goods are kept out of America and homemade goods are sold at Free Trade prices This latest attempt of the British Protectionists to defraud the unretlecting voter is their silliest attempt, and their w orst.—Vonte truly, THE EDIFOR OF "THE FREE TB %DEN.- Free Trade Union, S. Victoria-street, S.W. -I,,gost Ist, 1911.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-05_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>Mr. Harold Smith, M.P., and the Wire Trade.</head>
        <p>Mr. Harold Smith, M.P., and the Wire Trade.</p>
        <p>Sir.—l on sorry tint Mr. Harold Smith could not find time to discuss wick Mr. Pierce Williams the point at variance, for I feel sure your reader, would have been much edified. Like Mr. Williams I myself belong to neidier Liberal or Tory party and therefore approach this question of international exchange from an independent standpoint. Ido not agree with the Tariff Reformers because in the first place I do not want to see England. as a certain Professor stated at the unveiling of the stone to the memory ef the Roman camp at Warrington, a Lime Wigan, Warrington or Sheik, Id. Ido not want England to do all the work while the foreign country I eople bask in the sun all day. For after all the consuming capacity of the whole peop'e of the enrth is limited For any section of the English community to want the Engli•l: pe,ple to do any more of the world's work is unfair to the English people. The process of evolution in industry has brought es to the position that rn.e nation is dependent opon another for commodities. England exports its mineral resource in en t hinge for wheat etc., and so we find n hen we probe deep enough into the complex in.ernetional trade the aims process of give and take tanning through cur whole inter,ationd commerce, the intlexible law o' sul ply and demand tallying with the value and quantity of goals exchanged. For inettare,, &amp;we anyone imagine th,d it would be wise for ass. • nation to do nr.thing but nerd the- product of our labour, whitener it may be, tot ermany. France or Hong-Kong nithout getting some of their labcnr pro- duct in exchange. Or to put it in a plainer manner, do y• u think it would be jarinock for Bill bmith the j iner to work for Harry Jon, s, the fa 11,r making him scull if Harry Jones did not work for Bill Smith the joit.er, producing the fo deter for him? Of C6ll-se fri eay "N.." By our scientific system of dividing labour into sections of experts we have bee, r. interdependent tipm one another's labour, product iu order to live. One sect on of workers make shirts, another makes soap, another wire and so on and, by • standard of exchange known as money, exchange one another prod.'s. The same applies internationally, with one exception—that instead of money we have lag books. Thus far I appreciate the desire of the Twill Reformers in float they desire to develop our own reaeurees. The export of coal, iron and steel trim this country and the importation of wheat, eggs, cheese in a scandal and a certain proof c f bad government. The supply of coal, ir,ia and steel is beyond doubt limited, and such things as wheat eggs, and these it is certain we can produce curselves A good Government would see that this was doue. If , we had good government and sufficient patri tine, the development of our own resources could be our first consideration, and then whatever we could not prodoce ourselves exchange some of our products, of which we had an abundance for ivlich our brothers over the sea, not neceesarily Colonials, had an abundance of. Such. I believe, is a sound productive and international exchange policy. It should be of a mutually beneficial nature, ar.d not of the filibustering nature such as the scandalous Morocco affair, in which foreign capitalists try to force their over-production upon foreign countries, while at the same time the people who have produced that over-production are suffering from want. The element of profit should be entirely eradicated from such exchange, and then perhaps we should not see the present inconguous spectacle' of England supplying foreign natim a with battleship. which may one day be used to shoot the very people who made them. This question of interaatiotial exchange mutt in future become of more and more importance as time goes on. The fanatical idea of Englat,tl becoming the workshop of the world way sure to Lail whilst we had means of communicatl.n. Knowledge of Dew methods of producing permeates the whole of the trorlPs people to Which, as we know, every notion Is now adapting itself. The consuming psser of the people is practically stationary as at present constitoted, and in the near hem.° we shall have the paradoxical spectacle of the people idle amid starving because they have produ,:d t o much and simply because the capitalists cannot sell their goods at a profit. By that time the people may then see dist they produce things in order to satisfy certain natural requirement, necessary to life. They may then find out in order to satisfy the requirements, the means of production should not be owned for the profit of • few individuals but by the nation for the nation's benefit and use.—l am yours truly, I ABOR OMNIA</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-05_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>ONE PENNY PER WEEK</head>
        <p>ONE PENNY PER WEEK</p>
        <p>Reduction of a Warrington Work- man s Compensation Award.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington County Court on Thursday,before his Honour Judge Stanger, EP., no application was made by Nlessra. Monks, Hall, and Co., Ltd., iron manufacturers, for a review of the weekly composure- ' Goo of 9s. payable by them to Fred Norton, of 13, Gandy'a-walk, Tilley-street, Warrington. Mr. Adshead Elliott, barrister (insttucted by Mr. IL K. Fieldhonae, of Manchester) appeared for the applicants and Mr. A. Browne represented the respondent. Mr. Elliott said the accident occurred on April 23rd 1910. The respondent at that time was a sub-foreman over the scrap iron wheelers. On that dote the steam hammer fell on his Land, crushing the thumb, and a portion of the hand had to be taken off. The hand was seriously injured, but in conjunction with the und band it we.. useful limb for liglit work. In February this year hewn, given some light work, for which he received LI he. 21. per week, al compared with Pis. prior to the accident. Ile continued the work from February until June, when the applicants were com- pelled to discharge him for misconduct in various ways. On several °outdone he absented himself Crone work without king any notice, fie had also been found on premises in. helpless condition of drunken... Enta Woods, William Fester, Sums] Mould, Thomas Hill, Hauge Henry Thompsm, and Mr. William Murray (works monger) gave evidence in support of counsel's statement, Mr. Murray stating that if the man's character were good, his physical disabilities should not prevent him from Letting work. 3lr. Browne said he 1,18 not going to contest the points given in evidence with reference to thecondition of the man. He did not preps"nn to submit that the full amount of 91. should be kept up. He, however, said it wits all very well for Mr. Murray t s state that it • man is. similarly crippled a edition to the applicaot applied for moth and his character was good, there would he no objection to employing him. Imegine a man going to any works in the town with bin band mutilated and applying for work. There would not be the elighteat chance for him. Although the applicaet had been guilty of the aliegedmiaconduct, he contended that that fact did not entitle the employers to reduce the order to Id. per "rook. Ile suggestel that he. would ben just allowance. . .</p>
        <p>Ili; Honour : I cannot see any mid-way point between full compenes: ion and a peony per week. It enema to me that it must ha either one or the other. If m.^conduct is established, surely it would be inequitable to make the employers pay. On the oth, hand you might try to set aside these facts and say that tbe man should not he punished for misconduct. If so, he would be entitled to compensation and then you shouid have the full amount.</p>
        <p>Mr. browoe intimated tint he could not carry the case say further. Ills Honour granted the application and reduced the order to Id. per wtek, with costs to tho emplo3ers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-05_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>HOUSEBREAKING IN MANCHESTER ROAD.</head>
        <p>HOUSEBREAKING IN MANCHESTER ROAD.</p>
        <p>Between Monday and Thursday tits house of Mr. W . Mason, headmaster of the B,ainant Schools, near Bruclie Bridge, Manchester-road, was broken into and numerucs articlea of value stolen. For the past fortnight Mr. an I Mrs. Mason and tin ir eon have been on holiday in North Wales but the police were not notified of their absence or a closer watch would have been kept upon the premises. The house was male on Monday, but on Thursday morning it was , discovered that an entry had been effected by breaking a pane of glans in a back window. The place had , been ranaacked, the booty consisting chiefly of jewellery and cutlery. The robbery had been clumsily done, showing that the thieves taunt have bad ample time to 'rack undisturbed. No other cases of I housebreaking have occurred of late.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-05_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>TERRITORIALS IN CAMP.</head>
        <p>TERRITORIALS IN CAMP.</p>
        <p>only parade in the afternoon was .quad drill .for the naen who were not efficient. The sth South Lanes-</p>
        <p>shire. hare gone out for the night, and it is rumoured that they will attack the camp to-night. INTERESTING WORK AT FARLETON A staff officer vi,ited the battalion's lines to-day and CAMP. congratulated the battalion on the cleanliness of the INTERESTING MAN,EUVRES. Wino ESI, iv. Last night's rumours about the night attack proved to be unfounded. A storm raged all through the night but this morning broke rearm and bright. Company drill was again the rder of the day before breakf ad. An in :eresting scheme had been arranged for the battalion after breakfast The general idea was that the Corr:unending Officer Ith South Lancashire Regiment had received the following message :—" A party of scat mounted troops of the enemy has broken through the outpost line and occupied Ililnethorpe at I urn. this morning. March out immediately and attack this force." The Commanding Officer sends cut scouts who report about 20') or the enemy occupying Iliggenbead. This forco War immediately attacked and defeated. The charge was ace ,mpanied with deafening cheers. Son afterwards the scouts I reported 500 of the enemy on the hill cod of Milne- thorpe. and the battilion immediately reformed and proceeded to demolish thie Igoes. The cyclists, machine guns awl signallers accompanied the battalion an] rendered efficient eervice in their respec- tive departments. The battalion reached camp lama aft, 1.311. Some amusement was caused this evening by the cyclist section, who arranged spurts in camp. Slow races a•al obstacle races helped to enliven the ramp. routine, and were watched with great interest Several sulfa took place, but no damage was dune.</p>
        <p>On Sunday the Oh South Lancashire Regiment entered ms.ri their annual fortnight under canvas. The scene of ope.stions this year is Farleton. Westmoreland. This is an extremely suitable plane I r manouvres as there are p'enty of good roads and the hille, though high, are easily climbed. There inn good supply of water, both for drinking and bathing purposes. The keel battalion again forms part of the South Lancashire Brigade under Colonel Verschoyle as Brigadier. The brigade in turn is part of the West Laccashire Division. under the command of Major- General Bethune. The fficers in camp are Lieutenant-Colonel J D Fairclough (commanding), Majors 13 Fairclough and FM Appleton, Captains H ri Roberts, C F Cary, A B Layton and C N Williamson, Lientennuts F It Monks, Egerton Fairclough, W Slaclure, B S Townroe, Rue Fairelough (cyclists), 1, S Ifenahall, Second Lieutenants, A Skinner and Robertson, Surgeon Major Adams with Captain Ii Elgood, adjutant. The wants of the " inner man " are this year attended to by Lieutenant and Quartermaster O. 11. Ingram. A rew departure has been made with regard to the meals of the men. Instead of having their meals in their own tents as before, the Territorials ate all accommodated in a large mesa tent. When this new scheme 11113 been tt ied once Or twice, the men settled down to the arrangement, and • great success is assured.</p>
        <p>• A .41b TukdcAttegt,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-05_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>GENERAL VIEW OF THE CAMP.</head>
        <p>A GENERAL VIEW OF THE CAMP.</p>
        <p>THE DEPARITRE.</p>
        <p>Me six Warrington Corarpanie. ra,aded in /root of the town Hall at It) 30 a in. on Suntivy and, after the necessary preliminaries had been performed and each man oupplied wits an excellent haversack ration. marched down Sankey-street and Wrlson-Pattenetre t to Bank (JuAy Stet on, headed by the Battalion Dead, unchr the control of Bandnra,ter Richardson. The ' men were quickly entraioed in two trains, the rrceud of which stopped at Newton In pick up the two companies there. Very little time was lost on the way and Burton and dolma was reached shorty after tl,r, e o'clock. A march of a little over three miles had to be cegotiated before liarleton way reached. A brief thunderstorm was experienced while on the march, but, on the whole, the weather has been all that could Le desired. On arrival on the ground. the various companies were marched to their urn lines and allotted tents where they immediately proceeded to make rhemoelves comfortable. Beds. blankets, .0-c, were dietrilmted, and a good tea was partaken of and the rest of the day was spent in getting the tente into something like ahip-shape form.</p>
        <p>ACCIDENT TO THE ADJUTANT. Shortly after the arrival of the battalion in camp, the Adjntant met with a rather nasty accident. In trying to stop a rearing horse, the animal kicked him, cutting his face and damaging his hand. Captain Elgood was immediately attended to by Suraeon- Major Adams. The accident hen not ir eveirtNl the ~trlpt'ant from performing hie mual -jut.es. 111,,NDAY. , '</p>
        <p>To-day tile camp roes early a,tir and after aver, welcome cup of tea, the battalion was marched out ot camp on to the runny fields in tl e vicinity and engaged in company drill. Alter breakfast, a start was made with the more seriolls work and skirmishing work was engaged in on the hills to the s uth of the camp. The battalion signaller, under Sergeant Betts formed part of a chain of stations and were posted on Farleton , Fell, Ltne Tree Hill and Scout Bill and worked in a very creditable manner.</p>
        <p>The Y.ll CA. tent ia again very much in evidence and the concert to-night was well attended. A crowded house listened to n concert which would not have disgraced any concert hall. . . .</p>
        <p>Some alarm was expel ienced this evening by loud explosion, which mode many a face go while. Oil inquiry it woe found it vras only the " nine-o'clock " gun which i 4 fired every night in the Aitillery camp</p>
        <p>Tr-o-i ti This mr rning opened very fine and the companies were out before breakfast fore mipany drill under the I company officers and instructors. The signallern were round the camp practising semaphore signalling. The battalion carried out the same programme as yesterday getting ready for the more serious work of next week. The machine gun, cyclist and signalling sections were under their own commanders. The</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-05_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>LOCAL WEDDING.</head>
        <p>LOCAL WEDDING.</p>
        <p>Worthington—Bridge.</p>
        <p>An interesting marriage was celebrated at the Primitive Methodist Cherub, Legh-street, Warrington. on Monday. the contacting Cantles being M 139 Bridge of Batteraby-lane, and Mr. Charles Worthington of Hanover-street. non if the late Mr. CS. Wm thir gum chartered accountant.</p>
        <p>On account of domestic affliction, the proceedings were of a quiet nature, but as Mins Bridge is a well- known v enlist, many of her friends assembled to itne-s the ruble part of the ceremony. The bride true neatly attired to pale grey ninno, over eilk, a itb hat to mabb, and carried a shower bouquet of white roses, lilies and white heather. She was given awny by her father, Mr. Richard Bridge. The bridesmaids were Miss E. M. Worthington, sister of the bridegroom, and Mins L. K. Worthington. They were attired in blue silk, and carried bouquets of pale pink sweet puts and maiden-hair fern. Mr. William D. Turner acted as best man. The ceremony was performed by ills Rev. W. Cooper, circuit minister, w bile Mr. W. If. Payton, Muir. line, prenided at the organ. At the clone of the ceremony the party adjourned to Atkinson's Cafe, Bridge-etreet, where luncheon Ivan served. The toast of the newly. married couple wan proposed by Rev. W. Cooper, and after Mr. Worthington had suitably responded, the happy pair journeyed to London, where the honeymoon is being spent. Numerous messages of congratulation were rezeived.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-05_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>RATING OF CEMETERIES AND GRAVEYARDS.</head>
        <p>THE RATING OF CEMETERIES AND GRAVEYARDS.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Warrington Assessment Committee ass held at the Workhouse yesterday, Mr. A. Brooks presiding. There were also present:— Nimes J Evens, J Billinge, W 6ill, %V C Brooks. W F'urness, W Houghton, A J Jolley, J W Knowles, A Bottomley ;clerk), E GUPEOD (valuer), and W H Woodcock (assistant overseer). ' ' '</p>
        <p>An interesting discussion arose on en appeal which bed teen lodged against the assessment of St. James' burial ground, II tydock at £25 gross, £2O ratable. In muster to several mens'nsre, the Clerk said that according to a rerent decision of the House of Lords, burial grounds were ratable to the poor rate. Mr. Houghton said that roorelly he was against such places being seamed, although he could nut pretend to speck ab nit the lewd aspect. He would he glad to know what was the nano in their ems district with regard to Churchyards and cemeteries. The Chairmen to Hr. Woodcock) Do pen asses any in Warrington ? Ale. Woodcock t The cemetery is rated. The Chairman t You don't rate church burial grounds ? Mr. Woocdrck t No, became they are not in mse. ' The Valuer said the effect or the deciison of the House of Lords was that graveyards attached to amebae were to be rated, prorilaki there wee certain Income. If charges were made for interments there was</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-05_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>BATTALION ON OUTPOST DUTY.</head>
        <p>THE BATTALION ON OUTPOST DUTY.</p>
        <p>Tin n •I,‘y.</p>
        <p>Breakfast was served. flute earlier this morning and at II o'clock the battalion marched out of carnp to lake part in a scheme of outpost work. The Ith , South lancashires worked the road from Ilevetalmm to Ililnthorpe where they coupled up with the :oh South Lancashires, who extended into fleethato, the whole front f the outpost line covering a di.dance of , 3', miles. The idea was that the enemy defeated yesterday had concentrated on Lindale and had bvea reinforced. This force was renewing the attack and was marching on our outposts. The movemeld was well carried out, all the available cover being made use of. A chain of signallers coveting the whole: front conveyed information from one end of the line' to the other. Uncooked meat and bread was carried by each man and cooked in the field in the mess-tins: The men arrived buck in camp shortly after 1:30, , after having spent a very interesting day. WARRINGTON ()MUER INJURED.</p>
        <p>During the maimmvres on Thursday, Majw B. Fairclough, of the Ith Battalion South Lancashire Regiment, met with an accident while riding by the side of the troops on the return to camp. Hi, horse slipped in a hole and rolled over, pinning its rider beneath it. Aesistance was promptly rendered, and Muj r Fairclough was fourd to be ruffering frole e fractured rib and ah:ck. IJ was coat eyed t 9 ale by the field aalbula-ce. On inqu;,s, yesleplay to Ir,spa.lent woo informed by Colonel Fairclough that his cousin, Mdkn. Fair- clough, who was thrown froin his horse on Thursday night, is progrea,ing favourably —Major Fairclough will be out of action for this camp, and will be taken }mule when able to be removed.</p>
        <p>Tbe South Lancashire Brigade yesterday Ind a field ' day of their own to enable the officers to pass for promotion. The enemy were the sth S oath Lanca- shire with eight machine guns. A red force, tinder Colonel Ifacfie (Liverpool Scottish), assisted by artillery with blank shell, stormed the hills, driving the enemy back. Cammunications were well con- veyed lay signallers and cyclists. The Warringtol men were in reserve.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-05_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>NEW SENSATION.</head>
        <p>A NEW SENSATION.</p>
        <p>General Bethune proposes having a day when the [mope, instead of firing the usual Minsk rimunition, shall all torn out of camp as if in an enemy's country. Pickets will be placed along every road leading to Farleton Fell, which is a commanding tusght over- looking the camp. Their duties will be to warn off all natives who might be entering the danger zone. When all is clear howitzers and other field artillery will belch forth real liar shells, firing over the heads of the infantry who will exprienee the feeliog of being under fire.</p>
        <p>not the alrghteat dotilit according to the deciainn of the 'louse of Lords, that graveyards and connotedon were rat ahle. _ .</p>
        <p>I; answer to Mr. Knowles, the assistant oveneer for Ilaydock (Mr. Dickinson) stated that no charge wes made in the country districts other than the sexton's fees.</p>
        <p>The Veliner r If no charge is roads for • grave, I take it tbe graveyard is not ratable. In the absence of the appellant (the Vicar of SL James', Ilaydock) consideration of the matter was deferred.</p>
