      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-05_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>FREE TRADE v. TARIFF REFORM.</head>
        <p>FREE TRADE v. TARIFF REFORM.</p>
        <p>Debate at Bridge Foot</p>
        <p>lire or six thoussnd peopla wended their wsy to Bridge Root on Thursday evening to hear a dehisce on Free Trade T. Tariff Returns between Mr. Harry New sod Mr. H. Henshall rNlsneliester Tsriff Reform</p>
        <p>League). Throughout there were few inter-ru—fitio.o.;-;l7e political hecklers being appealed to by Mr. Jetties Ball, who presided, to curb their euthusissm. The Chairman said that they were not cwicerned with the political aspect of the question, but they wanted to look at the matter from a detached standpoint as it was of importance to all of them. Oa rising, Mr. Nen was accorded • hearty welcome awl he began his forceful apeech by stating the Tariff policy ea outlined try Mr. Chamberlain to 1903. Mr, Chamberlain's object, he stated, WAD to bind the colonies and the Mother Country more clomly together. To do that we should have to readjust our tenth. First of all, we most put a tariff of 2a. on foreign corn, Is. on colonial, 5 per cent, on dairy pro- duce, and 10 per cent. on manufactured goods. That! meant, if it meant BUJ thing, that either the consumer or tbe foreigner would pay the tax. Free Tradera wail without the slightest hesitation that if they Put tax upon nay commodity the consumer must inevitably pay. (Applause.) In 1903 AIL Chamberlain bad ale, aarrl that if we were t t given preference br thecolonies we most put a tax on food and he added " There it is, the murder is out." Even the 21. tax proproaedi Mr• New continued, would hava a detrimental effect upon the workiugclastme, and what was more the tax wee</p>
        <p>HOUND TO INCRKA,F. For could any Tariff" Reformer "give lme guarantee that the tax would rernsin stationary or would decline in view of the fact that never in the history of this country, with the exception of the coal ten, bad taxes for revenue purposes remained atstionary ? As to whether a tax on food would affect the price he would point out that wheat la. tax was imposed upon wheat to 191)3 tic Nlichael Ilia. Beach, the Tory Chancellor of the Exchequer, declared that the pike had Tifton in consequence. Mr. New gave example, of how the tines on commodities in - countries had</p>
        <p>risen from a email surn to a highforeign rate. As recently as 1903 Mr. Chamberlain held the views he held in 14/11 when he said " A tax on food would mean a decline In wages, which would certainly involve a reduction in the productive mdue of the people and this would raise the price of every artier. in ths United Kidgdom" and be aided that" it would indubitably bring ali rut the loss of our gigantic export trade." (Loud •ppleurie.i After stating that it , estimated that the workingclasass would base to pay £13,000,000 a year if a tax of in, per quarter were reposed upon wheat the speaker remarked that the colonies and Ehgland thil• supplied us w i.h one third of the wheat consumed in this country sod it would take ywlrs,if ever it nould be done, retorn they could supply us with the whole. And yet the Tariff Reformeri wanted them to 11(XVHN I TWo-TIIIRDI Or clue ci 'PLY 113911 COMING IN.</p>
        <p>I As on example of how keeping out foreign goods would I result her rid that during the recent doctors' etrike when they would not cart fruit and other good• the price of food went up in SUMO pieces, lie went to a shop during the strike and naked for some fruit and when they asked more for it than be expecte] he asked the reason sod they told hint it wets because of not being able to get hold of much fruit. Tariff "Reform" —shut out your snot lies ! (hood applause.) If they shut out their nupplie• they paid more heavily for the reduced quantity. (.%pplause.) Mr. Chamberlain in 11103 said that cotton, wool, iron and steel tradee were going. Where were clay going? (A Voir,: Upsrirds.) la 1907 we imported £6,000,000 worth of iron nod steel missulacturers sod exported .C 16,000,000 Of wool we ireported .C 9,000,000 and exported £34,00,000, and of cotton we impacted £7.00,00) and exported .C 110,000,000. mil applause). We were told at the net eleotich that if we could</p>
        <p>SHUT OUT HICHNIAN WIRT</p>
        <p>more prosperitY;;Ouldionie—it; Warrington. What was the position of affair. Meows. Itylands exported a thousand tons of drawn wire per week. But then Messrs 14lands had, monopoly because they were part of Muer.. Pearson and Knowles, and the eooner they introduced Protection into Ry lands and Pearson and Knowles, the sooner Measre.lThitecross, Co., Ltd., and other firms would go under. (Applause.) Mr. New was remarking that under Free Trade and depressed cnnditions a new eteel works wee being erected at Itlem—when the Chaim,na announced that</p>
        <p>his time was up. Mr. New resumed hi, Cyst amid loud applatme from the greater portion of the audience.</p>
