      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-05_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>MK. HEYSEIALL REPLIES,.</head>
        <p>MK. HEYSEIALL REPLIES,.</p>
        <p>A good reception was accorded to Mr. Henshall who was allowed half an hour to reply to Mr. New. Ha said be was only aorry that Mr. Nov had not gives him something to light. He wan very much afraid before Mr. New's speech had been delorered that he was going to meet his Waterloo on the contrary ts. found be bad come out for a holiday. (I.sualtter.l Continuing, he said that he was not an anti-Free Trader but be was out for an alterati in of our system and not for Pr9tocti.a. 11.4-100 that or pscnl policy could oot be one of Pres Trade when Very year • ---</p>
        <p>- -,„ V 50000.10 WAS COLLECTS° IN 1,,,0D TEES ...• • . .</p>
        <p>and if it were right to tar the workingmen's tea 30 per cent, could it he wrong to tar a rich min's motor car id per cent? lt was:paid Brit Tsriff Reform 111111 going to hen bad thing, hot let them see how it worked in other conntries where there high tadffs. 'I he Americana to long no they were content to buy American clothe., whiob tumid work and anger for the I American people, did not have to Till a tan at all.. According to the Royal Commission appointed by • Radical Government, the American skilled workers had 2' times more work than the British workers under I alleged free imports, and though they her to pay from 15 to 50 per cent. more for food, they were from lit to 80 percent. better off than we were. They were told that the commie, would have to pry the tra, but if that were Ito it would only teach us is very ralutary lemon, and thst was to be depend., s upon ourselves fir what we wanted, rather thin upon other countries of the world. Mr. New had spoken 1.40.1 t the cotton tradeland pointed out that our exports ...dad our imports, hut he (the speaker contended that as there were thou, inda of pounds worth of cotton good. coming into this cruntry every month the Eng- lishman would be .noch letter occupied in making them (Applause. Ile indicted Free 'hods, for one thing, because the people who had lived longest under it were the people who were least able to help themselves. That was provnd by the fact that those who lived to the Paalmiat's)alloitted span were the people who had to ark for a pension of 55. to eke out • psltry existence. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-05_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>DIR. NEWS SECOND SPEECH.</head>
        <p>DIR. NEWS SECOND SPEECH.</p>
        <p>Mr. New then commenced his second speech—this time for • quarter of an hour. Ife mid that Mr. Reuben had observed that we ahould develop our own resources rnore. It. folly agreed with him and the only wey to e” that was to unlock the 20,00,000 aorta of land which were uncultivated. Tariff " Reform " or Free Trade, (rete. the land were released woold never bring aboutfideal conditions. (Applause.) It wee impoesible to extend certain icduatries unless the land could be got, becluse all wealth w. obtained front the land. And besides ustil they could abolish royalties on minerals they would never do as well as they might. (Applause.) (letting back to theqoestion at issue, ihe speaker mid that when Mr. Chamberlain proposed Lis scheme he laid it down that for whatever taner were imposed others would be taken off, but sale recent years the taxes on tea and sugar bad been greatly reduced what were they going to take off? It. considered it</p>
        <p>rorosssn INIPCD[NcE</p>
        <p>- on the part of the Tarlflars to sty that we should not admit manufactured goods Mull this country and then say that we bad an undisputed right to send our sum. factures abroad. England was an isolated country, dependent upon other °Gnarl., nod unless we could get certain goods from abroad we should not ta able to compete in the world's markets. (Loud •pplause .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-08-05_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>CONCLUSION.</head>
        <p>CONCLUSION.</p>
        <p>Itbe course of his reply Mr. Flenshall said that I there wee plenty of land to he had very Lbeep in this country. I r,ve minutes apt eche. were after alkyds delivered by I the epeakers and tbe meeting concluded, great enthusiasm being shown on both sides. • Death of a Local Butcher.</p>
        <p>We regret to record the death of Mr Richard Thomas Bridr. 47. Wash-lan.., Latchford, which took place on Sunday, at the age of 17 years. Deceased had only been ailing for • abort time. Mr. Bridge was in business as a butcher in Latchford for 20 year, Ile served as vice-presiderd of the Warrington Butcher's Asacciation for about eight years. He wan a keen angler and was s'ao president of tie Latchford Angling S,ciety. He leave/ a widow and one son. The funeral took place at the Warrington Cemetery on Tuesday, the Rev Cl C Briggs officiating. The mourners were Messrs Jos. Bridge (son), Her•ry Bridge (brother), T noon, Jos ! Nixon, John Nixon, It Holland, Samuel Snatch, T Gandy, W Nerfoot, T Floecow, A Bosomy., T Brecon., jun., J Boacow, It Holland, F. Nixon, T 11 Allen, J W Starkey, Ashley, Greases, Flood, I Ford, James Garnett, Jame, Ashley, E Bennett, T Hewitt, E Openshaw, W Stringer, E Phillips, T Allen, Bowles, M,rries, Burgess, Holland, G !Li. Griffiths and other.. The Warriegton Butchers 1 Association were represented by Messrs R. Shaw (president U Ruseel (secretary W Shaw (treasurer;, E Wright, T Ford, S Ws;nwright, A W Lashford, J Greenwood, J W Bowers, C Bowers, Green, Shepherd, Sburt, Hewitt, Connelly, T Starkey, ' W Cook, W Shan, A Mynekyme and T Hill, Mr. Leonard Bennett represented the Farmers' Auction Company and Mr. Mainwaring, jun., represented his , father —A large number of floral tributes were received. and these included —Warrington Butchers' I Aosociation, Black Bear Angling Swiety end the Brook Inn Angling Society. The funeral arrange. cuenta were in the hands of Mr. Edward Cmnor.</p>
      </div>
